= Electromagnetism
{wiki}

Electromagnetism describes how <electric charge> and <electric current> interact through the <electric field> $\mathbf E$ and <magnetic field> $\mathbf B$. The <Maxwell equations> describe the fields and their sources; the <Lorentz force> describes the response of a particle of <electric charge> $q$ and <velocity> $\mathbf v$:
$$
\mathbf F=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B),\qquad
\frac{d\mathcal E_{\mathrm{particle}}}{dt}=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v.
$$
The <electric field> can change the particle's <energy>. The magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> is perpendicular to <velocity>, so <magnetic forces do no work>; they can bend a trajectory. These are local interactions: a particle responds to the <electromagnetic field> at its position. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_01.html][Feynman lectures, II.1: fields and forces]{ref}.

The remarkable unification is that the same <Maxwell equations> connect <Coulomb's law>, <Faraday's law of induction>, and freely propagating <electromagnetic waves>. A changing <electric field> contributes to magnetic circulation through <displacement current>; together with <Faraday's law>, this permits waves in vacuum without conducting matter. The <Poynting theorem> then explains how the fields store and transport <energy>. This connects the force on a charge to the energy carried by light.

In his 1865 paper, <James Clerk Maxwell> derived transverse disturbances and compared the propagation speed inferred from electrical measurements with independently measured light speeds. Part VI, especially §§95–97, argues that their agreement supports identifying light as an <electromagnetic wave>. This was a comparison with finite experimental accuracy, rather than a numerical identity between the historical measurements. \a[https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rstl.1865.0008][Maxwell, A Dynamical Theory of the Electromagnetic Field, Philosophical Transactions 155 (1865), 459–512]{ref}; \a[https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/A_Dynamical_Theory_of_the_Electromagnetic_Field/Part_VI][transcription of Maxwell's original Part VI]{ref}.

= Thermoelectric effect
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A <thermoelectric effect> couples heat flow and electrical transport. Temperature differences can generate an electromotive force, while <electric currents> can transport heat. These effects concern nonequilibrium transport and must be distinguished from equilibrium contact potentials.

= Seebeck effect
{c}
{parent=Thermoelectric effect}

The <Seebeck effect> is the generation of a thermoelectric voltage by a temperature difference. Two materials with different thermopowers form a circuit whose electromotive force is the temperature integral of their Seebeck-coefficient difference, with sign set by circuit orientation. In a nondegenerate <semiconductor>, <electron> and hole transport usually give opposite thermopower signs.

= Seebeck coefficient
{c}
{parent=Seebeck effect}
{title2=$S$}

The <Seebeck coefficient> is thermoelectric voltage per temperature difference in the small-gradient limit, with its sign depending on the stated voltage and orientation convention. In a two-material loop the net electromotive force is an oriented integral $\int_{T_1}^{T_2}(S_1-S_2)\,dT$. It is a transport coefficient, so equilibrium density-of-states data alone do not determine it.

= Lorenz-gauge reduction of the electromagnetic action
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}

The <electromagnetic field tensor> action differs from the component-gradient potential action by $(2\mu_0)^{-1}\int \partial^bA^a\partial_aA_b\,d^4x$. Integration by parts expresses this term as a boundary integral plus $(2\mu_0)^{-1}\int(\partial_aA^a)^2\,d^4x$. With the <Lorenz gauge> and fixed or vanishing boundary data, the two actions therefore differ only by a boundary term. Varying the component-gradient action without restriction gives the potential wave equations; the Lorenz condition must be supplied to recover all <Maxwell equations>.

= Bioelectromagnetism
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

= Volume conductor
{parent=Bioelectromagnetism}

A <volume conductor> is a conducting three-dimensional medium through which <electric currents> flow. In the quasistatic bioelectric model, the total <current density> is $\mathbf J=\mathbf J^p-\sigma\nabla u$, comprising an impressed source current and its Ohmic return current. <Electric charge conservation> gives $\nabla\cdot(\sigma\nabla u)=\nabla\cdot\mathbf J^p$. An insulating boundary has zero normal total current.

= Geselowitz formula
{c}
{parent=Volume conductor}
{title2=$\mathbf B=\mathbf B^p-\frac{\mu_0\sigma}{4\pi}\int_S u\,\mathbf n'\times\frac{\mathbf r-\mathbf r'}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|^3}\,dS'$}

For a homogeneous <volume conductor> with an insulating boundary $S$, the <Geselowitz formula> expresses the exterior <magnetic field> as the primary <Biot-Savart law> field minus a surface contribution from the induced <electric potential>. For a <current dipole>, $\mathbf B^p=\mu_0\mathbf Q\times(\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0)/(4\pi|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|^3)$. On a spherical boundary, $\mathbf n'$ is parallel to $\mathbf r'$, so the surface term has zero radial component at the observation point.

= Spherical volume conductor model
{parent=Volume conductor}
{title2=$|\mathbf r|<a$}

A <spherical volume conductor model> approximates a conducting body by a sphere, usually with uniform or radially stratified conductivity. It provides explicit forward models for <electroencephalography> and <magnetoencephalography>. Its symmetry makes a <radial current dipole> magnetically silent outside the sphere; departures from spherical symmetry need not retain that property.

= Sarvas formula
{c}
{parent=Spherical volume conductor model}
{title2=$\mathbf B=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\nabla\frac{(\mathbf Q\times\mathbf r_0)\cdot\mathbf r}{F}$}

For the exterior of a <spherical volume conductor model>, put $P=|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|$, $r=|\mathbf r|$ and $F=rP^2+P\mathbf r\cdot(\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0)$. The displayed <Sarvas formula> follows by taking the radial component of the <Geselowitz formula> and integrating the <magnetic scalar potential> from infinity. It is independent of a spherically symmetric conductivity profile. The sign shown uses $\mathbf B=+\mu_0\nabla U/(4\pi)$; conventions defining a magnetic potential with a minus sign reverse $U$. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3823129/[Sarvas's original paper] derives the spherical-conductor field and its consequences for the inverse problem.

= Spherical current-dipole logarithmic kernel
{parent=Sarvas formula}
{title2=$\phi=\frac1b\log\frac{r+P+b}{r+P-b},\quad b=|\mathbf r_0|$}

For $r>b$ and $P=|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|$, the displayed kernel satisfies $\phi=\int_r^\infty ds/[s|s\widehat{\mathbf r}-\mathbf r_0|]$. The limit at $b=0$ is $1/r$. Differentiating along $\mathbf Q\times\mathbf r_0$, which preserves $b$, gives $(\mathbf Q\times\mathbf r_0)\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf r_0}\phi=(\mathbf Q\times\mathbf r_0)\cdot\mathbf r/F$. Thus it generates the normalized <magnetic scalar potential> in the <Sarvas formula>.

= Maxwell–Chern–Simons one-form equation
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{title2=$d{*F}+kF=0$}

For a <differential one-form> $A$ on an oriented three-dimensional spacetime, varying $\frac12\int(F\wedge*F+kA\wedge F)$, with $F=dA$, gives $d*F+kF=0$. With signature $(-,+,+)$, ${*}^2=-1$ on one- and two-forms; writing $B=*F$ gives $dB-k*B=0$ and the identity $d*B=0$. An Abelian <gauge transformation> changes the action by $\frac k2\int_{\partial M}\Lambda F$. Thus the bulk field equation is invariant, while invariance of the integrated action depends on boundary conditions.

= Electromagnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{title2=$\mathbf E,\mathbf B$}
{wiki}

= Maxwell field
{synonym}
{c}

The combined <electric field> and <magnetic field> that determine the <Lorentz force> on a charged particle.

= Electrical circuit
{parent=Electromagnetism}

An electrical circuit connects components that carry <electric current> and sustain <electric potential difference> differences. Its dynamics may couple <electrical resistance>, <inductance> and <capacitance>.

= Electrical impedance
{parent=Electrical circuit}
{title2=$Z(\omega)=\widehat V/\widehat I$}
{wiki}

Electrical impedance is the ratio of voltage and current <complex amplitudes> for sinusoidal steady response at a specified <angular frequency>. With time dependence $e^{\mathrm i\omega t}$, an ideal <resistor>, <inductor> and <capacitor> have $Z_R=R$, $Z_L=\mathrm i\omega L$ and $Z_C=1/(\mathrm i\omega C)$. Their <electrical impedances> add in a <series RLC circuit>, giving $Z=R+\mathrm i(\omega L-1/(\omega C))$. The current <amplitude> is $V_0/|Z|$, so cancellation of the imaginary terms explains the <resonant series RLC response>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/15-3-rlc-series-circuits-with-ac][OpenStax University Physics 2, §15.3: impedance and series resonance]{ref}.

= Electrical reactance
{parent=Electrical impedance}
{title2=$X=\operatorname{Im}Z$}

Electrical reactance is the <imaginary part> of <electrical impedance>. With time dependence $e^{\mathrm i\omega t}$, it is $X_L=\omega L$ for an <inductor> and $X_C=-1/(\omega C)$ for a <capacitor>. In a <series RLC circuit> they cancel at $\omega=1/\sqrt{LC}$, while the <electrical resistance> remains.

= Electric battery
{parent=Electrical circuit}
{wiki}

= Battery
{synonym}

An <electric battery> supplies <energy> to an <electrical circuit> by moving <electric charge> through a terminal <electric potential difference>. In the ideal fixed-voltage model used in <fixed-voltage capacitor energy balance>, it maintains a prescribed terminal <electric potential difference> while <electric charge> flows to or from the <capacitor>.

= RL circuit
{c}
{parent=Electrical circuit}
{title2=$L\,dI/dt+RI=V$}
{wiki}

An <RL circuit> combines <electrical resistance> $R$ and <self-inductance> $L$. In the series model, the same <electric current> $I$ flows through both components. An ideal <battery> maintains terminal <electric potential difference> $V$; the drops in the direction of positive <electric current> obey <Ohm's law> and the <inductor> relation:
$$
v_R=RI,\qquad v_L=L\frac{dI}{dt},\qquad
V=v_R+v_L.
$$
This is <Kirchhoff's voltage law> with the <inductor>'s terminal drop, rather than its oppositely signed induced <electromotive force>. For constant $R>0$, $L>0$, and $V$, the <first-order linear differential equation> has solution
$$
I(t)=I_\infty+(I(0)-I_\infty)e^{-t/\tau},\qquad
I_\infty=\frac VR,\qquad \tau=\frac LR.
$$
The <time constant> $\tau$ measures <exponential decay> of the difference from the final <electric current>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-4-rl-circuits][OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.4: RL circuits]{ref}.

For a stipulated example, take <electrical resistance> $R=2\,\Omega$, <self-inductance> $L=0.5\,\mathrm H$, terminal <electric potential difference> $V=6\,\mathrm V$, and initial <electric current> $I(0)=0$. Then
$$
\tau=0.25\,\mathrm s,\qquad I_\infty=3\,\mathrm A,
\qquad I(t)=(3\,\mathrm A)(1-e^{-t/(0.25\,\mathrm s)}).
$$
With $t$ measured numerically in seconds, this is $I(t)=3(1-e^{-4t})$ in amperes. At one <time constant>,
$$
I(\tau)=3(1-e^{-1})\,\mathrm A\approx1.89636\,\mathrm A,
$$
and the terminal <electric potential difference> drops are
$$
v_R(\tau)=6(1-e^{-1})\,\mathrm V\approx3.79272\,\mathrm V,
\qquad v_L(\tau)=6e^{-1}\,\mathrm V\approx2.20728\,\mathrm V.
$$
Their sum is exactly $6\,\mathrm V$. At $t=0^+$, the <inductor> takes the entire terminal <electric potential difference> while the <electric current> is still zero. At steady <electric current>, the <inductor>'s terminal drop is zero and the entire drop is across the <electrical resistance>.

\Image[rl-current-and-voltage.png]
{title=<Electric current> and terminal <electric potential difference> drops during an <RL circuit> response}
{description=The <battery> maintains $6\,\mathrm V$ across <electrical resistance> $2\,\Omega$ and <self-inductance> $0.5\,\mathrm H$ in series. The dashed vertical line marks <time constant> $0.25\,\mathrm s$; $v_L$ uses the positive terminal-drop convention.}

The final <magnetic energy> stored by the <inductor> is $U_\infty=LI_\infty^2/2=2.25\,\mathrm J$. At one <time constant>, the <electric current> has reached about $63.21\%$ of its final value, but its <magnetic energy> has reached only about $39.96\%$:
$$
U(\tau)=\frac12L I(\tau)^2
=2.25(1-e^{-1})^2\,\mathrm J\approx0.899047\,\mathrm J.
$$
This difference follows from the square of <electric current> in the <inductor>'s <magnetic energy>.

For release, take $t=0$ at the start of a separate experiment with initial <electric current> $3\,\mathrm A$. Remove the <battery> from the model while maintaining a closed path through the same <electrical resistance> and <inductor>. Then
$$
L\frac{dI}{dt}+RI=0,\qquad
I(t)=(3\,\mathrm A)e^{-t/(0.25\,\mathrm s)}.
$$
Now $v_L=-v_R$: the induced <electromotive force> supports the continuing positive <electric current> as its magnitude decreases. This closed-loop model describes <exponential decay>, not opening the sole path and imposing an instantaneous jump to zero <electric current>. The <inductor>'s <magnetic energy> and the <power> of <Joule heating> are
$$
U(t)=(2.25\,\mathrm J)e^{-2t/\tau},\qquad
P_R(t)=RI(t)^2=(18\,\mathrm W)e^{-2t/\tau},\qquad
\frac{dU}{dt}=-P_R.
$$
Their <time constant> is $\tau/2=0.125\,\mathrm s$. The total <energy> dissipated by <Joule heating> is exactly the initial <magnetic energy>:
$$
\int_0^\infty P_R(t)\,dt
=(18\,\mathrm W)\frac{\tau}{2}=2.25\,\mathrm J.
$$
The second diagram in <inductor> shows this <energy> transfer. A finite closed path lets us predict both how long the <electric current> persists and how much <energy> becomes heat. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-4-rl-circuits][OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.4: decay in a closed RL loop]{ref}.

= RLC circuit
{c}
{parent=Electrical circuit}
{wiki}

An <RLC circuit> combines <electrical resistance> $R$, <inductance> $L$, and <capacitance> $C$. The <capacitor> stores <energy> in its <electric field>; the <inductor> stores <energy> in its <magnetic field>. Their exchange can produce an <oscillation>, while <Joule heating> removes <energy>. A <series RLC circuit> gives a particularly clear example: changing $R$ turns ringing into a direct return to equilibrium, and changing $L$ or $C$ tunes a <resonance>.

These circuits connect two useful viewpoints. In time, they describe how stored <energy> is released after switching. In <frequency>, they select a range of signals: the <resonant series RLC response> explains radio tuning and simple filters. The same mathematics describes a <damped harmonic oscillator>, so insight about mechanical ringing transfers directly to an <electrical circuit>. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_23.html#Ch23-S3][Feynman lectures, I.23.3: electrical and mechanical resonance]{ref}; \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_22.html#Ch22-S7][Feynman lectures, II.22.7: frequency filters]{ref}.

= Series RLC circuit
{parent=RLC circuit}
{title2=$Lq''+Rq'+q/C=V(t)$}

In a <series RLC circuit>, the same <electric current> $i=q'$ flows through the <resistor> with <electrical resistance> $R$, the <inductor>, and the <capacitor>. Choose $q$ as the signed <electric charge> on the <capacitor> plate into which positive $i$ flows. With terminal drops measured in that direction, <Kirchhoff's voltage law> gives
$$
v_R=Ri,\qquad v_L=Li',\qquad v_C=q/C,\qquad
Lq''+Rq'+q/C=V(t).
$$
Here $V(t)$ is the applied <electric potential difference>; assume constant $L>0$, $C>0$, and $R>0$. With the applied source set to zero and the loop kept closed, the free response satisfies
$$
q''+2\alpha q'+\omega_0^2q=0,\qquad
\alpha=\frac{R}{2L},\qquad \omega_0=\frac{1}{\sqrt{LC}}.
$$
The <angular frequency> $\omega_0$ is the value for lossless free <oscillation>; $\alpha$ is the decay parameter. The three possibilities are <underdamped RLC response> for $\alpha<\omega_0$, <critically damped RLC response> for $\alpha=\omega_0$, and <overdamped RLC response> for $\alpha>\omega_0$. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-6-rlc-series-circuits][OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.6: free RLC response]{ref}.

For a fair comparison, start each circuit with the same <electric charge> $q(0)=q_0$ and zero <electric current> $q'(0)=0$. Set $\tau=\omega_0t$ and $a=\alpha/\omega_0$; then $x=q/q_0$ obeys $d^2x/d\tau^2+2a\,dx/d\tau+x=0$. The plot compares $a=0.5,1,2$. These dimensionless curves apply to any $L,C$ after rescaling time and <electric charge>.

\Image[rlc-free-damping.png]
{title=Free <series RLC circuit> responses with identical <initial conditions>}
{description=Left: <electric charge> for <underdamped RLC response>, <critically damped RLC response>, and <overdamped RLC response>. Right: total stored <energy>, which decreases in all three cases even when <electric charge> oscillates. The normalization is $\omega_0=1$, $q_0=1$, $q'(0)=0$.}

The stored <energy> is the sum of <magnetic energy> and <capacitor energy>:
$$
E=\frac12Li^2+\frac{q^2}{2C}.
$$
Differentiating and using the circuit equation gives the complete <power> balance,
$$
E'=i\left(Li'+\frac qC\right)=V(t)i-Ri^2.
$$
In the free response, $E'=-Ri^2\leq0$: <Joule heating> accounts exactly for the lost <energy>. For the plotted <initial conditions>, $E(0)=q_0^2/(2C)$ and $E/E(0)=x^2+(dx/d\tau)^2$. Since every free solution tends to zero for $R>0$,
$$
\int_0^\infty Ri(t)^2\,dt=E(0).
$$
In the lossless limit $R=0$, <energy> stays constant and shuttles between the <capacitor> and <inductor>. Thus an <oscillation> of <electric charge> and a decrease of total <energy> are compatible statements.

The analogy with a <damped harmonic oscillator> is exact within these ideal models: <electric charge> corresponds to displacement, <electric current> to <velocity>, <inductance> to <mass>, $1/C$ to <spring constant>, and $R$ to the coefficient of a resisting force proportional to <velocity>. The two stored <energy> terms play the roles of <kinetic energy> and elastic <potential energy>. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_23.html#Ch23-S3][Feynman lectures, I.23.3: the oscillator analogy]{ref}.

Differentiating the driven equation also gives $Li''+Ri'+i/C=V'$. When using this equation to solve for <electric current>, retain the original constraint $Li'(0)+Ri(0)+q(0)/C=V(0)$: the initial <electric charge> fixes the initial rate of change of <electric current>.

= Resonant series RLC response
{parent=Series RLC circuit}
{title2=$I_{\mathrm{amp}}(\omega_0)=V_0/R$}

Drive a <series RLC circuit> with <electric potential difference> $V(t)=V_0\cos(\omega t)$, where $V_0>0$ and $\omega>0$. After the free transient decays, the <electric current> has the same <angular frequency> as the source:
$$
i_{\mathrm{ss}}(t)=I_{\mathrm{amp}}\cos(\omega t-\phi),\qquad
I_{\mathrm{amp}}(\omega)=\frac{V_0}{\sqrt{R^2+(\omega L-1/(\omega C))^2}},\qquad
\tan\phi=\frac{\omega L-1/(\omega C)}{R},\quad -\frac\pi2<\phi<\frac\pi2.
$$
For fixed positive $R,L,C,V_0$, the <electric current> <amplitude> is largest exactly at $\omega=\omega_0=1/\sqrt{LC}$. The two <electrical reactance> contributions cancel there, leaving only the drop from <electrical resistance>. In particular,
$$
i_{\mathrm{ss}}(t)=\frac{V_0}{R}\cos(\omega_0t),\qquad
q_{\mathrm{ss}}(t)=\frac{V_0}{R\omega_0}\sin(\omega_0t).
$$
Every solution approaches this periodic response because its difference from the displayed solution is a decaying free response. The peak remains at $\omega_0$ even for an <overdamped RLC response>; free ringing is not required for a maximum of driven <electric current>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/15-3-rlc-series-circuits-with-ac][OpenStax University Physics 2, §15.3: current amplitude, phase, and resonance]{ref}.

For example, stipulate $L=10\,\mathrm{mH}$, $C=1\,\mu\mathrm F$, $R=20\,\Omega$, and $V_0=1\,\mathrm V$. Then $\omega_0=10^4\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, $f_0=\omega_0/(2\pi)\approx1591.55\,\mathrm{Hz}$, and the peak <electric current> <amplitude> is $50\,\mathrm{mA}$. Holding the source <amplitude> fixed is essential when comparing responses at different <frequencies>.

\Image[rlc-current-resonance.png]
{title=Driven <electric current> <resonance> and the distinct <electric charge> peak}
{description=Left: fixed $L=1\,\mathrm H$, $C=1\,\mathrm F$, $V_0=1\,\mathrm V$, with positive $R=0.5,1,2,4\,\Omega$. Right: at $R=1\,\Omega$, the charge-amplitude peak, free ringing frequency, and current-amplitude peak occur at $\omega/\omega_0\approx0.707,0.866,1$, respectively. Both driven curves are divided by their own peak amplitude.}

Three <frequencies> must be distinguished. The free <underdamped RLC response> rings at $\omega_d=\sqrt{\omega_0^2-\alpha^2}$. Driven <electric current> peaks at $\omega_0$. Driven <electric charge> has <amplitude>
$$
q_{\mathrm{amp}}(\omega)=\frac{I_{\mathrm{amp}}(\omega)}{\omega}
=\frac{V_0/L}{\sqrt{(\omega_0^2-\omega^2)^2+4\alpha^2\omega^2}},
$$
whose peak occurs at $\sqrt{\omega_0^2-2\alpha^2}$ only if $\alpha<\omega_0/\sqrt2$. Otherwise it decreases from its zero-frequency limit $CV_0$, with no positive-frequency peak. The different peak locations follow because <electric current> is the time <derivative> of <electric charge>, multiplying a sinusoidal <amplitude> by $\omega$. The limiting responses are $I_{\mathrm{amp}}\sim V_0C\omega$ as $\omega\to0^+$ and $I_{\mathrm{amp}}\sim V_0/(L\omega)$ as $\omega\to\infty$.

The <Q factor> measures selectivity:
$$
Q=\frac{L\omega_0}{R}=\frac{\omega_0}{2\alpha}.
$$
The average <Joule heating> <power> is $RI_{\mathrm{amp}}^2/2$. Its half-maximum <angular frequencies> and their separation are exactly
$$
\omega_\pm=\sqrt{\omega_0^2+\alpha^2}\pm\alpha,\qquad
\Delta\omega=\omega_+-\omega_-=2\alpha=\frac RL=\frac{\omega_0}{Q}.
$$
These follow by setting $|\omega L-1/(\omega C)|=R$. In the numerical example, $Q=5$ and $\Delta\omega=2000\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$. Reducing $R$ makes a taller, narrower <electric current> peak; varying $C$ tunes the selected <frequency>. Measuring the drop $v_R=Ri$ gives a <band-pass filter> that suppresses very low and very high <frequencies> while passing a range near <resonance>. This is useful for selecting a radio signal, though the range must be broad enough to pass its modulation. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_22.html#Ch22-S7][Feynman lectures, II.22.7: resonant filters and radio sidebands]{ref}.

= Overdamped RLC response
{parent=Series RLC circuit}
{title2=$\alpha>\omega_0$}

An <overdamped RLC response> is a free <series RLC circuit> response with $R>2\sqrt{L/C}$, equivalently $\alpha>\omega_0$. Put $\beta=\sqrt{\alpha^2-\omega_0^2}$. The distinct negative <characteristic roots> are $r_\pm=-\alpha\pm\beta$, so the general <electric charge> is
$$
q(t)=Ae^{r_+t}+Be^{r_-t},\qquad i(t)=q'(t).
$$
For <initial conditions> $q(0)=q_0$, $i(0)=0$,
$$
q(t)=q_0e^{-\alpha t}\left(\cosh(\beta t)+\frac\alpha\beta\sinh(\beta t)\right),\qquad
i(t)=-\frac{q_0\omega_0^2}{\beta}e^{-\alpha t}\sinh(\beta t).
$$
For $q_0>0$, this <electric charge> decreases to zero without reversing sign. In the dimensionless example $\omega_0=1$, $\alpha=2$, the <characteristic roots> are $-2\pm\sqrt3\approx-0.268,-3.732$. The slow term dominates late times. Stronger <damping> can therefore make the final return slower: for $\alpha\gg\omega_0$, $r_+\sim-\omega_0^2/(2\alpha)=-1/(RC)$. Nonoscillation alone does not imply a fast response, and monotonicity here uses the specified <initial conditions>.

= Critically damped RLC response
{parent=Series RLC circuit}
{title2=$\alpha=\omega_0,\quad R=2\sqrt{L/C}$}

A <critically damped RLC response> is the boundary between <underdamped RLC response> and <overdamped RLC response>. The free <series RLC circuit> has a repeated <characteristic root> $-\omega_0$, with general solution
$$
q(t)=(A+Bt)e^{-\omega_0t}.
$$
For <initial conditions> $q(0)=q_0$, $i(0)=0$,
$$
q(t)=q_0(1+\omega_0t)e^{-\omega_0t},\qquad
i(t)=-q_0\omega_0^2t e^{-\omega_0t}.
$$
In the plotted normalization, $q(1)=2/e\approx0.735759$ and $i(1)=-1/e\approx-0.367879$. The <electric charge> returns without ringing; the <polynomial> factor $1+\omega_0t$ matters, so a single <exponential function> does not give the whole response.

For fixed $L,C$ and these <initial conditions>, <critical damping> avoids ringing while giving a faster asymptotic return than any larger $R$: the <overdamped RLC response> has a root closer to zero. This makes <critical damping> a useful tuning target for settling after switching. An MIT laboratory exercise makes the distinction observable by asking students to compare all three cases with $L=47\,\mathrm{mH}$ and $C=47\,\mathrm{nF}$. Those values give $R_{\mathrm{crit}}=2\sqrt{L/C}=2000\,\Omega$ and $\omega_0\approx21276.6\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$. \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-071j-introduction-to-electronics-signals-and-measurement-spring-2006/491e7e50ee65521c601ee51d3c6f7e5f_lab15_transients.pdf][MIT 6.071, Spring 2006, Laboratory 15, exercise 2]{ref}.

= Underdamped RLC response
{parent=Series RLC circuit}
{title2=$\omega_d=\sqrt{\omega_0^2-\alpha^2}$}

An <underdamped RLC response> is a free <series RLC circuit> response with $0<R<2\sqrt{L/C}$, equivalently $0<\alpha<\omega_0$. Its <characteristic roots> are $-\alpha\pm\mathrm{i}\omega_d$, and its general <electric charge> is
$$
q(t)=e^{-\alpha t}\left(A\cos(\omega_dt)+B\sin(\omega_dt)\right).
$$
For <initial conditions> $q(0)=q_0$, $i(0)=0$,
$$
q(t)=q_0e^{-\alpha t}\left(\cos(\omega_dt)+\frac\alpha{\omega_d}\sin(\omega_dt)\right),\qquad
i(t)=-\frac{q_0\omega_0^2}{\omega_d}e^{-\alpha t}\sin(\omega_dt).
$$
In the dimensionless example $\omega_0=1$, $\alpha=0.5$, the ringing <angular frequency> is $\omega_d=\sqrt3/2\approx0.866025$, below the driven <electric current> <resonance> at $\omega_0=1$. The <electric charge> first crosses zero at $t=4\pi/(3\sqrt3)\approx2.41840$ and reaches its first negative extremum at $t=2\pi/\sqrt3\approx3.62760$, where $q/q_0=-e^{-\pi/\sqrt3}\approx-0.163034$.

The sinusoidal factor changes sign while its envelope decays as $e^{-\alpha t}$. The total <energy> follows the exact balance $E'=-Ri^2$, rather than a pure <exponential decay> at every instant; in particular, its rate of loss is zero whenever $i=0$. As $\alpha\to0$, the displayed solution tends to the lossless <harmonic oscillator> $q=q_0\cos(\omega_0t)$. As $\alpha\to\omega_0$ from below, $\sin(\omega_dt)/\omega_d\to t$, recovering the <critically damped RLC response> continuously. The <overdamped RLC response> reaches the same limit through $\sinh(\beta t)/\beta\to t$.

= Gaussian units
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

= Capacitor
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A <capacitor> stores <electrostatic energy> by separating <electric charge> between two <electrical conductors>. In the ideal two-terminal model their <electric charges> are $+Q$ and $-Q$, with a vacuum or insulating gap preventing direct transfer between them. Write $V=\phi_+-\phi_-$ for the terminal <electric potential difference>, where $\phi_+$ and $\phi_-$ are the <electric potentials> of the two <electrical conductors>. A linear <capacitor> obeys
$$
Q=CV,
$$
where its <capacitance> $C$ depends on the geometry and <permittivity>, and is independent of the applied <electric potential difference> while these stay fixed. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance][OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.1: capacitors and capacitance]{ref}.

For example, the <parallel-plate capacitor> below has <capacitance> $C=88.54\,\mathrm{pF}$. At terminal <electric potential difference> $V=10\,\mathrm V$, the <electric charge> magnitude on either plate is
$$
Q=CV=(8.854\times10^{-11})(10)\,\mathrm C
=8.854\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm C=0.8854\,\mathrm{nC}.
$$
The total <electric charge> of the device is $Q+(-Q)=0$: <charge neutrality> does not imply zero <capacitor energy>. This <capacitor> stores $4.427\,\mathrm{nJ}$ in its <electric field>. The schematic in <parallel-plate capacitor> shows the separation of <electric charge>; the plots in <capacitor energy> show how storage grows with terminal <electric potential difference>.

The useful distinction is between storing net <electric charge> and storing separated <electric charge>. A <capacitor> can release <electrostatic energy> into an <electrical circuit>; in an ideal <RLC circuit>, <energy> moves between the <capacitor>'s <electric field> and the <magnetic field> associated with <inductance>. An ideal disconnected <capacitor> retains its separated <electric charges> and <capacitor energy> because there is no path for discharge.

= Parallel-plate capacitor
{parent=Capacitor}
{title2=$C=\epsilon A/d$}

A <parallel-plate capacitor> consists of two parallel <electrical conductors> with overlapping <area> $A$ and separation $d$. If the gap is small compared with both plate widths and is filled by a uniform material of positive <permittivity> $\epsilon$, its <capacitance> is approximately
$$
C\simeq\frac{\epsilon A}{d}.
$$
This neglects the <fringing field of a capacitor> near the plate edges. The interior <electric field> points from the plate of positive <electric charge> to the plate of negative <electric charge>, and is approximately uniform. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance][OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.1: the parallel-plate approximation]{ref}.

For a worked vacuum example, stipulate $A=0.01\,\mathrm{m^2}$, $d=0.001\,\mathrm m$, and <vacuum permittivity> $\epsilon_0\approx8.854\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{F/m}$. This rounds the value in \a[https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/Constants/Table/allascii.txt][NIST's current constants table, 2022 CODATA adjustment: vacuum electric permittivity]{ref}. Within the approximation,
$$
C\simeq\frac{(8.854\times10^{-12})(0.01)}{0.001}\,\mathrm F
=8.854\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm F=88.54\,\mathrm{pF}.
$$
At terminal <electric potential difference> $V=10\,\mathrm V$, the <electric field> magnitude is $E\simeq V/d=10^4\,\mathrm{V/m}$, the plate <electric charge> magnitude is $Q=CV=0.8854\,\mathrm{nC}$, and the <capacitor energy> is $U=CV^2/2=4.427\,\mathrm{nJ}$. These numbers use the stipulated rounded <vacuum permittivity>; finite plates also have corrections from the <fringing field of a capacitor>.

\Image[parallel-plate-capacitor-field.png]
{title=Uniform <electric field> in a <parallel-plate capacitor>}

The formula makes design changes easy to predict: doubling the overlapping <area> doubles the <capacitance>, and halving the gap doubles the <capacitance>, with <permittivity> fixed and the <fringing field of a capacitor> neglected. Doubling <permittivity> also doubles <capacitance> if the material uniformly fills the gap and remains linear. This lets a compact device store more separated <electric charge> at the same terminal <electric potential difference>.

The short derivation uses <surface charge density> $\sigma=Q/A$. In the interior, <Gauss's law> gives <electric field> magnitude $E\simeq\sigma/\epsilon=Q/(\epsilon A)$. The <electric potential difference> is $V\simeq Ed=Qd/(\epsilon A)$, so dividing $Q$ by $V$ gives $C\simeq\epsilon A/d$. A narrow curved <capacitor> gap has the same leading local approximation when its width is much less than the curvature radius.

= Fringing field of a capacitor
{parent=Parallel-plate capacitor}

= Capacitor fringing field
{synonym}

The <fringing field of a capacitor> is the nonuniform <electric field> near the edges of its <electrical conductors>, extending outside the gap. For a <parallel-plate capacitor>, neglecting it is useful when the gap is much smaller than both plate widths; the approximation $C\simeq\epsilon A/d$ is less reliable when these lengths become comparable.

= Spherical capacitor
{parent=Capacitor}
{title2=$C=4\pi\epsilon_0ab/(b-a)$}

Two concentric conducting spherical shells of radii $a<b$ with charges $Q,-Q$ have an <electric field> only in the intervening vacuum: $E_r=Q/(4\pi\epsilon_0r^2)$ by <Gauss's law>. Integrating gives a potential difference $Q(1/a-1/b)/(4\pi\epsilon_0)$, and hence the displayed <capacitance>. The <electrostatic energy> is $Q^2/(2C)$, either by charging work or by integrating $\epsilon_0|E|^2/2$ over the gap.

= Capacitor energy
{title2=$U=Q^2/(2C)$}
{parent=Capacitor}

The <capacitor energy> is the <electrostatic energy> stored when <electric charge> is separated. For a linear <capacitor> of fixed positive <capacitance> $C$, plate <electric charge> magnitude $Q$, and terminal <electric potential difference> $V=Q/C$, it is
$$
U=\frac{Q^2}{2C}=\frac12CV^2=\frac12QV.
$$
The zero is the uncharged state. Thus the <capacitor energy> is positive for nonzero $Q$, even though the total <electric charge> of the two plates is zero. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-3-energy-stored-in-a-capacitor][OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.3: energy stored in a capacitor]{ref}.

For the <parallel-plate capacitor> example, <capacitance> $C=8.854\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm F$ and terminal <electric potential difference> $V=10\,\mathrm V$ give
$$
U=\frac12(8.854\times10^{-11})(10)^2\,\mathrm J
=4.427\times10^{-9}\,\mathrm J=4.427\,\mathrm{nJ}.
$$
The plate <electric charge> magnitude is $Q=0.8854\,\mathrm{nC}$. Doubling terminal <electric potential difference> with <capacitance> unchanged doubles the plate <electric charge> magnitude and quadruples the <capacitor energy>.

\Image[capacitor-charge-and-energy.png]
{title=Linear <electric charge> and quadratic <capacitor energy> versus <electric potential difference>}

The <electric charge> is a <linear function> of terminal <electric potential difference>, while <capacitor energy> is a <quadratic function>. The second curve in each plot uses twice the <capacitance>: at a given terminal <electric potential difference>, both plate <electric charge> magnitude and <capacitor energy> double. These relations let us calculate how much <energy> an <electrical circuit> can receive when a <capacitor> discharges.

A brief charging argument explains the factor $1/2$. If the plate <electric charge> magnitude has reached $q$, the terminal <electric potential difference> is $q/C$. Transferring a further amount $dq$ of <electric charge> quasistatically requires <work> $dW=(q/C)\,dq$. With the geometry and <permittivity> fixed, the <integral> is
$$
U=\int_0^Q\frac qC\,dq=\frac{Q^2}{2C}.
$$
Using $Q=CV$ gives the other two forms. This <integral> accounts for the gradually rising terminal <electric potential difference>; it is the <work> stored in the <capacitor>'s <electric field>, rather than a claim that all <energy> drawn from a fixed-voltage <battery> during charging ends up stored.

The field picture gives the same result. In the vacuum gap of a <parallel-plate capacitor>, the <electric field> has <energy density> $u=\epsilon_0 E^2/2$. Multiplying by the gap <volume> $Ad$ gives the <electrostatic energy>
$$
U\simeq\frac12\epsilon_0E^2Ad
=\frac12\frac{\epsilon_0 A}{d}V^2
=\frac12CV^2.
$$
For the worked example, $u=4.427\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{J/m^3}$ and $Ad=10^{-5}\,\mathrm{m^3}$ reproduce $U=4.427\,\mathrm{nJ}$. The <electrostatic energy> resides in the <electric field> throughout the gap, despite overall <charge neutrality>.

When <capacitance> changes, specify the constraint. At fixed plate <electric charge> magnitude $Q$, increasing <capacitance> decreases <capacitor energy> $Q^2/(2C)$; doubling <capacitance> halves it. At fixed terminal <electric potential difference> $V$, increasing <capacitance> increases <capacitor energy> $CV^2/2$; doubling <capacitance> doubles it. In the latter case a connected <battery> supplies additional <electric charge> and <energy>, and there may also be mechanical <work>. Use the <fixed-voltage capacitor energy balance> to account for all transfers before inferring a <force> from changing <capacitor energy>.

= Fixed-voltage capacitor energy balance
{parent=Capacitor energy}
{title2=$dU_{\rm field}=dW_{\rm mech}+V\,dQ$}

For a quasistatic change of <capacitance> at fixed terminal <electric potential difference>, the <capacitor energy> is $U=CV^2/2$ and plate <electric charge> is $Q=CV$. Electrical <work> supplied to the <electric field> by the <battery> is $V\,dQ$, so mechanical <work> done on the <capacitor> is $dW_{\rm mech}=dU-V\,dQ=-V^2dC/2$. Increasing the separation of a <parallel-plate capacitor> makes $dC<0$: mechanical <work> is positive even though <electrostatic energy> decreases, because electrical <energy> returns to the source. Conversely, when $dC>0$, the <battery> supplies $V^2dC$: half increases <capacitor energy> and half is available as mechanical <work> done by the <electric field>. Differentiating <capacitor energy> alone at fixed terminal <electric potential difference> omits this exchange with the <battery>.

= Electromagnetic interaction
{parent=Electromagnetism}

The electromagnetic interaction is the long-range interaction of electrically charged matter with the electromagnetic field. Its massless gauge boson is the <photon>.

= Electromagnetic radiation
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

<Electromagnetic radiation> consists of propagating oscillations of the <electric field> and <magnetic field> that carry <energy> and <momentum>.

= X-ray
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}

X-rays are high-frequency <electromagnetic radiation>, conventionally with wavelengths of roughly $0.01$ to $10\,\mathrm{nm}$. The precise wavelength convention varies with context. Hot accreting gas and energetic electronic processes can emit X-rays, whose short wavelengths make them useful probes of compact objects and atomic structure.

= Larmor formula
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{c}
{title2=$P=\frac{q^2\mu_0a^2}{6\pi c}$}
{wiki}

The nonrelativistic radiated power of an accelerated point charge is $P=q^2\mu_0a^2/(6\pi c)$ in <SI> units. Integrating its dipole radiation pattern over solid angle gives this result; relativistic motion requires the Lienard generalization.

= Liénard radiated-power formula
{c}
{parent=Larmor formula}
{title2=$P=\mu_0q^2\gamma^6(|a|^2-|v\times a|^2)/(6\pi)$, $c=1$}

= Lienard formula
{c}
{synonym}

The relativistic generalization of the <Larmor formula> uses the invariant proper-acceleration magnitude: $P=-\mu_0q^2 A^aA_a/(6\pi)$ in signature $(+---)$. In coordinate time this is the displayed expression. For perpendicular magnetic circular motion, $\gamma m|a|=|q|vB$, giving $P=\mu_0q^4(\gamma^2-1)B^2/(6\pi m^2)$. The statement concerns total instantaneous emitted power; directional radiation flux is not itself invariant.

= Mie scattering
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{c}
{wiki}

The electromagnetic scattering solution for a sphere describes the transition between <Rayleigh scattering> and <geometrical optics>. Grain-size-dependent efficiencies can produce structure in the <radiation-pressure coefficient>.

= Bremsstrahlung
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{wiki}

<Bremsstrahlung> is <electromagnetic radiation> emitted when a charged particle accelerates during interaction with another charged particle or field. The microscopic process $e^-+Z\to e^-+Z+\gamma$ creates a <photon>, while its inverse absorbs one. Thus it contributes to a photon-number collision source in the <photon number current>, unlike ordinary <Compton scattering>. <Thermal bremsstrahlung> averages this emission over a thermal distribution of charged particles.

= Thermal radiation
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{wiki}

Thermal radiation is <electromagnetic radiation> emitted by matter because of its temperature. Matter in <thermal equilibrium> emits a spectrum governed by its absorptivity, approaching the <Planck law> for a <blackbody>.

= Scattering efficiency
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{title2=$Q_{\rm sca}=\sigma/(\pi a^2)$}

For a <sphere> of <radius> $a$, the <scattering efficiency> is its effective scattering <area> divided by the projected <geometric cross-section> $\pi a^2$. It is a <dimensionless quantity>. For <Rayleigh scattering by a small sphere> with relative <refractive index> $m$ and incident <wavenumber> $k$,
$$
Q_{\rm sca}=\frac{\sigma}{\pi a^2}=\frac83(ka)^4\left(\frac{m^2-1}{m^2+2}\right)^2.
$$
At fixed <wavelength> and fixed <refractive index>, the <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> scales as $a^6$, whereas the <scattering efficiency> scales as $a^4$. Doubling <radius> multiplies the former by $64$ and the latter by $16$. An efficiency here is an effective scattering <area> relative to a reference <area>, rather than a fraction of all light in an arbitrary beam.

For a worked toy example, take $a=10\,\mathrm{nm}$, $m=1.5$, and $\lambda=550\,\mathrm{nm}$. Then $ka=2\pi(10/550)\simeq0.1142$ and $(m^2-1)/(m^2+2)=5/17$. Substitution gives <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> $\sigma\simeq1.234\times10^{-20}\,\mathrm{m}^2$ and <scattering efficiency> $Q_{\rm sca}\simeq3.929\times10^{-5}$. At incident <light intensity> $1\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$, the scattered <power> is approximately $1.234\times10^{-20}\,\mathrm W$. These are leading small-particle estimates, not a measured air-<molecule> cross-section.

= Rayleigh scattering
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{wiki}

<Rayleigh scattering> is <elastic scattering> of <electromagnetic radiation> by particles much smaller than the incident <wavelength>. In the stationary particle approximation, the scattered <electromagnetic radiation> keeps the incident <frequency>: its direction changes, rather than its color. The incident <electric field> induces an oscillating <electric dipole moment>, which produces <electric dipole radiation>. This is a useful example of how a microscopic response can explain a familiar large-scale sight, <why the sky is blue>. \a[https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/lectures/node108.html][Richard Fitzpatrick, Rayleigh Scattering: bound-charge response and wavelength dependence]{ref}.

The smallness condition for <Rayleigh scattering by a small sphere> of <radius> $a$ is $ka\ll1$, where the <wavenumber> is $k=2\pi/\lambda$ and $\lambda$ is the surrounding medium's <wavelength>. For ordinary materials with modest <refractive index>, the incident <electric field> then varies little across the particle, making the <electric-dipole approximation for radiation> useful. For very large <refractive index>, also check the internal size $|m|ka\ll1$ before applying the simple <electric dipole> model. Once these conditions fail, use <Mie scattering> for a <sphere> instead of extrapolating the <Rayleigh scattering wavelength dependence>.

Historically, the 1871 paper \a[https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Scientific_Papers/Volume_1/On_the_Light_from_the_Sky,_Its_Polarization_and_Colour][Rayleigh, On the Light from the Sky, Its Polarization and Colour: original-paper transcription]{ref} connected the color and <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> of scattered skylight with particles smaller than the <wavelength>. Its <dimensional analysis> anticipates scattered <light intensity> proportional to particle <volume> squared and to $\lambda^{-4}$. The original mechanical picture is historical; the modern explanation uses induced <electric dipole radiation>.

= Rayleigh scattering by a small sphere
{c}
{parent=Rayleigh scattering}
{title2=$\sigma=\frac{8\pi}{3}k^4a^6\left(\frac{m^2-1}{m^2+2}\right)^2$}

= Rayleigh scattering cross-section
{c}
{synonym}

Consider a fixed, homogeneous, nonabsorbing, nonmagnetic <sphere> of <radius> $a$ in <vacuum>. Let $m$ be its relative <refractive index>, a fixed <real number>; in <vacuum> this equals the <sphere>'s <refractive index>. Treat $m$ as independent of <wavelength> over the range being compared, neglecting <optical dispersion>. Air can be approximated by <vacuum> here because its <refractive index> is close to one. These assumptions describe a toy particle, rather than a literal model of an individual air <molecule>.

The <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> $\sigma$ is an effective <area>: the total scattered <power> is $P_{\rm sca}=I_{\rm inc}\sigma$, where $I_{\rm inc}$ is the incident <light intensity>. For $ka\ll1$ its leading approximation is
$$
\sigma=\frac{8\pi}{3}k^4a^6\left(\frac{m^2-1}{m^2+2}\right)^2,\qquad k=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}.
$$
The units are <area>, since $k^4a^6$ has units $\mathrm{m}^{-4}\mathrm{m}^6=\mathrm{m}^2$. This <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> measures scattering, not conversion of the incident <electromagnetic radiation> into <heat>. The definition follows \a[https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/lectures/node107.html][Fitzpatrick, Thomson Scattering: total and differential scattering cross-sections]{ref}.

To see the mechanism, the nearly uniform applied <electric field> produces an <electric dipole moment> with peak magnitude
$$
p_0=4\pi\epsilon_0a^3\frac{m^2-1}{m^2+2}E_0,
$$
where $E_0$ is the incident <electric field> peak <wave amplitude> and $\epsilon_0$ is <vacuum permittivity>. This is the <sphere>'s response in <electrostatics>, with its <permittivity> divided by <vacuum permittivity> equal to $m^2$ under the nonmagnetic assumption. \a[https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/lectures/node42.html][Fitzpatrick, Boundary Value Problems with Dielectrics, equation 554: induced sphere dipole]{ref}. The averaged <electric dipole radiation> <power> is $P_{\rm sca}=\omega^4p_0^2/(12\pi\epsilon_0c^3)$, with <angular frequency> $\omega=ck$ and <speed of light> $c$. Dividing by $I_{\rm inc}=\epsilon_0cE_0^2/2$ gives the displayed <Rayleigh scattering cross-section>. Thus doubling <radius> multiplies the <electric dipole moment> by eight and the scattered <power> by $64$, provided the larger <sphere> still satisfies $ka\ll1$. \a[https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node95.html][Fitzpatrick, Electric dipole radiation: angular power and its integral]{ref}.

= Rayleigh scattering wavelength dependence
{c}
{parent=Rayleigh scattering}
{title2=$\sigma\propto\lambda^{-4}$}

For a fixed <sphere> and fixed <refractive index>, both the <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> and <scattering efficiency> are proportional to $\lambda^{-4}$. Halving <wavelength> multiplies either by $16$, as long as the shorter <wavelength> still satisfies $ka\ll1$. If <optical dispersion> changes $m$, the material factor changes too; the pure fourth-power comparison then needs correction.

For equal incident <light intensities> at blue <wavelength> $450\,\mathrm{nm}$ and red <wavelength> $650\,\mathrm{nm}$, the same toy <sphere> gives
$$
\frac{\sigma(450\,\mathrm{nm})}{\sigma(650\,\mathrm{nm})}=\left(\frac{650}{450}\right)^4=\frac{28561}{6561}\simeq4.35315.
$$
Blue <light> therefore produces about $4.35$ times the scattered <power>. This compares the particle's response at two <wavelengths>; it does not assert that an actual sky has this ratio of observed blue and red <light intensities>.

\Image[rayleigh-wavelength-scaling.png]
{title=<Rayleigh scattering wavelength dependence> for a fixed small <sphere>, normalized at $650\,\mathrm{nm}$}
{description=The curve is $(650\,\mathrm{nm}/\lambda)^4$ over $350$ to $750\,\mathrm{nm}$. Blue and red comparison points are marked at $450$ and $650\,\mathrm{nm}$. Fixed <radius> and nondispersive <refractive index> are assumed, with $ka\ll1$ throughout. This is a particle response curve, not a simulated sky <optical spectrum>.}

= Rayleigh scattering angular pattern
{c}
{parent=Rayleigh scattering}
{title2=$F(\theta)=(1+\cos^2\theta)/2$}

For incident <unpolarized light> and the isotropic <electric dipole> response of <Rayleigh scattering by a small sphere>, let $\theta$ be the angle between the incident beam's propagation direction and the outgoing direction. The <differential scattering cross-section> and its relative angular pattern are
$$
\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}=\frac{3\sigma}{16\pi}(1+\cos^2\theta),\qquad F(\theta)=\frac{1+\cos^2\theta}{2},
$$
where $d\Omega$ is an element of <solid angle>. Averaging over the two transverse directions of incident <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> gives this pattern. Forward scattering at $0^\circ$ and backward scattering at $180^\circ$ are equally strong: $F=1$. Sideways scattering at $90^\circ$ is weakest, but nonzero: $F=1/2$. \a[https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/lectures/node107.html][Fitzpatrick, Thomson Scattering, equations 1283–1287: averaging the dipole angular pattern]{ref}.

\Image[rayleigh-angular-scattering.png]
{title=<Rayleigh scattering angular pattern> for incident <unpolarized light>, measured from the incident beam}
{description=The plotted radial coordinate is $F(\theta)$, a relative <differential scattering cross-section>, rather than the distance travelled by <light>. The incident propagation axis points right: $0^\circ$ is forward, $180^\circ$ backward, and $90^\circ$ sideways. Rotating this plane about the incident axis gives the three-dimensional pattern.}

The normalization is consistent because
$$
\int(1+\cos^2\theta)\,d\Omega=2\pi\int_0^\pi(1+\cos^2\theta)\sin\theta\,d\theta=\frac{16\pi}{3}.
$$
Thus integrating $d\sigma/d\Omega$ gives the total <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> $\sigma$. The plotted $F$ has maximum one; it is not itself normalized to unit <solid angle> integral.

For a single <electric dipole> driven by incident <linear polarization>, the <angular distribution of electric dipole radiation> instead has a $\sin^2\alpha$ factor, where $\alpha$ is measured from the <electric dipole moment> direction, parallel to the incident <electric field>. That direction is perpendicular to the incident beam. Confusing $\alpha$ with the beam angle $\theta$ would incorrectly predict zero forward scattering. Averaging the dipole patterns over incident <unpolarized light> yields the displayed $F(\theta)$.

= Why the sky is blue
{parent=Rayleigh scattering}

In a clear <terrestrial atmosphere>, <Rayleigh scattering> by air <molecules> redirects some sunlight toward an observer looking away from the <Sun>. Shorter <wavelengths> are preferentially scattered, enriching the scattered <light> in blue compared with the incoming sunlight. Along the longer atmospheric path to the low <Sun>, more short-<wavelength> <light> is removed from the direct beam, helping produce red sunsets. \a[https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/blue-sky/en/][NASA Space Place, Why Is the Sky Blue?: scattered sunlight and red sunsets]{ref}.

Violet <light> has an even shorter <wavelength> than blue <light>, so the <Rayleigh scattering wavelength dependence> alone cannot predict the perceived sky color. The incoming solar <optical spectrum> and the eye's differing response to <wavelengths> both matter; human vision is less sensitive to violet than to blue. \a[https://www.weather.gov/fgz/SkyBlue][NOAA National Weather Service, Why Is The Sky Blue?: solar spectrum and visual response]{ref}. <Multiple scattering> and other atmospheric particles also affect the observed sky. Cloud droplets are generally too large for this simple <Rayleigh scattering> model: <Mie scattering> describes spherical droplets, with much weaker visible <wavelength> selectivity, while repeated scattering helps make clouds appear white. \a[https://www.giss.nasa.gov/edu/icp/research/ppa/1997/reyes/][NASA GISS, Aerosols in the Atmosphere: particle size, Mie scattering and white clouds]{ref}.

= Right-hand rule
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

The right-hand rule fixes the orientation of a <cross product> and relates the circulation direction of an <electric current> to the direction of its <magnetic field>.

= Permittivity
{title2=$\epsilon$}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

Permittivity describes a medium's response to an <electric field>. In a linear isotropic medium, the electric displacement is $\mathbf D=\epsilon\mathbf E$; <vacuum permittivity> is its vacuum value $\epsilon_0$.

= Vacuum permittivity
{title2=$\epsilon_0$}
{parent=Permittivity}
{wiki}

Vacuum permittivity is the electromagnetic constant $\epsilon_0$ appearing in <Coulomb's law> and the SI vacuum <Maxwell equations>.

= Electric charge
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

Electric charge is the conserved source of the electric field and the coupling carried by charged matter.

= Elementary charge
{parent=Electric charge}
{wiki}
{title2=$e=1.602176634\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm C$}

The <elementary charge> is the positive magnitude $e$ of the <electron>'s <electric charge>; the <electron> has <electric charge> $-e$. Its <SI> value is fixed exactly. Multiplying it by the <Avogadro constant> gives the <Faraday constant>. \a[https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/wall_2022.pdf][NIST, CODATA 2022 defining constants]{ref}.

= Charge neutrality
{parent=Electric charge}

A system is charge neutral when its total positive and negative <electric charge> cancel.

= Charge conservation
{parent=Electric charge}
{wiki}
{title2=$\partial_t\rho+\nabla\cdot\mathbf J=0$}

= Conservation of electric charge
{synonym}

= Electric charge conservation
{synonym}

<Charge conservation> says that the total signed <electric charge> of an isolated collection stays constant. More generally, a collection closed to <electric charge> exchange has constant total <electric charge>, even if <energy> crosses its boundary. This is the charge bookkeeping relevant to a <closed system> or an <isolated system>; isolation from <energy> exchange is not required. Positive and negative <electric charge> count with opposite signs. Creating or removing an <electron> and a <positron> together is compatible with <charge conservation>, because their combined <electric charge> is zero. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_04.html#Ch4-S4][Feynman, Leighton and Sands, The Feynman Lectures on Physics, I.4.4: net charge conservation]{ref}.

Imagine a fixed box initially containing <electric charge> $Q(0)=10\,\mu\mathrm C$. Stipulate incoming <conventional current> $I_{\mathrm{in}}=3\,\mu\mathrm A$ and outgoing <conventional current> $I_{\mathrm{out}}=1\,\mu\mathrm A$ throughout $0\le t\le2\,\mathrm s$, with no other boundary transport. Since one <ampere> is one <coulomb> per second, the stored <electric charge> grows at $2\,\mu\mathrm{C/s}$:
$$
\frac{dQ}{dt}=I_{\mathrm{in}}-I_{\mathrm{out}}=2\,\mu\mathrm{C/s},\qquad Q(t)=10\,\mu\mathrm C+(2\,\mu\mathrm{C/s})t,\qquad Q(2\,\mathrm s)=14\,\mu\mathrm C.
$$
If instead the outgoing <electric current> also equals $3\,\mu\mathrm A$, the stored <electric charge> remains $10\,\mu\mathrm C$ while <electric charge> passes through. These are stipulated transport examples; constant terminal <electric currents> alone do not guarantee a time-independent interior <charge density>.

\Image[charge-conservation-control-volume.png]
{title=Signed <electric current> through a fixed <control volume>}
{description=The dashed box is an accounting boundary. Outward <unit normals> point left at the inlet and right at the outlet: the respective contributions to outward <electric current> are $-I_{\mathrm{in}}$ and $+I_{\mathrm{out}}$. Arrow lengths are schematic.}
{source=charge-conservation-control-volume.py}

\Image[charge-storage-versus-steady-current.png]
{title=Stored <electric charge> with unequal and balanced terminal <electric currents>}
{description=Synthetic curves from the stipulated rates over $0\le t\le2\,\mathrm s$, with the same initial <electric charge>; these are not measurements. Balanced transport keeps total <electric charge> fixed, and is compatible with steady transport.}
{source=charge-storage-versus-steady-current.py}

Locally, <charge conservation> is the <charge continuity equation>, an instance of the general <continuity equation>:
$$
\partial_t\rho+\nabla\cdot\mathbf J=0.
$$
Here $\rho(\mathbf r,t)$ is signed volume <charge density> and $\mathbf J(\mathbf r,t)$ is <current density>. A positive <divergence> of <current density> means net outward transport from a small neighborhood, so its <charge density> decreases. For a fixed <control volume> $V$ with <closed surface> $\partial V$ and outward <unit normal> $\mathbf n$, the equivalent integrated balance is
$$
Q_V(t)=\int_V\rho\,dV,\qquad \frac{dQ_V}{dt}=-\oint_{\partial V}\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf n\,dA=-I_{\mathrm{out,net}}.
$$
The <surface integral> is signed: incoming positive <electric charge> contributes negatively to outward <electric current>, and outgoing positive <electric charge> contributes positively. Integrate the <charge continuity equation> over fixed $V$ and apply the <divergence theorem> to obtain this balance. Zero net boundary transport gives constant $Q_V$; the global and local statements describe the same <conservation law> at different scales. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_13.html#Ch13-S2][Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.13.2: local and integrated charge conservation]{ref}.

In a steady transport state, <charge density> and <current density> are time-independent, so the <charge continuity equation> reduces to $\nabla\cdot\mathbf J=0$. At an <electrical circuit> junction, choose each terminal <electric current> $I_k$ positive outward and assume the terminals account for all boundary transport. Then
$$
\sum_k I_k=-\frac{dQ_{\mathrm{junction}}}{dt}.
$$
<Kirchhoff's current law>, $\sum_k I_k=0$, follows when accumulation of <electric charge> at the junction is negligible. During storage, retain the same balance with its nonzero <derivative>; <charge conservation> continues to hold. This makes <charge conservation> useful for checking <electrical circuit> models and for tracking the charging of a <capacitor>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-3-kirchhoffs-rules][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics II, §10.3: Kirchhoff's junction rule]{ref}.

The beautiful consistency with <Maxwell equations> is that the <electric field> and <magnetic field> already enforce this transport balance. For sufficiently smooth fields in vacuum <SI>, take the <divergence> of the <Ampère-Maxwell equation>, use that the <divergence of a curl is zero>, and substitute <Gauss's law>, $\nabla\cdot\mathbf E=\rho/\epsilon_0$. Constant <vacuum permittivity> $\epsilon_0$ gives
$$
0=\nabla\cdot\mathbf J+\epsilon_0\partial_t(\nabla\cdot\mathbf E)=\nabla\cdot\mathbf J+\partial_t\rho.
$$
The <displacement current> term supplies the part needed when <electric charge> accumulates. Historically, Maxwell added that term to extend the steady-current equation; see the existing <charge conservation from Maxwell equations> for this connection. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_18.html#Ch18-S1][Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.18.1: Maxwell's added term and charge conservation]{ref}.

= Charge density
{parent=Electric charge}
{wiki}

Charge density is electric charge per unit volume, area, or length according to the dimension of its support.

= Linear charge density
{parent=Charge density}
{title2=$\lambda=dQ/d\ell$}

= Charge per unit length
{synonym}

Linear charge density is <electric charge> divided by length along a charged line, or locally $dQ/d\ell$. Its SI unit is coulombs per metre. For a uniformly charged long cylindrical conductor, it equals the total <surface charge density> integrated around the cylinder's circumference; it need not represent a charge literally supported on a one-dimensional line.

= Line charge
{parent=Charge density}
{title2=$\mu=dQ/d\ell$}

A line charge is a charge distribution idealized as lying on a curve, described by charge per unit length. An infinite straight line with constant density $\mu$ produces the radial <electric field> $E_r=\mu/(2\pi\varepsilon_0r)$ by <Gauss's law>, and a logarithmic <electrostatic potential>.

= Electric current
{parent=Electric charge}
{wiki}
{title2=$I=dQ_{\mathrm{cross}}/dt$}

An <electric current> measures how quickly signed <electric charge> crosses a chosen fixed <oriented surface> $S$. Choose its <unit normal> $\mathbf n$; positive <electric charge> crossing along $\mathbf n$ counts positively, while crossing against it counts negatively. If $Q_{\mathrm{cross}}(t)$ is the accumulated signed <electric charge> transported through $S$, then
$$
I_S(t)=\frac{dQ_{\mathrm{cross}}}{dt}=\int_S\mathbf J(\mathbf r,t)\cdot\mathbf n\,dA,\qquad Q_{\mathrm{cross}}(t_2)-Q_{\mathrm{cross}}(t_1)=\int_{t_1}^{t_2}I_S(t)\,dt.
$$
Here $\mathbf J$ is the <current density>; the <surface integral> collects its local normal contributions. Reversing the <unit normal> reverses $I_S$. The <electric current> is a signed <scalar> associated with the whole chosen <oriented surface>, whereas <current density> is a <vector> at each point. The <SI> unit is the <ampere>, one <coulomb> per second. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-1-electrical-current][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics II, §9.1: transported charge and current]{ref}; \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-2-model-of-conduction-in-metals][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.2: current density and its surface integral]{ref}.

For a constant <electric current> of $2\,\mathrm A$ over a <time> interval of $3\,\mathrm s$, the transported <electric charge> is $6\,\mathrm C$. This need not be <electric charge> accumulating in the wire: in steady transport, <electric charge> enters and leaves. For a closed <oriented surface> with outward <unit normal>, <electric charge conservation> instead gives $I_{\mathrm{out}}=-dQ_{\mathrm{inside}}/dt$. Keeping transported and enclosed <electric charge> distinct connects <electrical circuit> bookkeeping to the local <continuity equation>.

= Conventional current
{parent=Electric current}

<Conventional current> uses the direction in which positive <electric charge> flows. A moving negative <electric charge> contributes <electric current> in the opposite direction to its <velocity>. Thus <electrons> drifting left produce <conventional current> to the right; the convention describes signed <electric charge> transport, without requiring positive <charge carriers>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-1-electrical-current][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.1: conventional current direction]{ref}.

= Ampere
{parent=Electric current}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm A=\mathrm{C/s}$}

The <ampere> is the <SI> unit of <electric current>. Since 20 May 2019 its definition fixes the <elementary charge> exactly at $e=1.602176634\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm C$, with $\mathrm C=\mathrm{A\,s}$. This gives electrical measurements a reference based on a fixed <elementary charge>. \a[https://www.nist.gov/si-redefinition/ampere-introduction][NIST, Ampere: introduction to the 2019 definition]{ref}; \a[https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/Constants/Value/e.html][NIST, CODATA value of the elementary charge]{ref}.

= Current density
{parent=Electric current}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathbf J$}

= Electric current density
{synonym}

The <current density> $\mathbf J(\mathbf r,t)$ describes the amount and direction of local <electric charge> transport per <area> per <time>. Its <SI> unit is $\mathrm{A/m^2}$. Through a small <area element> with <unit normal> $\mathbf n$, the contribution to <electric current> is $dI=\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf n\,dA$: the <dot product> selects transport through the surface. A <current density> tangent to the surface contributes zero. If <current density> is uniform and perpendicular to a wire's transverse slice, its magnitude $j=|\mathbf J|$ satisfies
$$
I=jA,\qquad j=\frac IA\quad\text{with the normal chosen along conventional current}.
$$
Here $A$ is the <wire cross-sectional area>. For nonuniform <current density>, $I/A$ is only the average normal component, and the <surface integral> is needed. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-2-model-of-conduction-in-metals][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.2: local current density]{ref}.

Consider two separate uniform-flow cases, each carrying <electric current> $I=2\,\mathrm A$. A <wire cross-sectional area> of $1\,\mathrm{mm^2}=10^{-6}\,\mathrm{m^2}$ gives $j=2\times10^6\,\mathrm{A/m^2}$; doubling the <wire cross-sectional area> to $2\,\mathrm{mm^2}$ gives $j=10^6\,\mathrm{A/m^2}$. Equal <electric current> can therefore mean different local transport intensity. This distinction matters for <Joule heating>, whose local <power> per volume depends on <current density> and <electric field>.

\Image[current-density-and-wire-area.png]
{title=Equal <electric current> through different <wire cross-sectional areas> gives different <current densities>}
{description=Both cases carry $2\,\mathrm A$ with uniform normal <current density>. The transverse slices use the same spatial scale; the bars compare $j=I/A$.}
{source=current-density-and-wire-area.py}

For a toy <electron> transport model, stipulate mobile <electron number density> $n=8\times10^{28}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}$ and <drift velocity> $\mathbf v_d=-10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m/s}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$, with the positive <unit normal> $\widehat{\mathbf x}$. Each <electron> has <electric charge> $-e$, where the <elementary charge> $e=1.602176634\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm C$ is exact in <SI>. These are defined model inputs, rather than measured parameters of a particular material. \a[https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/Constants/Value/e.html][NIST, exact elementary charge]{ref} The <current density> and its magnitude are
$$
\mathbf J=n(-e)\mathbf v_d=j\widehat{\mathbf x},\qquad j=ne|\mathbf v_d|=1.2817413072\times10^6\,\mathrm{A/m^2}.
$$
For <wire cross-sectional area> $A=1\,\mathrm{mm^2}$ and constant transport over <time> $10\,\mathrm s$, this gives
$$
I=jA=1.2817413072\,\mathrm A,\qquad \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cross}}=I\Delta t=12.817413072\,\mathrm C.
$$
The two minus signs make <conventional current> point opposite to the <electron> <drift velocity>. A small <drift velocity> can carry an appreciable <electric current> because many <charge carriers> contribute; it does not specify the propagation speed of an electrical disturbance.

= Wire cross-sectional area
{parent=Current density}
{title2=$A$}

The <wire cross-sectional area> is the <area> of a slice perpendicular to the wire's axis. For a round wire of radius $a$, it is $A=\pi a^2$. It enters both the uniform-flow formula $I=jA$ for <current density> and the formula $R=L/(\sigma A)$ for the <electrical resistance> of a uniform <Ohmic conductor>.

= Charge carrier
{parent=Current density}
{wiki}

A <charge carrier> transports <electric charge> by moving: <electrons> are one example, and <ions> are another. For species $s$ with <number density> $n_s$, <electric charge> $q_s$ and mean <velocity> $\mathbf v_s$, the transport contributions add as $\mathbf J=\sum_s n_sq_s\mathbf v_s$. A stationary charged background contributes to <charge density> but not to this <current density>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-1-electrical-current][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.1: positive and negative carriers]{ref}.

= Drift velocity
{parent=Charge carrier}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathbf v_d$}

The <drift velocity> is a <charge carrier> population's mean <velocity> relative to its material's rest frame. It describes the net transport superposed on microscopic motion. For a single species, <current density> is $\mathbf J=nq\mathbf v_d$; for <electrons>, $q=-e$ reverses the direction. It is distinct from the propagation speed of an electrical disturbance. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-2-model-of-conduction-in-metals][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.2: drift and transport]{ref}.

= Current dipole
{parent=Current density}
{title2=$\mathbf J^p(\mathbf r)=\mathbf Q\delta(\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0)$}

A <current dipole> idealizes a localized impressed <electric current density> by the displayed <Dirac delta function> distribution. Its moment $\mathbf Q$ has units of current times length. In a homogeneous <volume conductor> of conductivity $\sigma$, its primary <electric potential> is $\Psi(\mathbf r;\mathbf r_0)=\mathbf Q\cdot(\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0)/(4\pi\sigma|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|^3)$.

= Radial current dipole
{parent=Current dipole}
{title2=$\mathbf Q\times\mathbf r_0=0$}

A <radial current dipole> has moment parallel to its displacement from the sphere centre. The <Sarvas formula> gives identically zero exterior <magnetic field> for such a source in a <spherical volume conductor model>, although its <electric potential> need not vanish. This is a nonuniqueness mechanism for the inverse problem in <magnetoencephalography>.

= Four-current
{parent=Current density}
{title2=$J^\mu=(c\rho,\boldsymbol J)$}

The four-current combines charge density and <current density> into the <four-vector> $J^\mu=(c\rho,\boldsymbol J)$. With $x^\mu=(ct,\boldsymbol x)$, its <four-divergence> vanishes by the charge <continuity equation>: $\partial_\mu J^\mu=\partial_t\rho+\nabla\cdot\boldsymbol J=0$. It is the source in the covariant <Maxwell equations>.

= Surface current density
{title2=$\mathbf K$}
{parent=Current density}
{wiki}

A surface current density is electric current per unit transverse length confined to a surface. Integrating a thin layer's <current density> through its thickness gives $\mathbf K$.

= Current sheet
{parent=Surface current density}
{wiki}

A current sheet idealizes electric current as supported on a surface. It produces a jump in the tangential <magnetic field>.

= Force on a current sheet from the mean magnetic field
{parent=Current sheet}
{title2=$\mathbf f_S=\mathbf K\times(\mathbf B_++\mathbf B_-)/2$}

The <Lorentz force> per unit area on an ideal <current sheet> is computed using the mean of its limiting <magnetic fields>. The sheet's own local <magnetic field> is equal and opposite on its two sides and cancels in this mean. Equivalently, integrate the <Lorentz force density> through a thin regularized layer: its self-field contribution averages to zero, leaving the external field acting on the <surface current density>.

= Magnetic-field jump across a surface current
{parent=Current sheet}

For a surface current density $\mathbf K$ on an interface with unit normal $\mathbf n$, <Ampère's law> gives
$$
\mathbf n\times(\mathbf B_+-\mathbf B_-)=\frac{4\pi}{c}\mathbf K
$$
in Gaussian units, or $\mu_0\mathbf K$ in <SI> units.

= Surface conductivity
{title2=$\Sigma$}
{parent=Surface current density}

Surface conductivity is the conductivity of a thin layer integrated through its thickness. The sheet form of <Ohm's law> is $\mathbf K=\Sigma\mathbf E_\parallel$.

= Line current
{parent=Electric current}

A line current idealizes an <electric current> as flowing along a curve; its current-density volume integral reduces to $I\,d\ell$ along that curve.

= Electromotive force
{parent=Electric current}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathcal E$}

<Electromotive force> is the driving <work> per unit <electric charge> around an <electrical circuit>, measured in <volts>, despite its name containing "force". For a stationary loop its electromagnetic contribution is $\mathcal E=\oint_C\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l$; for a moving <electrical conductor>, include the <motional electromotive force>. A nonzero closed-loop <line integral> of the <electric field> cannot be represented by a single-valued <electric potential> alone. An <electromotive force> specifies the drive; the resulting <electric current> also depends on the <electrical resistance> and <self-inductance>. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_17.html#Ch17-S1][Feynman lectures, II.17.1: emf as a circuit integral]{ref}.

= Motional electromotive force
{parent=Electromotive force}
{title2=$\mathcal E_{\rm mot}=\oint_C(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\mathbf l$}

<Motional electromotive force> is the contribution $\oint_C(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\mathbf l$ from an <electrical conductor> moving with local <velocity> $\mathbf v$ through a <magnetic field>. The <cross product> comes from the magnetic part of the <Lorentz force>. For a straight rod of length $\ell$, perpendicular to both its <velocity> and a uniform <magnetic field>, its magnitude is $B\ell v$. The sign depends on the direction of integration along the rod. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-3-motional-emf][OpenStax University Physics 2, §13.3: motional emf]{ref}.

For a computed example, take $\ell=0.50\,\mathrm m$, <velocity> $\mathbf v=2.0\widehat{\mathbf x}\,\mathrm{m/s}$ to the right, and <magnetic field> $\mathbf B=0.40\widehat{\mathbf z}\,\mathrm T$ out of the page. The rod lies along $y$. Then
$$
\mathbf v\times\mathbf B=-0.80\widehat{\mathbf y}\,\mathrm{V/m},\qquad
\int_{\mathrm{top}}^{\mathrm{bottom}}(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\mathbf l
=B\ell v=0.40\,\mathrm V.
$$
Positive <electric charge> is driven downward. For an isolated rod after charge separation has equilibrated, $\mathbf E=-\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$ inside it, and the <electric potential difference> is $V_{\mathrm{bottom}}-V_{\mathrm{top}}=+0.40\,\mathrm V$; the bottom is positive. This open-circuit statement does not imply a sustained <electric current>.

Close the rod's path with stationary U-shaped rails and an <electrical resistance> $R>0$ at the left. With out-of-page <unit normal>, the positive loop direction is counterclockwise, hence upward along the rod. If the rod's horizontal position is $x(t)$, the enclosed <area> is $A=\ell x$ and the external <magnetic flux> is $\Phi_{\mathrm{ext}}=B\ell x$. Thus
$$
\mathcal E_{\mathrm{CCW}}=-B\ell\dot x=-0.40\,\mathrm V,\qquad
I_{\mathrm{CCW}}=-\frac{0.40\,\mathrm V}{R}.
$$
Here the resistive approximation neglects <self-inductance>. The negative <electric current> is clockwise, downward along the rod, consistent with the charge-separation calculation and <Lenz's law>.

\Image[motional-emf-sliding-rod.png]
{title=<Motional electromotive force> from a rod sliding right through an out-of-page <magnetic field>}
{description=The U-shaped return path closes the <electrical circuit> to the left of the rod. Increasing <area> increases positive <magnetic flux>; orange arrows show the clockwise induced <electric current>. The rod's bottom end is positive in the isolated-rod limit. The <electromotive force> has magnitude $0.40\,\mathrm V$, or signed value $-0.40\,\mathrm V$ for counterclockwise orientation.}

The closed-loop result illustrates why the moving-circuit form of <Faraday's law of induction> includes <motional electromotive force>: the <magnetic field> is constant in time, so the <electric field> circulation alone would miss this drive. Its mechanical consequence is <electromagnetic braking of a sliding rod>.

= Electromagnetic braking of a sliding rod
{parent=Motional electromotive force}
{title2=$\tau=mR/(B^2\ell^2)$}

A conducting rod sliding on rails separated by $\ell$ in a transverse magnetic field has induced current magnitude $B\ell v/R$ when self-inductance is neglected and resistance is $R$. The <Lorentz force> opposes motion with magnitude $B^2\ell^2v/R$, so free motion decays exponentially on the displayed time constant. <Joule heating> integrates to the rod's lost kinetic energy. Current direction follows the <Lenz's law>.

= Magnetic flux transport for a moving circuit
{parent=Motional electromotive force}
{title2=$\frac{d\Phi}{dt}=\int_S\partial_t\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S-\oint_C(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\mathbf r$}

For a smoothly moving circuit and a divergence-free <magnetic field>, the <magnetic flux> derivative is $d\Phi/dt=\int_S\partial_t\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S-\oint_C(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\mathbf r$. The minus sign follows by orienting the swept side surface compatibly with the two spanning surfaces. Combining this transport identity with <Faraday's law> yields the moving-loop electromotive-force law.

= Electrical resistance and conductance
{parent=Electric current}
{wiki}

For an ohmic element, electrical resistance is $R=V/I$ and conductance is its reciprocal $G=I/V$. They describe the same voltage-current response using reciprocal units. The relation $V=IR$ is <Ohm's law>.

= Electrical conductance
{parent=Electrical resistance and conductance}
{title2=$G=1/R$}

For an ohmic element with finite nonzero <electrical resistance> $R$, its <electrical conductance> is $G=1/R$: an <electric potential difference> $\Delta V$ drives <electric current> $I=G\Delta V$. Greater positive <electrical conductance> means a greater <electric current> for the same positive <electric potential difference>. <Electrical conductivity> is instead a bulk material coefficient relating <current density> to <electric field>.

= Electrical resistance
{parent=Electrical resistance and conductance}
{title2=$R$}

<Electrical resistance> measures the <electric potential difference> needed to drive a given <electric current> through a component. For an <Ohmic conductor> under fixed material conditions, $R=V/I$ is constant and <Ohm's law> gives $V=IR$. The <SI> unit is the <ohm>, $1\,\Omega=1\,\mathrm{V/A}$. At a fixed positive <electric potential difference>, increasing <electrical resistance> reduces <electric current>. <Electrical conductance> $G=1/R$ expresses the same response reciprocally. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-3-resistivity-and-resistance][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.3: resistance and its unit]{ref}.

For a straight uniform wire at rest, with constant <electrical conductivity> $\sigma>0$, <length> $L$ and <wire cross-sectional area> $A$, the uniform <Ohmic conductor> model gives
$$
R=\frac{L}{\sigma A}=\rho_{\mathrm{el}}\frac LA,\qquad \rho_{\mathrm{el}}=\frac1\sigma.
$$
The <electrical resistivity> $\rho_{\mathrm{el}}$ characterizes the material; <electrical resistance> also includes geometry. Doubling <length> doubles $R$, while doubling <wire cross-sectional area> halves $R$, with material and <temperature> fixed. The uniform-field assumption includes insulated sides and equipotential end faces, as explained in <uniform current in a straight homogeneous wire>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-3-resistivity-and-resistance][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.3: wire geometry and resistivity]{ref}.

As a defined wire example, take $L=1\,\mathrm m$, $A=1\,\mathrm{mm^2}$ and $\sigma=5\times10^5\,(\Omega\,\mathrm m)^{-1}$. Then $R=2\,\Omega$; applying <electric potential difference> $5\,\mathrm V$ drives <electric current> $2.5\,\mathrm A$. With the same <length> and material, a second wire of <wire cross-sectional area> $2\,\mathrm{mm^2}$ has $R=1\,\Omega$ and carries $5\,\mathrm A$ at that same <electric potential difference>. Fixing <electric potential difference> here differs from fixing <electric current> in the <current density> comparison: stating what is held fixed is essential.

= Ohm
{parent=Electrical resistance}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Omega=\mathrm{V/A}$}

The <ohm> is the <SI> unit of <electrical resistance>, one <volt> per <ampere>. A component of <electrical resistance> $2\,\Omega$ needs an <electric potential difference> of $2\,\mathrm V$ to carry <electric current> $1\,\mathrm A$ under <Ohm's law>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-3-resistivity-and-resistance][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.3: the ohm]{ref}.

= Resistor
{parent=Electrical resistance}
{wiki}

A resistor is an <electrical circuit> component whose intended behavior is <electrical resistance>. An ideal ohmic resistor obeys <Ohm's law>, $V=IR$, and converts electrical <energy> into heat at <power> $P=I^2R$ through <Joule heating>. It provides the dissipative term in a <series RLC circuit>.

= Ohm's law
{title2=$V=IR$}
{parent=Electrical resistance}
{c}
{wiki}

<Ohm's law> states that an <Ohmic conductor> has <electric current> proportional to its applied <electric potential difference>, with constant <electrical resistance> under fixed material conditions:
$$
V=IR,\qquad I=\frac VR.
$$
Here $V=\phi(A)-\phi(B)$ is the terminal <electric potential difference>, and positive $I$ enters at $A$ and exits at $B$. This sign convention makes $VI$ the electrical <power> absorbed. The straight-line relation through the origin lets one constant predict the response at many applied <electric potential differences>. Merely defining $R=V/I$ at one nonzero operating point does not establish <Ohm's law>: a component may have a ratio that varies with applied <electric potential difference>. Material and <temperature> must be specified and held fixed; many components have a nonlinear response. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-4-ohms-law][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.4: Ohmic and non-Ohmic responses]{ref}.

For the ideal <resistor> model $R=2\,\Omega$ at <electric potential difference> $V=5\,\mathrm V$,
$$
I=\frac{5\,\mathrm V}{2\,\Omega}=2.5\,\mathrm A,\qquad P=VI=I^2R=\frac{V^2}{R}=12.5\,\mathrm W.
$$
This <power> is converted to <internal energy> by <Joule heating>; a <watt> is a <joule> per second. If the model holds for <time> $10\,\mathrm s$, the transferred <energy> is $125\,\mathrm J$. Doubling <electric potential difference> doubles <electric current> and quadruples <power> for the same fixed $R$. The model keeps material <temperature> fixed even while calculating <Joule heating>; appreciable changes in <temperature> may require a changing <electrical resistance>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-5-electrical-energy-and-power][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.5: electrical power and heating]{ref}.

\Image[ohmic-wire-voltage-and-current.png]
{title=<Ohm's law> gives linear <electric current> and quadratic <Joule heating> <power> at fixed <electrical resistance>}
{description=The ideal model holds $R=2\,\Omega$ fixed. At $V=5\,\mathrm V$, the marked values are $I=2.5\,\mathrm A$ and $P=12.5\,\mathrm W$.}
{source=ohmic-wire-voltage-and-current.py}

In 1827, <Georg Ohm> published his mathematical account of electrical transport in \i[Die galvanische Kette, mathematisch bearbeitet]. On printed page 36, his relation $S=A/L$ expresses <electric current> in terms of total driving tension and a reduced <length> incorporating <electrical conductivity> and geometry. This is historical notation: his $A$ is not the <wire cross-sectional area> used here, and his $L$ is not simply the wire's physical <length>. The striking idea is that material response and geometry can be combined into one quantity governing an <electrical circuit>. \a[https://archive.org/details/diegalvanischek00ohmgoog][Ohm, Die galvanische Kette, mathematisch bearbeitet (1827), p. 36, original edition]{ref}.

= Georg Ohm
{parent=Ohm's law}
{c}
{wiki}

Author of the 1827 treatise developing the electrical transport relation now called <Ohm's law>. \a[https://archive.org/details/diegalvanischek00ohmgoog][Ohm, original 1827 treatise]{ref}.

= Electrical conductivity
{parent=Ohm's law}
{title2=$\sigma$}

<Electrical conductivity> $\sigma$ measures the local response of <current density> to <electric field>. In the rest frame of an <isotropic> <Ohmic conductor>, the local form of <Ohm's law> is
$$
\mathbf J=\sigma\mathbf E,\qquad [\sigma]=\frac{\mathrm{A/m^2}}{\mathrm{V/m}}=(\Omega\,\mathrm m)^{-1}.
$$
Larger positive <electrical conductivity> gives larger <current density> at the same <electric field>. It is a bulk material coefficient, distinct from the geometry-dependent <electrical conductance> of a whole component and from a sheet's <surface conductivity>. <Electrical resistivity> is $\rho_{\mathrm{el}}=1/\sigma$ in this scalar model. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-3-resistivity-and-resistance][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.3: conductivity and resistivity]{ref}.

The local and whole-wire forms fit together particularly neatly. For <uniform current in a straight homogeneous wire>, with <length> $L$, <wire cross-sectional area> $A$, constant <electrical conductivity> and terminal <electric potential difference> $V$, the axial <electric field> has magnitude $E=V/L$. Thus
$$
I=jA=\sigma EA=\frac{\sigma A}{L}V,\qquad R=\frac{L}{\sigma A}.
$$
For the defined <electrical resistance> example $\sigma=5\times10^5\,(\Omega\,\mathrm m)^{-1}$, $L=1\,\mathrm m$, $A=10^{-6}\,\mathrm{m^2}$ and $V=5\,\mathrm V$, the local values are $E=5\,\mathrm{V/m}$ and $j=2.5\times10^6\,\mathrm{A/m^2}$; integrating gives $I=2.5\,\mathrm A$. One material law therefore supplies both a spatial description and a terminal relation.

The scalar form requires <isotropy>. In an <anisotropic> material the response can instead be a <tensor>, $J_i=\sum_j\sigma_{ij}E_j$, and <current density> need not be parallel to <electric field>; <uniaxial conductivity tensor> is one existing example. These statements concern the material's rest frame. A moving <electrical conductor> instead uses the <moving-conductor Ohm law>.

= Electrical resistivity
{parent=Electrical conductivity}
{title2=$\rho_{\rm el}=1/\sigma$}

Electrical resistivity is the inverse of scalar <electrical conductivity> in an isotropic Ohmic medium. In its rest frame, $\mathbf E=\rho_{\rm el}\mathbf j$, so the <electric field> does work per unit volume $\mathbf j\cdot\mathbf E=\rho_{\rm el}|\mathbf j|^2$. In <Gaussian units>, finite conductivity gives <magnetic diffusivity> $\eta=c^2/(4\pi\sigma)$; neglecting this diffusion is a distinct approximation from neglecting electron inertia in <Hall magnetohydrodynamics>.

= Uniaxial conductivity tensor
{parent=Electrical conductivity}
{title2=$\sigma=\alpha I+\gamma nn^{\mathsf T},\quad|n|=1$}

A uniaxial <electrical conductivity> has one distinguished direction $n$ and equal response in its orthogonal plane. Its eigenvalues are $\alpha$ in $n^\perp$ and $\alpha+\gamma$ along $n$, so it is invertible precisely when $\alpha(\alpha+\gamma)\ne0$. For an <electric field> perpendicular to $n$, the <electric current density> is $J=\alpha E$. Positive dissipation requires both eigenvalues to be nonnegative, a physical constraint separate from algebraic invertibility. For $D_{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}n_k$, one has $D^2=nn^{\mathsf T}-I$, and $\sigma D^2=-\sigma$ is equivalent to $\gamma=-\alpha$; the conductivity then vanishes along $n$.

= Electrical conductor
{parent=Electrical conductivity}
{wiki}

An electrical conductor supports mobile charge carriers and hence an electric current under a field. In a band description, a partially filled band or available overlapping bands supply nearby accessible states. The contrast with a <band insulator> depends on band filling as well as spectral gaps; <semiconductors> provide carriers through thermal excitation or doping.

= Ohmic conductor
{c}
{parent=Electrical conductivity}
{title2=$\mathbf J=\sigma\mathbf E$}

A material whose <current density> is proportional to its <electric field>, with proportionality given by its <electrical conductivity>.

= Uniform current in a straight homogeneous wire
{parent=Ohmic conductor}
{title2=$\mathbf j=I\mathbf e_z/(\pi a^2)$}

In a homogeneous <Ohmic conductor>, a steady <electric current density> obeys $\nabla\cdot\mathbf j=0$ and $\nabla\times\mathbf j=0$. Uniform axial current in a straight cylinder follows if its side is insulated and its flat end faces are equipotentials. The <electric potential> then solves <Laplace's equation> with those boundary conditions, and uniqueness makes it linear in the axial coordinate. Steadiness alone does not impose these contact conditions: nonuniform end potentials can produce nonuniform current even with an insulating side. Bending also generally redistributes current; in an annular conductor the azimuthal current density is proportional to the inverse radius.

= Charge relaxation
{parent=Ohmic conductor}
{title2=$\tau=\epsilon/\sigma$}

In a homogeneous <Ohmic conductor>, the <charge continuity equation> and <Gauss's law> imply exponential decay of the bulk <charge density>, $\rho(t)=\rho(0)e^{-\sigma t/\epsilon}$. For an isolated finite body, bulk charge moves to its boundary; the accumulating <surface charge density> preserves total <electric charge>. The formula uses a constant permittivity $\epsilon$ and the corresponding free-charge convention, or $\epsilon_0$ for total charge in the vacuum form of <Gauss's law>.

= Charge relaxation in an isolated conducting cylinder
{parent=Charge relaxation}
{title2=$\rho(t)=\rho_0e^{-\sigma t/\epsilon_0}$}

A uniform cylindrical volume charge relaxes exponentially under <Ohm's law> and the <continuity equation for electric charge>. In a cylinder of radius $R$ initially carrying no surface charge, the radial conduction current deposits $\rho_s(t)=R\rho_0(1-e^{-\sigma t/\epsilon_0})/2$ on its surface. Total charge per unit length remains $\pi R^2\rho_0$, so the exterior radial <electric field> is time independent even though the interior field decays. Neglecting the deposited surface charge violates charge conservation.

= Drude model
{c}
{parent=Electrical conductivity}

The <Drude model> describes classical charge carriers accelerated by an applied electric field and randomized by collisions of constant mean waiting time $\tau$. Its mean drift obeys $\dot v+v/\tau=eE/m$, and harmonic conductivity is $ne^2\tau/[m(1-i\omega\tau)]$ in the negative-frequency exponential convention.

= High-frequency Drude plasma cutoff
{parent=Drude model}
{title2=$\omega_p^2=ne^2/(m\epsilon_0)$}

The <Drude model> relative permittivity is $1-\omega_p^2/[\omega(\omega+i/\tau)]$. At frequencies large compared with the collision rate but below $\omega_p$, its leading real part is negative and the physical spatial wave-number branch has positive imaginary part. The field therefore decays into the conductor; close to the threshold the full complex response must be retained.

= Joule heating
{c}
{parent=Ohm's law}
{wiki}

= Ohmic heating
{c}
{synonym}

= Electrical dissipation
{synonym}

Joule heating converts electrical work into internal <energy>. A resistor dissipates $P=IV=I^2R$; in an ohmic continuum the local rate per volume is $\mathbf j\cdot\mathbf E=\eta j^2$, with resistivity $\eta$. Wind-driven currents are a proposed source of <heating of an inflated giant planet>.

= Watt
{parent=Joule heating}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm W=\mathrm{J/s}$}

The <watt> is the <SI> unit of <power>, one <joule> per second. <Joule heating> at $12.5\,\mathrm W$ transfers $125\,\mathrm J$ of <energy> over a <time> of $10\,\mathrm s$ if that <power> stays constant. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-5-electrical-energy-and-power][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.5: power in watts]{ref}.

= Electric field
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

The electric field is the <force> per unit charge on a stationary test particle.

= Electric force
{parent=Electric field}
{title2=$\mathbf F_E=q\mathbf E$}

The <electric force> on an <electric charge> $q$ is $\mathbf F_E=q\mathbf E$, using the <electric field> of the other sources and excluding the charge's own self-field. Positive <electric charge> gives <force> along the <electric field>; negative <electric charge> reverses it. A field $\mathbf E=400\,\mathrm{N/C}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$ therefore gives $\mathbf F_E=-8\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm N\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$ on $q=-2\,\mathrm{nC}$. This is the electric part of the <Lorentz force>; the magnetic contribution is $q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-4-electric-field][OpenStax, University Physics II, §5.4: electric field and force]{ref}.

= Electric flux
{parent=Electric field}
{title2=$\Phi_E=\int_S E\cdot n\,dS$}
{wiki}

<Electric flux> measures how much <electric field> crosses a chosen <oriented surface>, with a sign distinguishing the two crossing directions. A strong <electric field> running along a surface contributes nothing to its <electric flux>: only the <normal component> counts. This lets <Gauss's law> turn a potentially complicated <electric field> into one number recording the net enclosed <electric charge>.

For a surface $S$ with chosen <unit normal> $\mathbf n$, the <electric flux> is the <surface integral>
$$
\Phi_E(S)=\int_S\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf n\,dA=\int_S\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf S,\qquad d\mathbf S=\mathbf n\,dA.
$$
Here $dA$ is positive <surface area>, while $d\mathbf S$ is the <vector area element>. The <dot product> makes <electric flux> a signed <scalar>, rather than a <vector> like the <electric field>. Its <SI> unit is $\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}$: <electric field> measured using the <newton (unit)> per <coulomb>, multiplied by <surface area> in square metres. Reversing the <orientation of a surface> replaces $\mathbf n$ by $-\mathbf n$ and reverses the <electric flux>. Calling it <electric flux> does not imply that <electric charge> is moving through the surface. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_03.html#Ch3-S2][Feynman lectures II.3, §3–2: flux of a vector field]{ref}.

For an open patch in a <plane>, with <surface area> $A$ and a constant <electric field>, the <unit normal> is constant and the <surface integral> simplifies to
$$
\Phi_E=\mathbf E\cdot(A\mathbf n)=EA\cos\theta,\qquad E=|\mathbf E|.
$$
The <angle> $\theta$ is between the <electric field> and the chosen <unit normal>, not between the <electric field> and the <plane>. The <cosine> describes the signed projected <surface area> seen by the <electric field>. Thus <electric flux> is positive for $0\leq\theta<90^\circ$, zero at $90^\circ$, and negative for $90^\circ<\theta\leq180^\circ$. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-1-electric-flux][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics II, §6.1: electric flux]{ref}.

For example, take $E=100\,\mathrm{N/C}$ and $A=0.02\,\mathrm{m^2}$, so $EA=2\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}$. Three choices of <angle> give
$$
\begin{aligned}
\Phi_E(0^\circ)&=2\cos0^\circ=2\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C},\\
\Phi_E(60^\circ)&=2\cos60^\circ=1\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C},\\
\Phi_E(120^\circ)&=2\cos120^\circ=-1\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Keeping the $60^\circ$ patch fixed and reversing its <unit normal> gives $120^\circ$ and changes $+1$ to $-1\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}$. The <electric field> and physical patch have not changed. If the <electric field> is instead tangent to the patch, its <dot product> with the <unit normal> vanishes: <electric flux> is zero even though $E=100\,\mathrm{N/C}$.

\Image[open-surface-electric-flux.png]
{title=Three open patches in a <plane> in a constant <electric field>: the <angle> to the chosen <unit normal> gives <electric fluxes> of 2, 1 and minus 1 newton square metres per <coulomb>}

For a <closed surface> enclosing a volume $V$, choose the outward <unit normal>. <Gauss's law> gives
$$
\Phi_E(\partial V)=\oint_{\partial V}\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf n\,dA=\frac{Q_{\mathrm{enclosed}}}{\varepsilon_0}.
$$
Here $\varepsilon_0$ is the <vacuum permittivity>, and $Q_{\mathrm{enclosed}}$ is the signed total <electric charge> inside. The <electric field> in the <surface integral> includes all sources, including external <electric charges>. Assume no ideal <point charge> lies on the integration surface. Zero enclosed <electric charge> implies zero total <electric flux>; it does **not** imply zero <electric field> everywhere on the <closed surface> or inside it. Positive and negative contributions to the <surface integral> can cancel. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-2-explaining-gausss-law][Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics II, §6.2: outward flux and enclosed charge]{ref}.

To see the cancellation with sources inside, place <point charges> $+q$ at $(-a,0,0)$ and $-q$ at $(a,0,0)$, where $q>0$, $a=0.30\,\mathrm m$, and both lie inside the <sphere> of radius $R=1\,\mathrm m$ centered at the origin. This separated pair has the <electric dipole> geometry. Its enclosed <electric charge> is $q-q=0$, so its total outward <electric flux> is zero. At the point $P=(0,0,R)$ on the <sphere>, <Coulomb's law> and the <superposition principle> give the individual <electric fields>
$$
\mathbf E_+(P)=\frac{kq(a,0,R)}{(R^2+a^2)^{3/2}},\qquad
\mathbf E_-(P)=\frac{kq(a,0,-R)}{(R^2+a^2)^{3/2}},\qquad k=\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}.
$$
The $z$ components cancel and the positive $x$ components add:
$$
\mathbf E(P)=\frac{2kqa}{(R^2+a^2)^{3/2}}\widehat{\mathbf x}\approx0.527244\frac{kq}{R^2}\widehat{\mathbf x}\ne\mathbf0.
$$
For the stipulated approximation $k=8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C^2}$ and $q=1\,\mathrm{nC}$, this is about $4.740\,\mathrm{N/C}$ along positive $x$. At $P$, the outward <unit normal> is $\widehat{\mathbf z}$, so this nonzero <electric field> is tangent to the <sphere>. At other points of the <sphere>, its <normal component> can be positive or negative. The source-field formula applies away from the two <point charges>, where the <electric field> has zero <divergence>; it is singular at the sources. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_04.html#Ch4-S2][Feynman lectures II.4, §§4–1 and 4–2: electrostatic sources and superposition]{ref}.

\Image[zero-net-flux-nonzero-field.png]
{title=Cross-section of the three-dimensional <electric field> of two opposite <point charges> inside a unit-radius <sphere>: zero total <electric flux> coexists with a nonzero tangential <electric field> at the top}

The diagram shows <streamlines> of the three-dimensional <electric field> in the section $y=0$. The circle is a cross-section of the <sphere>, not the integration surface: <Gauss's law> uses the entire three-dimensional <surface integral>. A two-dimensional <line integral> of outward field around that circle would be a different quantity, with units $\mathrm{N\,m/C}$ rather than the <electric flux> units $\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}$.

= Electric potential
{parent=Electric field}
{title2=$\Phi$}
{wiki}

= Electrostatic potential
{synonym}

In <electrostatics>, the <electric potential> $\Phi$ describes <potential energy> per unit <electric charge> in a prescribed <electric field>. A test <electric charge> $q$ has interaction <potential energy> $U=q\Phi$ up to a reference constant. The <electric potential> is a <scalar>, whereas the <electric field> is a <vector>: one spatial <function> encodes all three field components through its negative <gradient>,
$$
\mathbf E=-\nabla\Phi,\qquad E_x=-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x},\quad E_y=-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial y},\quad E_z=-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial z}.
$$
The <electric field> points toward decreasing <electric potential>. A positive test <electric charge> experiences <force> in that direction; a negative test <electric charge> experiences <force> in the opposite direction, since $\mathbf F=q\mathbf E$. This is why the sign of the test <electric charge> matters even when the source <electric potential> stays the same. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-4-determining-field-from-potential][OpenStax, University Physics II, §7.4: determining field from potential]{ref}.

For finitely many stationary <point charges> $q_j$ at positions $\mathbf r_j$ in <vacuum>, choosing $\Phi\to0$ at infinite distance gives
$$
\Phi(\mathbf r)=k\sum_j\frac{q_j}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_j|},\qquad
\mathbf E(\mathbf r)=k\sum_j q_j\frac{\mathbf r-\mathbf r_j}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_j|^3},\qquad
k=\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}.
$$
These formulas apply away from the source positions. The <superposition principle> adds <electric potentials> as signed <scalars> and <electric fields> as <vectors>. When computing the <force> on one source <point charge>, omit its own term: the field in $\mathbf F_i=q_i\mathbf E_{\mathrm{other}}(\mathbf r_i)$ is produced by the other charges. The singular self-field is not part of the mutual <Coulomb's law> force. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_04.html#Ch4-S2][Feynman lectures II.4, §§4–2 to 4–4: source fields, superposition, and potential]{ref}.

The <electric potential> is determined only up to an additive constant $\Phi\mapsto\Phi+\Phi_0$: its <gradient> and every <electric potential difference> stay fixed. One can set zero at infinity for a finite collection of <point charges>, or use an <electrical reference potential> such as ground. These are choices of baseline, rather than different <electric fields>. The <SI> unit is the <volt>, $1\,\mathrm V=1\,\mathrm{J/C}$. Thus a fixed source $Q=+2\,\mathrm{nC}$ at distance $r=0.20\,\mathrm m$, using the stipulated approximation $k=8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C^2}$, gives
$$
\Phi=\frac{kQ}{r}=89.9\,\mathrm V,\qquad
E_r=-\frac{d}{dr}\frac{kQ}{r}=\frac{kQ}{r^2}=449.5\,\mathrm{N/C}.
$$
A test <electric charge> $q=-3\,\mathrm{nC}$ there has $U=q\Phi=-2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J$ and inward <force> $qE_r=-1.3485\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm N$. This recovers both the <Coulomb potential energy> and <Coulomb's law> calculations from the same <electric potential>.

The negative <gradient> formula implies zero <curl>, $\nabla\times\mathbf E=\mathbf0$, wherever $\Phi$ is twice continuously differentiable. Conversely, a continuously differentiable <curl-free vector field> admits a potential locally; on a <simply connected> open region it admits one globally. On more general regions, global <path independence> requires zero <line integral> around every closed path. For the finite-source formula above, the explicit single-valued $\Phi$ already supplies the global potential on the region excluding the source points. This ties <electrostatics> to the <fundamental theorem for line integrals>. A time-dependent <magnetic field> can instead produce nonzero electric <curl> through <Faraday's law of induction>, so a spatial <electric potential> alone no longer describes the entire <electric field>.

For a concrete two-source example, place $+q$ at $(a,0,0)$ and $-q$ at $(-a,0,0)$, with $q>0$. This separated charge pair gives the <electric dipole> geometry. In the plane $z=0$, introduce <dimensionless> coordinates $X=x/a$, $Y=y/a$ and the potential scale $C=kq/a$, which has units of <volts>. Then
$$
\frac{\Phi}{C}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{(X-1)^2+Y^2}}-\frac{1}{\sqrt{(X+1)^2+Y^2}}.
$$
At $(X,Y)=(2,0)$, $\Phi/C=1-1/3=2/3$, while $E_x/(kq/a^2)=1-1/9=8/9$ and $E_y=0$. Taking $q=2\,\mathrm{nC}$, $a=0.10\,\mathrm m$ and the same stipulated $k$ gives $C=179.8\,\mathrm V$, $\Phi\approx119.867\,\mathrm V$ and $E_x\approx1598.222\,\mathrm{N/C}$. At the midpoint, the two <electric potentials> cancel but the two <electric fields> reinforce:
$$
\Phi(0,0,0)=0,\qquad \mathbf E(0,0,0)=-\frac{2kq}{a^2}\widehat{\mathbf x}=-3596\,\mathrm{N/C}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}.
$$
Zero <electric potential> therefore need not mean zero <electric field>. The <equipotentials> in the diagram meet the field arrows perpendicularly wherever the <gradient> is nonzero; the arrow lengths are normalized to show direction. The contours are <level sets> of the computed <electric potential>, rather than paths of moving charges.

\Image[electrostatic-dipole-potential.png]
{title=Computed dipole equipotentials and electric-field directions: zero potential on the vertical midline still permits a nonzero field}

= Electrical reference potential
{parent=Electric potential}
{title2=$\Phi_{\mathrm{ref}}=0$}

An <electrical reference potential> fixes the arbitrary additive constant of an <electric potential>. Assigning zero to ground makes other potentials differences from that baseline; it does not assert that the <electric field> vanishes there. If a source calculation gives $\Phi(a)=89.9\,\mathrm V$ and $\Phi(b)=179.8\,\mathrm V$ relative to infinity, choosing $a$ as the zero instead gives $\Phi'(a)=0$ and $\Phi'(b)=89.9\,\mathrm V$, with the same <electric potential difference>. Grounding an <electrical conductor> additionally connects it to a reservoir that can exchange <electric charge> to maintain the prescribed <electric potential>; that physical boundary condition differs from merely relabeling an unchanged solution. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-3-calculations-of-electric-potential][OpenStax, University Physics II, §7.3: potential references, including ground in Figure 7.18]{ref}; \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_06.html#Ch6-S8][Feynman lectures II.6, §6–8: a grounded conducting plane]{ref}.

= Constrained electrostatic equilibrium
{parent=Electric potential}

A particle constrained to a surface can have a stable minimum of <electrostatic potential> restricted to that surface even when the unrestricted equilibrium is unstable. Away from source charges, <Laplace's equation> makes the full potential <Hessian matrix> trace zero, which rules out a positive-definite three-dimensional Hessian but does not rule out a positive-definite restriction to two dimensions. Equal positive charges at the corners of a square give a planar minimum at its centre and an unstable perpendicular direction.

= Electrostatic reciprocity identity
{parent=Electric potential}
{title2=$\int_V(\phi_1\rho_2-\phi_2\rho_1)\,dV=\varepsilon_0\int_{\partial V}(\phi_2\nabla\phi_1-\phi_1\nabla\phi_2)\cdot d\mathbf S$}

For two <electrostatic potentials> satisfying the <Poisson equation> $\Delta\phi_i=-\rho_i/\varepsilon_0$, <Green's second identity> yields the displayed reciprocity formula. The <product rule for divergence> applied to $\phi_1\nabla\phi_2-\phi_2\nabla\phi_1$ cancels the cross-gradient terms. The normal points outward from the integration region; for an exterior region this means inward toward the excluded object on its inner boundary. Boundary terms may be removed only under suitable decay or boundary conditions.

= Image charge
{parent=Electric potential}

An image charge is a fictitious point charge placed outside the physical solution region so that its potential, combined with the real sources, enforces the required conductor boundary values. The <uniqueness of the Dirichlet problem> then identifies the constructed potential with the physical one. The image need not describe the actual induced surface-charge distribution.

= Fall time of a charge toward a grounded plane
{parent=Image charge}
{title2=$t_{\rm hit}=\pi\sqrt{2\pi\varepsilon_0 m d^3}/|q|$}

The image of a charge $q$ at height $z$ is $-q$ at height $-z$. Their separation is $2z$, giving force $-q^2/(16\pi\varepsilon_0z^2)$. Starting at rest at height $d$, energy conservation gives $\dot z^2=q^2(1/z-1/d)/(8\pi\varepsilon_0m)$. Integrating the reciprocal speed to the plane yields the stated finite time in the electrostatic Newtonian model. The induced-conductor potential energy is $-q^2/(16\pi\varepsilon_0z)$; using the full image-pair energy would double the force incorrectly.

= Falling charge above a grounded conducting plane
{parent=Image charge}
{title2=$T=\pi\sqrt{2\pi\varepsilon_0md^3}/|q|$}

A real charge of height $z$ has a reflected <image charge> at $-z$. Its acceleration is $\ddot z=-\kappa/z^2$, with $\kappa=q^2/(16\pi\varepsilon_0m)$. Released from rest at height $d$, its ideal-model arrival time is
$$
T=\frac{\pi d^{3/2}}{2\sqrt{2\kappa}}.
$$
The image follows the instantaneous reflected position. The induced-charge energy carries one-half the full real-image pair interaction factor, consistent with this force.

= Image charge for a grounded conducting sphere
{parent=Image charge}
{title2=$q'=-aq/R,\quad\mathbf p'=a^2\mathbf p/R^2$}

For a charge $q$ at $\mathbf p$, $R=|\mathbf p|>a$, outside a grounded sphere of radius $a$, the displayed image gives zero potential on the sphere and decay at infinity. On $|\mathbf x|=a$, one has $|\mathbf x-\mathbf p'|=(a/R)|\mathbf x-\mathbf p|$, proving boundary cancellation. The induced force is inward, of magnitude $q^2aR/[4\pi\varepsilon_0(R^2-a^2)^2]$, from <Coulomb's law> applied to the image field. The self-field is excluded.

= Electric potential difference
{parent=Electric potential}
{title2=$V_{AB}=\phi(A)-\phi(B)$}

An <electric potential difference> compares <electric potentials> at two points. For a move from $a$ to $b$, define $\Delta\Phi=\Phi(b)-\Phi(a)$; the terminal convention $V_{AB}=\Phi(A)-\Phi(B)$ instead means the potential at $A$ relative to $B$, so the order must always be stated. In <electrostatics>, the <line integral> of the <electric field> gives
$$
\Delta\Phi=-\int_a^b\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l,\qquad
\Delta U=q\Delta\Phi,\qquad
W_{\mathrm{field}}=q\int_a^b\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l=q\bigl(\Phi(a)-\Phi(b)\bigr)=-\Delta U.
$$
Here $\Delta U$ is the change in interaction <potential energy> of the test <electric charge> $q$ with fixed sources, while $W_{\mathrm{field}}$ is <work> done by the <electric field>. For an external agent moving the charge slowly without a change in <kinetic energy>, its <work> is $\Delta U$. A positive <electric charge> gains <kinetic energy> when accelerated toward lower <electric potential>; a negative <electric charge> gains <kinetic energy> when accelerated toward higher <electric potential>. Both motions lower $q\Phi$. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-2-electric-potential-and-potential-difference][OpenStax, University Physics II, §7.2: potential difference, work, and energy]{ref}.

For example, fix $Q=+2\,\mathrm{nC}$ at the origin and compare distances $r_a=0.20\,\mathrm m$ and $r_b=0.10\,\mathrm m$. With the stipulated approximation $k=8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C^2}$ and zero at infinity,
$$
\Phi(a)=\frac{kQ}{r_a}=89.9\,\mathrm V,\qquad
\Phi(b)=\frac{kQ}{r_b}=179.8\,\mathrm V,\qquad
\Delta\Phi=89.9\,\mathrm V.
$$
Moving $q=-3\,\mathrm{nC}$ inward therefore gives
$$
U_a=-2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J,\qquad
U_b=-5.394\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J,\qquad
\Delta U=-2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J,\qquad
W_{\mathrm{field}}=+2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J.
$$
If only the <electric force> does <work>, <conservation of energy> makes the gain in <kinetic energy> $2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J$. Direct integration confirms the signs: $\int_{r_a}^{r_b}kQ\,dr/r^2=kQ(1/r_a-1/r_b)=-89.9\,\mathrm V$, which multiplied by the negative test <electric charge> gives positive <work>. The <electric potential difference> is unchanged by choosing a different <electrical reference potential>.

In an <electrical circuit>, an <electric potential difference> is the voltage across a component's terminals. In an <electrical network>, a vertex <voltage> is specified relative to a reference vertex; its differences across edges drive <current flow>. For a uniform <electric field>, the integral reduces to $\Delta\Phi=-\mathbf E\cdot(\mathbf r_b-\mathbf r_a)$. A field $\mathbf E=300\,\mathrm{V/m}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$ across a displacement $0.04\,\mathrm m\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$ therefore gives $\Delta\Phi=-12\,\mathrm V$, as in the interior approximation for a <parallel-plate capacitor>. The <volt>, measured in <joules> per <coulomb>, expresses <energy> transfer per unit <electric charge>, which makes it useful for comparing components without specifying the amount of charge transported.

= Point charge
{parent=Electric potential}
{wiki}

A point charge idealizes electric charge concentrated at one point. In vacuum, a charge $Q$ at the origin has potential $Q/(4\pi\epsilon_0r)$.

= Equipotential
{parent=Electric potential}
{wiki}

An equipotential is a level set of the electric potential. The electric field is normal to every smooth equipotential.

= Magnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{title2=$\mathbf B$}
{wiki}

= Magnetic flux density
{synonym}

A <magnetic field> is a <vector field> $\mathbf B(\mathbf r,t)$ whose local value determines the magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> on a body with <electric charge> $q$ and <velocity> $\mathbf v$. In <SI> units,
$$
\mathbf F_B=q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B,\qquad
|\mathbf F_B|=|q|vB\sin\alpha,
$$
where $v$ is the <speed>, $B=|\mathbf B|$, and $\alpha$ is the <angle> between the <velocity> and the <magnetic field>. The <cross product> makes a nonzero magnetic <force> perpendicular to both the <velocity> and the <magnetic field>. The <right-hand rule> gives its direction for positive <electric charge>; negative <electric charge> reverses it. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-2-magnetic-fields-and-lines][OpenStax, magnetic fields and forces]{ref}.

The <SI> unit of <magnetic field> is the <tesla (unit)>:
$$
1\,\mathrm T=\frac{1\,\mathrm N}{(1\,\mathrm C)(1\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}})}.
$$
For a stipulated example in <SI> units, take <electric charge> $q=2\,\mathrm C$, <velocity> $\mathbf v=(3,0,0)\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, and <magnetic field> $\mathbf B=(0,0,4)\,\mathrm T$. The <cross product> gives magnetic <force> $\mathbf F_B=(0,-24,0)\,\mathrm N$. With the same <speed> directed along $+z$, the magnetic <force> is zero: motion parallel to a <magnetic field> produces no magnetic <acceleration>.

<Magnetic field lines> visualize the direction of a <magnetic field> by being tangent to it at each point. They are not generally the path of a body with <electric charge>: in a uniform <magnetic field> along $+z$, the <magnetic field lines> are straight while transverse <velocity> produces <circular motion>, and adding parallel <velocity> produces <helical motion in a uniform magnetic field>. The worked <Lorentz force> example below illustrates this distinction.

A <magnetic field> need not be static. <Magnetostatics> is the restricted case with no dependence on <time>; an <electromagnetic wave> has changing <electric field> and <magnetic field> even in vacuum. A changing <magnetic field> is coupled to the <electric field> through <Faraday's law of induction>. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_20.html][Feynman lectures, II.20: electromagnetic waves in free space]{ref}. The useful separation is between local bending, described by the <Lorentz force>, and the amount of <magnetic field> crossing a <surface>, described by <magnetic flux>.

= Tesla
{disambiguate=unit}
{parent=Magnetic field}
{title2=$\mathrm T$}
{wiki}

The <tesla (unit)> is the <SI> unit of <magnetic field>. From the magnetic <Lorentz force>, $1\,\mathrm T=1\,\mathrm{N\,s\,C^{-1}\,m^{-1}}$. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-2-magnetic-fields-and-lines][OpenStax, magnetic-field units]{ref}.

= Solenoid
{parent=Magnetic field}
{title2=$B=\mu_0nI$}
{wiki}

A <solenoid> is a coil of <electrical conductor>, usually wire wound into a <helix> around a <circular cylinder>. The same <electric current> $I$ flows through its successive turns. Each turn contributes a <magnetic field>; the <superposition principle> makes their axial contributions reinforce inside the coil. Curl the fingers of your right hand in the direction of the <electric current> around the turns: the thumb gives the interior <magnetic field> direction by the <right-hand rule>. Throughout this branch, $N$ is the total number of turns, $\ell$ is the coil <length>, and $n=N/\ell$ is the <solenoid turn density>. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_13.html#Ch13-S5][Feynman, Leighton and Sands, The Feynman Lectures on Physics, II.13–5: the field of a long coil]{ref}.

The attraction of a long <solenoid> is a controllable, nearly uniform <magnetic field> over an interior working region: change the <electric current> to tune its strength, or reverse the <electric current> to reverse its direction. This makes it a useful setting for studying the <Lorentz force>, measuring <magnetic flux>, and constructing an <inductor>. Its geometry turns the many contributions from individual turns into the simple <long-solenoid approximation>. A real finite coil has end fields and leakage; its exterior <magnetic field> is not zero. The <on-axis magnetic field of a finite solenoid> below quantifies that difference.

Historically, the coil helped connect <magnetic fields> to <electric currents>. In his memoir on the mathematical theory of electrodynamic phenomena, Ampère modeled the interactions of magnets using the ends of solenoids in place of microscopic magnetic constituents. This was a route toward explaining magnetism by <electric currents>, rather than a modern microscopic account of <magnetization>. \a[https://www.e-rara.ch/zut/download/pdf/1038813][Ampère, Mémoire sur la théorie mathématique des phénomènes électro-dynamiques, concluding discussion on p. 289]{ref}. The historical setting is documented by the \a[https://ampere-archives.fr/histoire-electricite/parcours-historique/lois-courants/ampere-loi][Ampère archives, investigations of current interactions in 1820–1826]{ref}.

= Solenoid turn density
{parent=Solenoid}
{title2=$n=N/\ell$}

The <solenoid turn density> $n$ counts turns per unit axial length in a uniformly wound <solenoid>. The total turn count $N$ is dimensionless, whereas $n$ has units $\mathrm{m^{-1}}$. A $1000$-turn coil of length $0.50\,\mathrm m$ has $n=2000\,\mathrm{m^{-1}}$; an axial interval of length $s=0.010\,\mathrm m$ then contains $ns=20$ turns. The effective circumferential <surface current density> in the closely spaced circular-turn model is $K=nI$, with units $\mathrm{A/m}$. \a[https://web.mit.edu/8.02t/www/test/materials/StudyGuide/guide09_revised.pdf][MIT 8.02 course notes, §9.4, equations 9.4.3–9.4.4: turns per length and surface current]{ref}.

For a single-layer <helix>, the axial advance per turn is approximately $1/n$. The circular-turn model neglects this advance and the associated axial component of <electric current>, as well as the detailed <magnetic field> close to individual wires. The number of turns around the whole coil is always $N=n\ell$ here; it must not be substituted for $n$ in $B=\mu_0nI$.

= Long-solenoid approximation
{parent=Solenoid}
{title2=$\mathbf B_{\rm in}=\mu_0nI\,\widehat{\mathbf z},\quad\mathbf B_{\rm out}=0$}

In <magnetostatics>, an ideal infinitely long <solenoid> is a cylindrical sheet of circumferential <surface current density> $K=nI$. It has negligible winding advance, no magnetic core, and no imposed external <magnetic field>. Choose positive <electric current> to produce <magnetic field> along $+z$. In this ideal model the interior <magnetic field> is uniform and axial, $B=\mu_0nI$, and the exterior <magnetic field> is zero; $\mu_0$ is the <vacuum permeability>. For a finite, closely wound air-core coil, the same result is an approximation well inside the coil, several radii from either end. Air is treated as having the <magnetic permeability> of vacuum. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_13.html#Ch13-S5][Feynman lectures, II.13–5: long-solenoid field and the return field near the ends]{ref}.

For a short proof, the translational and rotational <symmetries> of the ideal cylindrical <current sheet>, together with the <Biot-Savart law>, give an axial <magnetic field>. <Ampère's law> on rectangles wholly outside makes that axial exterior value independent of radius; the condition of no imposed <magnetic field> makes it zero at infinity and hence everywhere outside. Rectangles wholly inside similarly show that the interior value is independent of radius. Now choose an <Amperian loop> with axial length $s$, one long side inside and the other outside. The transverse sides are perpendicular to the <magnetic field>, so their contributions to the <line integral> vanish. The loop crosses $ns$ turns of the winding, giving
$$
\oint_\Gamma\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf l
=Bs=\mu_0(ns)I,
\qquad B=\mu_0nI.
$$
Here $s$ is the integration-path length, not the whole coil <length> $\ell$. An <Amperian loop> surrounding both sides of the winding encloses equal and opposite <electric currents>; zero net circulation alone would not prove zero <magnetic field> at every exterior point.

\Image[solenoid-ampere-loop.png]
{title=<Ampère's law> for the ideal <long-solenoid approximation>}
{description=Axial cross-section of an infinite ideal winding: paired dots and crosses show <electric current> out of and into the page. The indicated <right-hand rule> produces interior <magnetic field> along $+z$. The counterclockwise <Amperian loop> crosses only the upper row, with $ns=6$ positive crossings; its interior side runs along $+z$, and its exterior return lies where the ideal <magnetic field> is zero.}

For the $1000$-turn, $0.50\,\mathrm m$ coil with <electric current> $I=0.50\,\mathrm A$, the <long-solenoid approximation> gives
$$
B\approx(1.256637\times10^{-6})(2000)(0.50)\,\mathrm T
=1.256637\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm T
\approx1.257\,\mathrm{mT}.
$$
This illustrative calculation uses <vacuum permeability> $\mu_0\approx1.256637\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{H/m}$, a rounded value, not an exact constant. Since the 2019 redefinition of <SI>, <vacuum permeability> is determined experimentally. \a[https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/41483022/SI-Brochure-9.pdf][BIPM, SI Brochure, 9th edition, §2.3.1: the ampere and vacuum permeability]{ref}. Doubling $I$ doubles the ideal <magnetic field>; doubling $N$ at fixed $\ell$ does the same. Increasing $\ell$ while keeping $N$ fixed decreases $n$ and hence the ideal <magnetic field>.

= On-axis magnetic field of a finite solenoid
{parent=Solenoid}
{title2=$B_z(z)$}

Model a finite <solenoid> as a continuum of coaxial turns shaped as <circles> of <radius> $a>0$, uniformly distributed over length $\ell$ with <solenoid turn density> $n=N/\ell$. Put its centre at $z=0$, so its ends are $z=\pm\ell/2$. With the same <electric current> orientation as in the <long-solenoid approximation>, its axial <magnetic field> is
$$
B_z(z)=\frac{\mu_0nI}{2}
\left[
\frac{z+\ell/2}{\sqrt{a^2+(z+\ell/2)^2}}
-\frac{z-\ell/2}{\sqrt{a^2+(z-\ell/2)^2}}
\right].
$$
This formula applies only on the axis, where <symmetry> cancels the transverse <magnetic field>. It does not describe points off the axis. It is exact for the continuum circular-turn model, which neglects winding thickness, discrete wire spacing, and helical advance. \a[https://web.mit.edu/8.02t/www/test/materials/StudyGuide/guide09_revised.pdf][MIT 8.02 course notes, §9.4, equations 9.4.5–9.4.6: the finite-solenoid axial field]{ref}.

The derivation is an especially direct use of the <superposition principle>. The <on-axis magnetic field of a circular current loop> at source position $z'$ is $\mu_0Ia^2/[2(a^2+(z-z')^2)^{3/2}]$. There are $n\,dz'$ turns in an axial slice, so
$$
B_z(z)=\frac{\mu_0nIa^2}{2}
\int_{-\ell/2}^{\ell/2}
\frac{dz'}{[a^2+(z-z')^2]^{3/2}}.
$$
Substitute $u=z-z'$ and use the <antiderivative> $u/[a^2\sqrt{a^2+u^2}]$ to obtain the displayed expression. If the centre is instead at $z=z_0$, replace every $z$ in that expression by $z-z_0$.

At the centre and at either end, respectively,
$$
\frac{B_z(0)}{\mu_0nI}=\frac{\ell}{\sqrt{\ell^2+4a^2}},
\qquad
\frac{B_z(\ell/2)}{\mu_0nI}
=\frac{\ell}{2\sqrt{\ell^2+a^2}}.
$$
For aspect ratios $\ell/a=2,6,20$, the centre ratios are approximately $0.7071,0.9487,0.9950$. The end ratio tends to $1/2$ as $\ell/a$ becomes large, even though the centre ratio tends to $1$. Thus a long coil can have an excellent central <magnetic field> while the ends still differ strongly from that value. Convergence to the <long-solenoid approximation> is not uniform across the end region.

\Image[solenoid-axial-field-profile.png]
{title=Finite <solenoid> axial <magnetic fields> compared with the ideal interior value}
{description=Original profiles for $\ell/a=2,6,20$ on the axis, normalized by $\mu_0nI$. The dashed step is the approximation that assigns the ideal interior value within the coil and zero outside; an infinite <solenoid> itself has no ends. The finite profiles stay smooth through the end planes and remain nonzero beyond them. These curves describe no off-axis points.}

For a concrete geometry, give the earlier coil <radius> $a=0.025\,\mathrm m$, so $\ell/a=20$. Its central <magnetic field> is approximately $1.2504\,\mathrm{mT}$, its end value is $0.6275\,\mathrm{mT}$, and at $z=\ell$ its axial <magnetic field> is still $0.00277\,\mathrm{mT}$. The last point is outside the coil: finite length does not permit the zero-exterior conclusion of the infinite model. Off the axis, the <magnetic field lines> bend around to form the return field. A central axial value alone also does not determine the total <magnetic flux> through every turn of a short coil.

= Solenoid inductance
{parent=Solenoid}
{title2=$L\approx\mu_0N^2A/\ell$}

= Self-inductance of a solenoid
{synonym}

For a long vacuum-core or air-core <solenoid> with fixed geometry and linear magnetic response, neglecting end effects gives <self-inductance>
$$
L\approx\frac{\mu_0N^2A}{\ell}
=\mu_0n^2A\ell,
\qquad A=\pi a^2.
$$
Here $N$ is the total number of turns, $n=N/\ell$ is the <solenoid turn density>, and $A$ is the interior cross-sectional <area>. The reason for the square of $N$ is physical: more turns produce more <magnetic field>, and more turns link the resulting <magnetic flux>. Approximately equal <magnetic flux> $\Phi\approx BA$ through each turn gives <magnetic flux linkage> $\Lambda\approx N\Phi\approx\mu_0N^2AI/\ell$; division by the <electric current> yields $L$. \a[https://s3.amazonaws.com/edx-textbooks/mit_802_textbook/Chapter11.pdf][MIT 8.02 course notes, Example 11.2: self-inductance of a solenoid]{ref}.

For the same $N=1000$, $\ell=0.50\,\mathrm m$, and $a=0.025\,\mathrm m$, the <area> is $A=\pi(0.025)^2\,\mathrm{m^2}\approx1.9635\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m^2}$. The long-coil estimate is
$$
L\approx\frac{(1.256637\times10^{-6})(1000)^2\pi(0.025)^2}{0.50}\,\mathrm H
\approx4.9348\,\mathrm{mH}.
$$
At $I=0.50\,\mathrm A$, each turn links approximately $2.4674\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{Wb}$ and the total <magnetic flux linkage> is approximately $2.4674\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{Wb}$. Their ratio to $I$ is the stated <self-inductance>. A real finite coil need not give precisely these values: the axial centre correction above is not by itself a correction formula for <inductance>, which depends on <magnetic flux> across all the turns.

At fixed $A$ and $\ell$, doubling $N$ quadruples $L$. At fixed <solenoid turn density> $n$ and <area> $A$, doubling $\ell$ doubles both $N$ and $L$. These are different changes to the geometry, so there is no contradiction between $L\propto1/\ell$ at fixed $N$ and $L\propto\ell$ at fixed $n$. A magnetic core requires its own <magnetic permeability> model; nonlinear <magnetization> can make a constant-$L$ description inadequate.

= Stored energy of a solenoid
{parent=Solenoid inductance}
{title2=$U=\tfrac12LI^2$}

The <magnetic energy> stored in a linear <solenoid> of constant <self-inductance> $L$ is
$$
U=\frac12LI^2,
\qquad
U\approx\frac{B^2}{2\mu_0}A\ell
$$
in the <long-solenoid approximation>. The first formula is an <inductor> relation; the second identifies its storage with <magnetic energy> in the interior <magnetic field>, under the vacuum-core approximation. This agreement joins the <electrical circuit> description to the spatial <magnetic field> description. The expression $B^2/(2\mu_0)$ is the vacuum magnetic <energy density>. \a[https://s3.amazonaws.com/edx-textbooks/mit_802_textbook/Chapter11.pdf][MIT 8.02 course notes, §11.3: magnetic energy and its density]{ref}.

A short derivation starts from the terminal <electric potential difference> $v_L=L\,dI/dt$, with positive <electric current> entering the terminal at higher potential. The absorbed <power> is $v_LI$, and integrating from zero <electric current> gives
$$
U=\int_0^I Li\,di=\frac12LI^2.
$$
The result describes recoverable storage in the <inductor>; <Joule heating> in the wire is a separate expenditure of <energy>. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_17.html#Ch17-S8][Feynman lectures, II.17–8: inductance and magnetic energy]{ref}.

For the long-coil example, $L\approx4.9348\,\mathrm{mH}$ and $I=0.50\,\mathrm A$ give $U\approx6.1685\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm J=0.61685\,\mathrm{mJ}$. Substituting the ideal $B\approx1.256637\,\mathrm{mT}$ and volume $A\ell\approx9.8175\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m^3}$ into $B^2A\ell/(2\mu_0)$ gives the same value. Doubling <electric current> quadruples stored <magnetic energy>. In a real coil, reducing <electric current> can return this <energy> to an <electrical circuit>, or release it as <Joule heating> in a closed <RL circuit>.

= Solenoidal magnetic-field constraint
{parent=Magnetic field}
{title2=$\nabla\cdot\mathbf B=0$}

The <magnetic field> has zero divergence. The <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation> preserves this constraint from initial data, since the divergence of its curl is zero.

= Magnetic field line
{parent=Magnetic field}

A magnetic field line is an integral curve tangent to the <magnetic field>. In coordinates $x^i$, it obeys $dx^i/B^i=dx^j/B^j$ wherever the relevant components are nonzero.

= Flux tube
{parent=Magnetic field line}
{wiki}

A flux tube is a bundle of <magnetic field lines> bounded by a surface everywhere tangent to the <magnetic field>. <Gauss's law for magnetism> makes the <magnetic flux> through any transverse cross-section constant along the tube. For a narrow tube with nearly uniform field magnitude $B$ and transverse area $A$, this gives $BA=\mathrm{constant}$.

= Flux-tube area
{parent=Flux tube}
{title2=$A$}

The cross-sectional area of a narrow <flux tube>, measured normal to its <magnetic field>. Constancy of the <magnetic flux> gives $A|\mathbf B|=\mathrm{constant}$ along the tube. This area controls the mass transport when flow follows the field.

= Magnetic-field-line equation
{parent=Magnetic field line}

The magnetic-field-line equation states that the tangent to a field line is parallel to $\mathbf B$. In spherical coordinates for an axisymmetric poloidal field, it can be written $dr/(r\,d\theta)=B_r/B_\theta$.

= Magnetic dipole field
{parent=Magnetic field line}

Outside a localized magnetic dipole $\boldsymbol\mu$, the vacuum field in Gaussian conventions is
$$
\mathbf B(\mathbf r)=\frac{3(\boldsymbol\mu\mathbin\cdot\mathbf r)\mathbf r}{r^5}-\frac{\boldsymbol\mu}{r^3}.
$$
For a dipole along the polar axis, $B_r=2\mu\cos\theta/r^3$ and $B_\theta=\mu\sin\theta/r^3$.

= Dipole magnetic-field line
{parent=Magnetic dipole field}

An axisymmetric dipole field line satisfies $r=C\sin^2\theta$, where $C$ is its equatorial crossing radius. This follows by integrating $dr/(r\,d\theta)=B_r/B_\theta=2\cot\theta$.

= Dipolar flux-tube area
{parent=Dipole magnetic-field line}

Near the polar axis of a <magnetic dipole field>, $B\propto r^{-3}$. Conservation of <magnetic flux> in a <flux tube> consequently gives $A(r)\propto r^3$. A <dipole magnetic-field line> intersecting a sphere of radius $R_\star$ at small polar angle $\theta_\star$ obeys
$$
\theta(r)^2\simeq\theta_\star^2\frac r{R_\star},
\qquad
A(r)\simeq\pi r^2\theta(r)^2
=\pi\theta_\star^2\frac{r^3}{R_\star}.
$$
This area is for one polar tube. The approximation requires $\theta_\star^2r/R_\star\ll1$; the full <magnetic dipole field> does not remain a narrow polar tube at arbitrarily large radius.

= Magnetostatic equilibrium
{parent=Magnetic field}
{wiki}

A magnetostatic equilibrium is a time-independent configuration in which magnetic, pressure, gravitational, and any other body-force densities balance.

= Spherical magnetostatic pressure balance
{parent=Magnetostatic equilibrium}
{title2=$\Delta_*\chi+\psi\psi'=-\mu_0r^2\sin^2\theta\,p'$}

For an axisymmetric <magnetic field> $\mathbf B=\psi\mathbf e_\phi/(r\sin\theta)+\nabla\times[\chi\mathbf e_\phi/(r\sin\theta)]$, the <poloidal magnetic flux function> gives $B_r=\chi_\theta/(r^2\sin\theta)$ and $B_\theta=-\chi_r/(r\sin\theta)$. Force balance implies locally $p=p(\chi)$ and $\psi=\psi(\chi)$ on regular connected flux surfaces. Direct calculation gives $(\nabla\times\mathbf B)\times\mathbf B=-(\Delta_*\chi+\psi\psi')\nabla\chi/(r^2\sin^2\theta)$, with $\Delta_*\chi=\chi_{rr}+\sin\theta\,\partial_\theta(\chi_\theta/\sin\theta)/r^2$. Equating this to $\mu_0\nabla p$ proves the displayed <magnetostatic equilibrium> equation, including its sign and dimensions.

= Confined linear-pressure spherical magnetic equilibrium
{parent=Spherical magnetostatic pressure balance}
{title2=$ (x^2-3)\sin x+3x\cos x=0$}

Set $\psi=\alpha\chi$, $p=p_0+P\chi$ and $\chi=F(r)\sin^2\theta$. A regular family is $F=A[\cos(\alpha r)-\sin(\alpha r)/(\alpha r)]+cr^2$, where $P=-\alpha^2c/\mu_0$. A zero <magnetic field> at radius a requires $F(a)=F'(a)=0$. For nonzero A this yields the displayed condition with $x=\alpha a$ and $c=-A[\cos x-\sin x/x]/a^2$. The boundary pressure is $p_0$, independent of angle. The trigonometric pair is tied by regularity and the radial differential equation; its coefficients are not independent.

= Magnetized isothermal atmosphere
{parent=Magnetostatic equilibrium}

A plane-parallel <magnetostatic equilibrium> with constant downward <gravitational acceleration> $g$, horizontal <magnetic field>, constant <isothermal sound speed> $c_s$, and constant <Alfvén speed> $v_a$ has
$$
H=\frac{c_s^2+v_a^2/2}{g},\qquad \rho=\rho_0e^{-z/H},\qquad p=c_s^2\rho,\qquad B=B_0e^{-z/(2H)}.
$$
Indeed the <magnetohydrodynamic total pressure> is $(c_s^2+v_a^2/2)\rho$, and its vertical derivative is $-g\rho$. The <magnetic pressure> therefore increases the <scale height> above that of an unmagnetized atmosphere.

= Cylindrical magnetostatic equilibrium
{parent=Magnetostatic equilibrium}

A cylindrical magnetostatic equilibrium is an <axisymmetric vector field> and <pressure> configuration independent of the axial coordinate. Its radial <Lorentz force density> balances the <pressure gradient>, as expressed by <cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance>.

= Z-pinch
{c}
{parent=Cylindrical magnetostatic equilibrium}
{wiki}

In a Z-pinch, an axial <electric current> produces a <toroidal magnetic field> whose inward <magnetic tension> confines a plasma. A cylindrical <magnetostatic equilibrium> balances the resulting <Lorentz force density> against a <pressure gradient>.

= Cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance
{parent=Cylindrical magnetostatic equilibrium}

For an <axisymmetric vector field> independent of the axial coordinate and regular on the axis, <Gauss's law for magnetism> gives $B_R=0$. The magnetostatic <Ampère-Maxwell equation> then gives $j_R=0$, $j_\phi=-cB_z'/(4\pi)$, and $j_z=c(RB_\phi)'/(4\pi R)$ in Gaussian units. Radial <magnetostatic equilibrium> is
$$
\frac d{dR}\left(p+\frac{B_z^2+B_\phi^2}{8\pi}\right)+\frac{B_\phi^2}{4\pi R}=0.
$$
For enclosed axial <electric current> $I(R)$, the magnetostatic <Ampère-Maxwell equation> gives $B_\phi=2I/(cR)$, hence
$$
\frac d{dR}\left(p+\frac{B_z^2}{8\pi}\right)
+\frac1{2\pi c^2R^2}\frac{dI^2}{dR}=0.
$$
The first term contains <pressure> and axial <magnetic pressure>; the second combines toroidal <magnetic pressure> with the hoop force from <magnetic tension>.

= Flux-tube axial field virial identity
{parent=Cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance}
{title2=$\langle B_z^2\rangle=B_p^2-2\mu_0\langle p\rangle$}

For a regular cylindrical <flux tube> with $\mathbf B=(0,B_\phi(s),B_z(s))$, vanishing field beyond radius $s_0$, and exterior <pressure> $p_e$, define $\langle h\rangle=2s_0^{-2}\int_0^{s_0}sh(s)\,ds$ and $B_p^2=2\mu_0p_e$. Integrating <cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance> against $s^2$ gives the displayed identity. The toroidal <magnetic pressure> and hoop <magnetic tension> cancel in this cross-sectional axial-field average. Nonnegative gas <pressure> gives $\langle B_z^2\rangle\leq B_p^2$, with strict inequality precisely when $\langle p\rangle>0$.

= Uniform-twist central axial field bound
{parent=Flux-tube axial field virial identity}
{title2=$B_z(0)^2\leq(1+\gamma^2)B_p^2$}

For a <flux tube> with $B_\phi=\gamma(s/s_0)B_z$, regularity at the axis and <cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance> give $B_z(0)^2=B_p^2-2\mu_0p(0)+\gamma^2\langle B_z^2\rangle$. The <flux-tube axial field virial identity> and $p\geq0$ give the bound. A twisted <toroidal magnetic field> can support a central axial field above the untwisted <magnetic pressure> limit even though its cross-sectional mean obeys that limit.

= Power-law pressure-supported axial current
{parent=Cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance}

For constant axial <magnetic field> and $p=p_0(1+R^2/a^2)^{-k}$ with $p_0>0$ and $k>0$, <cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance> gives, with $x=R^2/a^2$,
$$
I^2(R)=2\pi c^2p_0a^2
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle\frac{1-(1+kx)(1+x)^{-k}}{k-1},&k\ne1,\\
\log(1+x)+(1+x)^{-1}-1,&k=1.
\end{cases}
$$
The nontrivial <electric current> is finite at infinity exactly when $k>1$, in which case $I^2(\infty)=2\pi c^2p_0a^2/(k-1)$. Either sign of $I$ is possible because the <pressure> balance fixes its square.

= Magnetic pressure support with vanishing axial field
{parent=Cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance}

With no axial <electric current>, <cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance> reduces to constant $p+B_z^2/(8\pi)$. For $p=p_0e^{-R^2/a^2}$ and $B_z(0)=0$, its radial solutions are
$$
B_z=\sigma\sqrt{8\pi p_0}\sqrt{1-e^{-R^2/a^2}},
\qquad
j_\phi=-\sigma\frac{c\sqrt{8\pi p_0}}{4\pi a^2}
\frac{R e^{-R^2/a^2}}{\sqrt{1-e^{-R^2/a^2}}},
\qquad \sigma\in\{-1,1\}.
$$
As $R\downarrow0$, $B_z\sim\sigma\sqrt{8\pi p_0}R/a$ and $j_\phi\to-\sigma c\sqrt{8\pi p_0}/(4\pi a)$. These finite scalar limits do not give a <smooth function> of Cartesian position: $R=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ has a cusp at the axis, and the azimuthal unit vector has no unique limit there. Thus these data describe radial equilibrium away from the axis, but cannot satisfy smooth vector-field regularity on the axis without modifying the data.

= Motional electric field
{parent=Magnetic field}

A conductor or plasma moving with nonrelativistic velocity $\mathbf u$ through a magnetic field experiences the motional electric field $\mathbf E=-\mathbf u\times\mathbf B/c$ in Gaussian units when the electric field vanishes in its rest frame.

= London equations
{c}
{parent=Magnetic field}
{wiki}

= London equation
{c}
{synonym}

The London equations relate the superconducting <electric current> to the <electromagnetic field>: $\partial_t\mathbf J=\mathbf E/(\mu_0\lambda_L^2)$ and $\nabla\times\mathbf J=-\mathbf B/(\mu_0\lambda_L^2)$. In a gauge with constant condensate phase the second follows from $\mathbf J=-\mathbf A/(\mu_0\lambda_L^2)$; it implies exponential <magnetic field> screening.

= London penetration depth
{c}
{parent=London equations}
{title2=$\lambda_L=\sqrt{m/(\mu_0n_sq^2)}$}
{wiki}

For uniform vortex-free <superconductivity>, the <London equation> and stationary Maxwell equations give $\nabla^2\mathbf B=\mathbf B/\lambda_L^2$. A field tangential to a planar superconducting half-space decays as $\mathbf B(z)=\mathbf B(0)e^{-z/\lambda_L}$. A nonzero normal boundary field is incompatible with this translation-invariant decaying Meissner solution, because divergence-free magnetic flux would require that component to remain constant.

= Meissner effect
{c}
{parent=London equations}
{wiki}

The Meissner effect is the expulsion of magnetic field from the bulk of a superconductor. A static field decays from its surface over the London penetration depth.

= Magnetic flux
{parent=Magnetic field}
{title2=$\Phi_B$}
{wiki}

<Magnetic flux> measures the signed amount of <magnetic field> crossing an <oriented surface> $S$. Choose a <unit normal> $\widehat{\mathbf n}$ and use the <oriented surface element> $d\mathbf S=\widehat{\mathbf n}\,dA$. The defining <surface integral> is
$$
\Phi_B=\int_S\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S
=\int_S\mathbf B\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}\,dA.
$$
For a uniform <magnetic field> across a flat <surface> of <area> $A$, the <dot product> reduces this to
$$
\Phi_B=BA\cos\theta,
$$
where $\theta$ is the <angle> between the <magnetic field> and the chosen <unit normal>, rather than the <plane> of the <surface>. The <SI> unit is the <weber (unit)>, $1\,\mathrm{Wb}=1\,\mathrm{T\,m^2}$. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-1-faradays-law][OpenStax, magnetic flux and its orientation]{ref}.

For a stipulated <magnetic field> $B=2\,\mathrm T$ and <area> $A=3\,\mathrm{m^2}$, the <magnetic flux> is $6\,\mathrm{Wb}$ at $\theta=0$, $3\,\mathrm{Wb}$ at $\theta=\pi/3$, and zero at $\theta=\pi/2$. In the last case the <magnetic field> lies in the <plane> of the <surface>, so it does not cross it. Reversing the chosen <unit normal> reverses the sign of <magnetic flux> without changing the physical <magnetic field>; at $\theta=\pi$ the same example gives $-6\,\mathrm{Wb}$.

\Image[magnetic-flux-orientation.png]
{title=<Magnetic flux> as a flat <surface> rotates in a fixed <magnetic field>}
{description=Left: an edge-on <surface> and its <unit normal> at three <angles> to the fixed <magnetic field>. Right: the signed <magnetic flux> $\Phi_B=6\cos\theta\,\mathrm{Wb}$, including reversal of the <unit normal>. The stipulated <magnetic field> is $2\,\mathrm T$ and the <area> is $3\,\mathrm{m^2}$.}

The geometric idea is projected <area>: tilting a flat <surface> reduces the <area> seen perpendicular to the <magnetic field> by the factor $\cos\theta$. This explains how rotation alone can change <magnetic flux> even when the <magnetic field> is constant; <Faraday's law of induction> turns such changes into an <electromotive force>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-1-faradays-law][OpenStax, induction from changing magnetic flux]{ref}.

= Weber
{disambiguate=unit}
{parent=Magnetic flux}
{title2=$\mathrm{Wb}$}
{wiki}

The <weber (unit)> is the <SI> unit of <magnetic flux>, with $1\,\mathrm{Wb}=1\,\mathrm{T\,m^2}$. A uniform <magnetic field> of $1\,\mathrm T$ crossing a flat <surface> of <area> $1\,\mathrm{m^2}$ along its chosen <unit normal> gives $1\,\mathrm{Wb}$. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-1-faradays-law][OpenStax, magnetic-flux units]{ref}.

= Capacitance
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{title2=$C$}
{wiki}

The <capacitance> of a linear two-terminal <capacitor> is the constant relating its separated <electric charge> to its terminal <electric potential difference>:
$$
Q=CV,\qquad C=\frac QV\quad(V\ne0).
$$
Here $Q$ is the <electric charge> on one <electrical conductor>, the other carries $-Q$, and $V$ is their <electric potential difference> with the same ordering. Reversing the terminal ordering reverses both $Q$ and $V$ and leaves <capacitance> unchanged. An ideal passive <capacitor> has $C\ge0$; an ordinary pair of separated <electrical conductors> with positive <permittivity> has $C>0$. At $V=0$, use $Q=CV$ rather than the undefined ratio $0/0$.

For example, <electric charge> magnitude $Q=0.8854\,\mathrm{nC}$ at terminal <electric potential difference> $V=10\,\mathrm V$ gives <capacitance> $C=88.54\,\mathrm{pF}$. At $20\,\mathrm V$, the same linear <capacitor> has $Q=1.7708\,\mathrm{nC}$ and the same <capacitance>. The plots in <capacitor energy> show that <capacitance> is the <slope> of the <electric charge> versus terminal <electric potential difference> graph.

For fixed geometry and linear material response, <capacitance> depends on dimensions and <permittivity>, rather than the source's terminal <electric potential difference>. This is why <capacitance> is a useful property of a component: once it is known, $Q=CV$ predicts the separated <electric charge> for each operating terminal <electric potential difference>. The <SI> unit is the <farad>, $1\,\mathrm F=1\,\mathrm{C/V}$. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance][OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.1: capacitance and its unit]{ref}.

For combinations of <capacitors>, the same terminal definition gives an equivalent <capacitance>. Two identical <capacitors> of <capacitance> $C$ connected in parallel share the same terminal <electric potential difference>, and their <electric charges> add, giving $C_{\rm eq}=2C$. Connected in series with an initially neutral isolated junction, they carry equal plate <electric charge> magnitudes and their terminal <electric potential differences> add, giving $C_{\rm eq}=C/2$. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-2-capacitors-in-series-and-in-parallel][OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.2: capacitors in series and in parallel]{ref}.

= Farad
{parent=Capacitance}
{title2=$\mathrm F$}
{wiki}

The <farad>, symbol $\mathrm F$, is the <SI> unit of <capacitance>:
$$
1\,\mathrm F=1\,\frac{\mathrm C}{\mathrm V}.
$$
A <capacitor> of <capacitance> $1\,\mathrm F$ carries separated <electric charge> magnitude $1\,\mathrm C$ at terminal <electric potential difference> $1\,\mathrm V$. A picofarad is $10^{-12}\,\mathrm F$, so the worked <parallel-plate capacitor>'s <capacitance> $88.54\,\mathrm{pF}$ is $8.854\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm F$. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance][OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.1: the farad]{ref}.

= Capacitance of an isolated conducting sphere
{parent=Capacitance}
{title2=$C=4\pi\epsilon R$}

An isolated conducting sphere of radius $R$ in a medium of <permittivity> $\epsilon$ has surface <electrostatic potential> $V=Q/(4\pi\epsilon R)$ relative to infinity. Thus its <capacitance> is $C=Q/V=4\pi\epsilon R$.

= Sphere-plane capacitance
{parent=Capacitance}
{title2=$C(R,h)$}

For a conducting sphere of radius $R$ at center height $h>R$ above a grounded infinite plane,
$$
C(R,h)=4\pi\epsilon R\sinh\alpha\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac1{\sinh(n\alpha)},
\qquad\cosh\alpha=\frac hR.
$$
The distance $h$ is measured to the center, so the surface gap is $h-R$. Relative to the <capacitance of an isolated conducting sphere>, the enhancement is $c(R/h)=C(R,h)/(4\pi\epsilon R)$.

The <method of images> gives a constructive proof. At fixed sphere potential $V_s$, start with $q_0=4\pi\epsilon RV_s$ at height $z_0=h$ and place its opposite image below the plane. Cancel that image's nonconstant sphere potential by the successive charges
$$
q_{n+1}=\frac R{h+z_n}q_n,
\qquad z_{n+1}=h-\frac{R^2}{h+z_n},
$$
with opposite mirror charges below the plane at every step. The resulting sphere potential is $V_s$ and the plane potential is zero. Since $q_n=4\pi\epsilon RV_s\sinh\alpha/\sinh((n+1)\alpha)$, summing the sphere charges gives the displayed <capacitance>. In particular $c(y)=1+y/2+O(y^2)$ for small $y$.

The exact sphere-plane boundary-value problem is also treated in \a[https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.6034][Behunin and colleagues' analysis of sphere-plane electrostatics].

= Capacitance per unit length
{parent=Capacitance}

Capacitance per unit length is the charge per unit length divided by the potential difference. A vacuum coaxial cable with radii $a<b$ has
$$
C'=\frac{2\pi\varepsilon_0}{\log(b/a)}.
$$

= Inductance
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{title2=$L$}
{wiki}

<Inductance> relates <electric currents> to the <magnetic flux linkage> they produce. For a single linear coil with fixed geometry and magnetic response, its <self-inductance> $L$ is the constant in
$$
\Lambda=LI.
$$
Here $\Lambda$ is <magnetic flux linkage> from the coil's own <electric current> $I$. The <SI> unit of <inductance> is the <henry (unit)>. Knowing <inductance> lets us predict the induced <electromotive force> when <electric current> changes, and the <magnetic energy> stored by an <inductor>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-2-self-inductance-and-inductors][OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.2: self-inductance and inductors]{ref}.

For example, a coil of <self-inductance> $0.5\,\mathrm H$ carrying <electric current> $3\,\mathrm A$ has <magnetic flux linkage> $1.5\,\mathrm{Wb}$. If its $100$ turns link the same <magnetic flux>, each turn has <magnetic flux> $0.015\,\mathrm{Wb}$. Keeping the geometry and magnetic response fixed while doubling <electric current> doubles <magnetic flux linkage> and quadruples the <inductor>'s <magnetic energy>.

The comparison with <capacitance> is useful: a linear <capacitor> has $Q=CV$ and stores <capacitor energy> $CV^2/2$ in its <electric field>, whereas a linear <inductor> has $\Lambda=LI$ and stores <magnetic energy> $LI^2/2$ in its <magnetic field>. The <RLC circuit> brings both forms of <energy> storage together.

= Magnetic flux linkage
{parent=Inductance}
{title2=$\Lambda=\sum_{k=1}^N\Phi_k$}

= Flux linkage
{synonym}

<Magnetic flux linkage> adds the signed <magnetic fluxes> through the turns of a coil:
$$
\Lambda=\sum_{k=1}^N\Phi_k,\qquad
\Phi_k=\int_{S_k}\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S.
$$
Choose each <oriented surface> $S_k$ consistently with positive <electric current> around its turn using the <right-hand rule>. If all $N$ turns link approximately the same <magnetic flux> $\Phi$, then $\Lambda\approx N\Phi$; equality holds when every turn links exactly the same <magnetic flux>. Uniform <magnetic field> perpendicular to identical flat turns of <area> $A$ gives $\Phi=BA$. For a stipulated $100$-turn coil with <magnetic flux> $0.015\,\mathrm{Wb}$ per turn, <magnetic flux linkage> is $1.5\,\mathrm{Wb}$, not $0.015\,\mathrm{Wb}$. The turn count is dimensionless. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-2-self-inductance-and-inductors][OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.2: flux through the turns of a coil]{ref}.

= Henry
{disambiguate=unit}
{parent=Inductance}
{title2=$\mathrm H$}
{wiki}

The <henry (unit)> is the <SI> unit of <inductance>:
$$
1\,\mathrm H=1\,\frac{\mathrm{Wb}}{\mathrm A}
=1\,\frac{\mathrm{V\,s}}{\mathrm A}.
$$
A coil with <self-inductance> $1\,\mathrm H$ has <magnetic flux linkage> $1\,\mathrm{Wb}$ at <electric current> $1\,\mathrm A$. A rate of change of <electric current> $1\,\mathrm{A/s}$ produces induced <electromotive force> of magnitude $1\,\mathrm V$. The <weber (unit)> measures <magnetic flux> and <magnetic flux linkage>; the <henry (unit)> measures their ratio to <electric current>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-2-self-inductance-and-inductors][OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.2: the unit of self-inductance]{ref}.

= Self-inductance
{parent=Inductance}
{title2=$\Lambda=LI,\quad\mathcal E_{\rm ind}=-L\dot I$}

<Self-inductance> is <inductance> associated with a coil's own <electric current>, rather than <magnetic flux> supplied by another coil. In the linear model, fixed geometry and magnetic response give constant $L$ in $\Lambda=LI$. With the turn orientations defined in <magnetic flux linkage>, <Faraday's law of induction> gives the self-induced <electromotive force>
$$
\mathcal E_{\rm ind}=-\frac{d\Lambda}{dt}=-L\frac{dI}{dt}.
$$
The final equality requires constant $L$; if the geometry changes, use $-d\Lambda/dt$. The minus sign expresses <Lenz's law>: the induced <electromotive force> opposes the change in <electric current>. It does not always oppose the <electric current> itself. When positive <electric current> decreases, the induced <electromotive force> acts in its direction. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-2-self-inductance-and-inductors][OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.2: self-induced emf and its direction]{ref}.

For terminal sign bookkeeping, label the ideal <inductor>'s terminals $a,b$, choose positive <electric current> entering $a$ and leaving $b$, and define $v_L$ as the terminal <electric potential difference> drop from $a$ to $b$ in the lumped <electrical circuit> model. This passive convention gives
$$
v_L=+L\frac{dI}{dt}=-\mathcal E_{\rm ind}.
$$
Thus a coil with <self-inductance> $0.5\,\mathrm H$ and increasing <electric current> $dI/dt=12\,\mathrm{A/s}$ has terminal drop $v_L=6\,\mathrm V$ and induced <electromotive force> $\mathcal E_{\rm ind}=-6\,\mathrm V$. These signs describe the same process with different reference conventions. The <RL circuit> uses the terminal-drop convention throughout.

A finite terminal <electric potential difference> cannot make the <electric current> of an ideal <inductor> with constant $L>0$ jump. Integrating its <differential equation> gives
$$
I(t_2)-I(t_1)=\frac1L\int_{t_1}^{t_2}v_L(t)\,dt.
$$
If $v_L$ stays bounded, the <integral> tends to zero as the interval shrinks, ensuring <continuity> of <electric current>. A step in terminal <electric potential difference> changes $dI/dt$ immediately, while <electric current> remains continuous. At constant <electric current>, the self-induced <electromotive force> is zero, although the <inductor>'s <magnetic energy> can remain nonzero.

= Inductor
{parent=Self-inductance}
{wiki}

An <inductor> is an <electrical circuit> component designed to provide <self-inductance>, often a coil such as a <solenoid>. An ideal linear <inductor> has constant $L>0$ and zero <electrical resistance>. Its stored <magnetic energy>, with zero chosen at zero <electric current>, is
$$
U=\frac12LI^2.
$$
For <self-inductance> $0.5\,\mathrm H$ and <electric current> $3\,\mathrm A$, this gives $U=2.25\,\mathrm J$. Unlike <Joule heating>, ideal storage is recoverable: the <inductor> can return <energy> to an <electrical circuit> when its <electric current> decreases. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-3-energy-in-a-magnetic-field][OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.3: energy stored by an inductor]{ref}.

The short derivation uses the terminal convention in <self-inductance>. The <power> absorbed by an ideal <inductor> is
$$
P_L=v_L I=LI\frac{dI}{dt}=\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac12LI^2\right).
$$
Starting from zero <electric current>, the <integral> of this <power> gives
$$
U=\int_0^t P_L(t')\,dt'=\int_0^I Li\,di=\frac12LI^2.
$$
Positive $P_L$ increases stored <magnetic energy>; negative $P_L$ returns <energy>. At constant <electric current> $3\,\mathrm A$ in the example, $P_L=0$ and induced <electromotive force> is zero, but $U=2.25\,\mathrm J$ still resides in the <magnetic field>. The <battery> in the steady <RL circuit> supplies <Joule heating> in the <electrical resistance>, rather than further increasing the <inductor>'s <magnetic energy>.

\Image[inductor-energy-storage-and-release.png]
{title=Stored <magnetic energy> and its release as <Joule heating> in an <RL circuit>}
{description=Left: the <inductor>'s <magnetic energy> during buildup and a separate decay experiment starting at $3\,\mathrm A$. Right: the <power> of <Joule heating> during decay; the shaded <integral> over all time is $2.25\,\mathrm J$. The decay path stays closed through <electrical resistance> $2\,\Omega$ and <self-inductance> $0.5\,\mathrm H$.}

During the closed-loop release in <RL circuit>, stored <magnetic energy> plus accumulated <Joule heating> stays $2.25\,\mathrm J$. A real coil also has <electrical resistance>, so its <Joule heating> must be included separately from ideal <inductor> storage. The constant-$L$ formulas assume a fixed linear magnetic response.

= Bound charge and bound current
{parent=Electromagnetism}

Polarization and magnetization produce the effective sources
$$
\rho_{\rm b}=-\nabla\cdot P,
\qquad
J_{\rm b}=\partial_tP+\nabla\times M.
$$
Defining $D=\epsilon_0E+P$ and $H=B/\mu_0-M$ moves these sources into the macroscopic fields.

= Bound current
{parent=Bound charge and bound current}
{title2=$\mathbf J_b=\partial_t\mathbf P+\nabla\times\mathbf M$}

The current associated with <electric polarization> and <magnetization> in matter. If the magnetic response is neglected, it is $\mathbf J_b=\partial_t\mathbf P$. It is distinguished from a free conduction current in the <Maxwell equations in matter>.

= Polarization density
{title2=$\mathbf P$}
{parent=Bound charge and bound current}
{wiki}

= Electric polarization
{synonym}

Electric polarization is electric dipole moment per unit volume. It produces bound volume and surface charge
$$
\rho_{\rm b}=-\nabla\cdot\mathbf P,
\qquad
\sigma_{\rm b}=\mathbf P\cdot\mathbf n.
$$
Across an interface, the net bound sheet charge is $(\mathbf P_1-\mathbf P_2)\cdot\mathbf n$, where $\mathbf n$ points from medium one to medium two.

= Electric displacement field
{title2=$\mathbf D$}
{parent=Polarization density}
{wiki}

The electric displacement is $\mathbf D=\epsilon_0\mathbf E+\mathbf P$ and obeys $\nabla\cdot\mathbf D=\rho_{\rm free}$. In a linear isotropic dielectric, $\mathbf D=\epsilon\mathbf E$.

= Magnetization
{title2=$\mathbf M$}
{parent=Bound charge and bound current}
{wiki}

Magnetization is magnetic dipole moment per unit volume. It produces bound volume and surface currents
$$
\mathbf J_{\rm b}=\nabla\times\mathbf M,
\qquad
\mathbf K_{\rm b}=\mathbf M\times\mathbf n.
$$
Across an interface, the net bound sheet current is $(\mathbf M_1-\mathbf M_2)\times\mathbf n$.

= Paramagnetism
{parent=Magnetization}
{wiki}
{title2=$\chi>0$}

In a linear isotropic regime, a paramagnetic material has positive <magnetic susceptibility>: its induced <magnetization> is aligned with the applied <magnetic field intensity>. <Molecular oxygen> supplies an example discussed through <molecular orbital theory> in the <covalent bond> article.

= Curie temperature
{c}
{parent=Magnetization}
{title2=$T_C$}
{wiki}

The Curie temperature is the transition temperature above which a ferromagnetic material loses spontaneous <magnetization>. A hot conducting planetary core can generate a <magnetic field> through <dynamo action> without behaving as a permanent magnet. The blocking temperature at which a mineral records crustal remanence also depends on its relaxation timescale and need not equal its Curie temperature.

= Remanent magnetization
{parent=Magnetization}

= Remanence
{synonym}

Remanent magnetization is <magnetization> retained after the applied <magnetic field> has been removed. Magnetic minerals cooling in an ambient field can preserve information about that field in a planetary crust. Such a record may outlive the <planetary dynamo> which originally produced it. Crustal remanence therefore differs from a present field maintained by conducting-fluid motion.

= Magnetization current
{title2=$J_{\mathrm{mag}}^\mu$}
{parent=Magnetization}

A relativistic magnetization current has the form $J_{\mathrm{mag}}^\mu=\partial_\nu M^{\nu\mu}$ for an antisymmetric polarization-magnetization tensor. It is identically conserved because commuting derivatives contract an antisymmetric tensor.

= Magnetic field intensity
{title2=$\mathbf H$}
{parent=Magnetization}

The magnetic field intensity is $\mathbf H=\mathbf B/\mu_0-\mathbf M$ and obeys $\nabla\times\mathbf H=\mathbf J_{\rm free}$ in magnetostatics. In a linear isotropic medium, $\mathbf B=\mu\mathbf H$.

= Magnetic spherical-shell matching
{parent=Electromagnetism}

Across an interface with no free surface current, the normal component of $B$ and tangential component of $H$ are continuous. For an axisymmetric spherical shell, harmonic scalar potentials have dipole form $(ar+b/r^2)\cos\theta$ in each radial region.

= Maxwell stress tensor
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

With electromagnetic momentum density $\mathbf g=\epsilon_0\mathbf E\times\mathbf B$, the momentum-flux convention
$$
\sigma_{ij}=-\epsilon_0E_iE_j+\frac{\epsilon_0}{2}E^2\delta_{ij}
-\frac1{\mu_0}B_iB_j+\frac1{2\mu_0}B^2\delta_{ij}
$$
satisfies
$$
\partial_tg_j+\partial_i\sigma_{ij}=-(\rho\mathbf E+\mathbf J\times\mathbf B)_j.
$$

= Electromagnetic momentum density
{title2=$\mathbf g=\epsilon_0\mathbf E\times\mathbf B=\mathbf S/c^2$}
{parent=Maxwell stress tensor}

The electromagnetic momentum density in vacuum is $\mathbf g=\epsilon_0\mathbf E\times\mathbf B$. It equals the <Poynting vector> divided by $c^2$, expressing the relativistic relation between energy flux and momentum density.

= Local conservation of electromagnetic momentum
{parent=Maxwell stress tensor}

With the momentum-flux sign convention used for the <Maxwell stress tensor>, Maxwell's equations imply
$$
\nabla\mathbin{\cdot}\boldsymbol\sigma
+\partial_t\mathbf g=-\mathbf f_{\rm L},
$$
where $\mathbf g$ is the <electromagnetic momentum density> and $\mathbf f_{\rm L}$ is the <Lorentz force density> exerted by the field on matter. Thus total field-plus-matter momentum is locally conserved.

= Radiation pressure on a perfectly absorbing surface
{parent=Maxwell stress tensor}

A normally incident vacuum wave of time-averaged intensity $I$ transfers momentum $I/c$ per unit area and time to a perfectly absorbing surface, so its radiation pressure is
$$
p=\frac Ic.
$$
A perfect reflector reverses the normal momentum and gives $2I/c$.

= Proca action
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

The Proca action adds $m^2A_\mu A^\mu/2$ to the Maxwell Lagrangian. It describes a massive spin-one field and explicitly breaks the Abelian gauge symmetry of the massless theory.

= Proca equation
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}

A massive vector field coupled to a conserved current obeys
$$
\partial_\mu F^{\mu\nu}-m^2A^\nu=-\mu_0J^\nu.
$$
The mass term breaks electromagnetic gauge invariance.

= Proca field
{c}
{parent=Proca equation}

A Proca field is a massive spin-one vector field. Its field equation enforces transversality and leaves three physical polarizations.

= Proca propagator
{c}
{parent=Proca field}

For metric signature $(+---)$, the momentum-space Feynman propagator of a Proca field is
$$
\widetilde\Delta_{\mu\nu}(p)=\frac{-i}{p^2-m^2+i\epsilon}\left(\eta_{\mu\nu}-\frac{p_\mu p_\nu}{m^2}\right).
$$
The longitudinal numerator term inverts the massive vector kinetic operator without gauge fixing.

= High-energy obstruction for a hard vector mass
{parent=Proca propagator}
{title2=$D_{\mu\nu}(k)=-i(g_{\mu\nu}-k_\mu k_\nu/m^2)/(k^2-m^2+i0)$}

A free <Proca field> is consistent and has three physical <particle polarizations>. The difficulty for generic interactions is the ultraviolet behavior of its <Proca propagator>: the longitudinal numerator makes part of it approach order $1/m^2$, rather than decay as $1/k^2$. Longitudinal external <polarization vectors> also behave as $\varepsilon_L^\mu=k^\mu/m+O(m/E)$. Without suitable identities and cancellations, amplitudes grow with energy and perturbative counterterms of increasing dimension are needed. Conserved-current Abelian models and the <Stueckelberg mechanism> are important exceptions; a massive interacting vector is not intrinsically inconsistent as an <effective field theory>.

= Proca time-ordering contact term
{c}
{parent=Proca propagator}
{title2=$\Delta^{\rm cov}_{ab}-\Delta^{T}_{ab}=i\delta_a^0\delta_b^0\delta^{(4)}/m^2$}

For a free massive <Proca field> in the mostly-plus <Minkowski metric>, the covariant inverse of the quadratic <action> and the canonical <time-ordered product> of vector potentials differ locally. With <Fourier transform> $e^{iq\cdot(x-y)}$, the covariant <Proca propagator> is $-i(\eta_{ab}+q_aq_b/m^2)/(q^2+m^2-i0)$, while the literal canonical <two-point correlation function> subtracts $i\delta_a^0\delta_b^0/m^2$ in momentum space. The time component is nondynamical; its physical-mode numerator is $|\boldsymbol q|^2/m^2$, rather than $-1+(q^0)^2/m^2$. The difference is an instantaneous <Dirac delta function>, not another propagating state. Both expressions agree away from coincidence. This distinction matters when identifying a covariant <Green function> with a canonical correlator in a constrained field theory.

= Transverse vector field
{parent=Proca field}

A vector field is transverse when $\partial_\mu V^\mu=0$ in spacetime, or $\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf V=0$ for a spatial Fourier mode.

= Lorenz constraint in Proca theory
{parent=Proca equation}
{c}

Taking a divergence and using antisymmetry of $F^{\mu\nu}$ and current conservation gives $m^2\partial_\mu A^\mu=0$. For nonzero mass, the Lorenz condition follows from the field equation rather than from a gauge choice.

= Yukawa potential
{parent=Proca equation}
{c}
{wiki}

The static Green function of $\nabla^2-m^2$ in three dimensions is proportional to $e^{-mr}/r$. It describes an interaction screened beyond range $m^{-1}$ and tends to the Coulomb potential as $m\to0$.

= Relativistic Lorentz force
{parent=Electromagnetism}

$m\,du^\mu/d\tau=qF^\mu{}_\nu u^\nu$ gives $d\mathbf p/dt=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$ and $d(\gamma mc^2)/dt=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v$.

= Constant-field relativistic Lorentz-force solution
{parent=Relativistic Lorentz force}

For a constant <electromagnetic field tensor>, the <relativistic Lorentz force> is a constant-coefficient linear system on <four-velocity>, with solution
$$
u(\tau)=\exp\!\left(\frac qmF\tau\right)u(0).
$$
The electric-type blocks of $F$ generate hyperbolic rotations, while magnetic-type blocks generate ordinary rotations.

= Relativistic motion in parallel electric and magnetic fields
{parent=Constant-field relativistic Lorentz-force solution}

When uniform $\mathbf E$ and $\mathbf B$ are parallel, the <relativistic Lorentz force> splits into independent hyperbolic motion along the fields and circular motion in the perpendicular plane. The circle is periodic in <proper time>, while electric acceleration makes the helix's longitudinal pitch grow exponentially from one proper-time cyclotron period to the next.

= Relativistic hyperbolic motion in a uniform electric field
{parent=Relativistic Lorentz force}

A particle released from rest in a constant electric field parallel to its motion follows a spacetime hyperbola and has rapidity proportional to proper time.

This force law realizes <hyperbolic motion> with constant <proper acceleration>.

= Null crossed electromagnetic field
{parent=Relativistic Lorentz force}

Perpendicular fields with $\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf B=0$ and $E^2-c^2B^2=0$ form a null electromagnetic field. The constant-field Lorentz-force generator is nilpotent, so its exponential terminates as a polynomial.

Both invariants of the <electromagnetic field tensor> vanish for this field class.

= Relativistic trajectory in a constant null crossed field
{parent=Null crossed electromagnetic field}

Choose
$$
\mathbf B=B\,\mathbf e_z,\qquad
\mathbf E=cB\,\mathbf e_x,
\qquad
\Omega=\frac{qB}{m}.
$$
A particle released from rest at the origin has the proper-time trajectory
$$
t(\tau)=\tau+\frac{\Omega^2\tau^3}{6},
\qquad
x(\tau)=\frac{c\Omega\tau^2}{2},
\qquad
y(\tau)=-\frac{c\Omega^2\tau^3}{6},
\qquad z(\tau)=0.
$$
Eliminating <proper time> gives the semicubical parabola
$$
\boxed{y^2=\frac{2qB}{9mc}x^3.}
$$
This signed coefficient assumes that the positive $x$ direction is chosen along $\mathbf E$; reversing that axis changes the signs of both $x^3$ and its coefficient.

= Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A velocity cross magnetic-field force bends perpendicular motion into a circle while doing no work.

= Magnetic angular-momentum invariant
{parent=Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field}
{title2=$\mathbf L\cdot\mathbf B+\frac q2|\mathbf r\times\mathbf B|^2$}

For a particle obeying $m\dot{\mathbf v}=q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$ in a constant uniform <magnetic field>, the displayed combination is conserved. The derivative of mechanical <angular momentum> projected onto the field is canceled by the quadratic position term. This is the rotational analogue of the distinction between mechanical and <canonical momentum> in an electromagnetic field.

= Helical motion in a uniform magnetic field
{parent=Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field}

In a uniform <magnetic field>, the <Lorentz force> leaves the component of <velocity> parallel to the field constant and rotates the perpendicular component at the nonrelativistic <cyclotron frequency> $|q|B/m$. The resulting path is a <helix> around a field line.

= Guiding center
{parent=Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field}
{wiki}

= Guiding centre
{synonym}

The guiding center is the center of a charged particle's circular cyclotron orbit. In a nonuniform field it drifts slowly compared with the rapid <cyclotron frequency>.

= Damped cyclotron motion
{parent=Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field}
{wiki}

Adding linear drag to cyclotron motion produces an exponentially contracting spiral.

= Electrostatics
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

Electrostatics studies time-independent electric fields and charge distributions.

= Electrostatic interface boundary conditions
{parent=Electrostatics}
{title2=$[D_n]=\sigma_{\rm fixed}$}

Across a charged dielectric interface the tangential <electric field> is continuous and the normal displacement jump is the fixed <surface charge density>. Without a dipole sheet the <electrostatic potential> is continuous. For a charge sheet with equal <permittivity> on both sides, its normal potential derivative has jump $-\sigma_{\rm fixed}/\varepsilon$. Setting the field to zero on one side is an additional physical boundary assumption, as in a conductor or a negligible-exterior-admittance idealization.

= Debye–Hückel approximation
{c}
{parent=Electrostatics}
{title2=$(\nabla^2-\kappa_D^2)\phi=-\rho_{\rm fixed}/\varepsilon$}

The Debye–Hückel approximation linearizes mobile-ion Boltzmann densities about a neutral bulk electrolyte. It is valid for ionic potential energies small compared with $k_BT$. The inverse <Debye–Hückel screening length> satisfies $\kappa_D^2=\sum_i n_i z_i^2e^2/(\varepsilon k_BT)$. The quadratic screened charging free energy includes both <electric field> energy and the linearized ionic-response term $\varepsilon\kappa_D^2\phi^2/2$.

= Screened sinusoidal surface-charge mode
{parent=Debye–Hückel approximation}
{title2=$p=\sqrt{k^2+\kappa_D^2}$}

A lateral sinusoidal <surface charge density> with wave number $k$ in the <Debye–Hückel approximation> generates vertical exponential or hyperbolic functions with inverse length $p=\sqrt{k^2+\kappa_D^2}$. In a homogeneous electrolyte filling all space, an isolated sheet at $z_s$ produces $\phi=\sigma_k(x)e^{-p|z-z_s|}/(2\varepsilon p)$. Boundary conditions determine the normalization when the electrolyte occupies only a gap.

= Phase registration of screened charged sheets
{parent=Screened sinusoidal surface-charge mode}
{title2=$\theta_{\min}=\pi\pmod{2\pi}$}

Two equally modulated charge sheets in identical full-space electrolyte have charging energy per area $\alpha^2[1+e^{-pd}\cos\theta]/(4\varepsilon p)$, where $p=\sqrt{k^2+\kappa_D^2}$. Its phase-dependent interaction favors a half-wavelength offset, with opposite charge patches facing each other. For an electrolyte gap with negligible exterior displacement response, the energy is instead $\alpha^2[\coth(pd)+\cos\theta/\sinh(pd)]/(2\varepsilon p)$, with the same minimizing phase. Absolute energies require specified exterior <electrostatic interface boundary conditions>.

= Bjerrum length
{parent=Electrostatics}
{c}
{title2=$\ell_B$}
{wiki}

For a chosen reference charge $q$, the separation at which its Coulomb interaction energy equals $k_BT$. The charge-$q$ convention is $\ell_B=q^2/(4\pi\epsilon_{\rm abs}k_BT)$ in SI, or $\ell_B=\beta q^2/\epsilon$ in <Gaussian units>. The commonly tabulated value uses the elementary charge; changing <counterion> valence requires tracking that reference convention. It sets the strength of <counterion condensation>.

= Poisson-Boltzmann equation
{parent=Electrostatics}
{c}
{wiki=Poisson–Boltzmann_equation}

A mean-field coupling of the <Poisson equation> to an ideal <Boltzmann distribution> of mobile <ions>. In <Gaussian units> with uniform dielectric constant $\epsilon$, $\nabla^2\phi=-(4\pi/\epsilon)[\rho_f+\sum_i q_i n_{i,0}e^{-\beta q_i\phi}]$. The potential reference and chemical potentials determine the normalization factors. It neglects discrete-ion correlations and assumes local <thermal equilibrium>. A counterion-only model is not the same equation as a symmetric salt reservoir.

= Neutral cylindrical cell model
{parent=Poisson-Boltzmann equation}
{title2=$E(R)=0$}

Confine the <counterions> of a uniformly charged infinite cylinder within a coaxial cylinder of radius $R$, with total mobile line charge $\lambda$. The <electric field> vanishes at $R$, while its surface value at $a$ is fixed by the bare line charge. This gives $a\phi'(a)=2\lambda/\epsilon$ and $R\phi'(R)=0$ in <Gaussian units>. Sending $R/a\to\infty$ defines the <infinite-dilution limit of cylindrical counterions>.

= Infinite-dilution limit of cylindrical counterions
{parent=Neutral cylindrical cell model}

In a neutral counterion-only cylindrical cell, send the outer radius to infinity at fixed cylinder charge. Below the <Manning parameter> threshold, number density tends to zero at each fixed radius. Above threshold, the <cylindrical counterion-only Poisson-Boltzmann profile> retains line charge $\lambda-q/\ell_B$ locally, while the rest recedes to infinity. Therefore the limit of the full finite-cell charge integral is not the charge integral of the pointwise limiting density.

= Cylindrical counterion-only Poisson-Boltzmann profile
{parent=Poisson-Boltzmann equation}
{title2=$\Phi=2\log[1+(\eta-1)u]$}

For $u=\log(r/a)$ and $\Phi=\beta q\phi-2u$, a counterion-only <Poisson-Boltzmann equation> reduces to $\Phi''=-Ce^{-\Phi}$. Conditions $\Phi(0)=0$, $\Phi\to\infty$ and $\Phi'\to0$ give $\Phi=2\log(1+bu)$, $b=\sqrt{C/2}>0$. Matching surface <Gauss's law> gives $b=\eta-1$, so this branch requires the <Manning parameter> $\eta>1$. Its number density is $n(r)=(\eta-1)^2(a/r)^2/[2\pi\ell_Ba^2(1+(\eta-1)\log(r/a))^2]$. It leaves residual line-charge magnitude $q/\ell_B$.

= Counterion condensation
{parent=Poisson-Boltzmann equation}
{wiki}

= Manning condensation
{c}
{synonym}

Localization of a finite fraction of oppositely charged <ions> near a charged object even as the confining volume grows without bound. In the salt-free infinite-cylinder mean-field model, the <Manning parameter> must exceed one for a nonzero limiting local density. The condensed charge does not include <ions> that escape to infinity; finite-cell charge neutrality and the local infinite-volume limit must not be interchanged.

= Manning condensed fraction
{parent=Counterion condensation}
{c}
{title2=$f_c=\max(1-1/\eta,0)$}

In the mean-field infinite-dilution cylinder model, the fraction of neutralizing charge that remains localized is $f_c=\max(1-1/\eta,0)$, where $\eta$ is the <Manning parameter>. For $\eta>1$, integrating the <cylindrical counterion-only Poisson-Boltzmann profile> gives $Q_c=\lambda-q/\ell_B$. Below threshold, the local limiting density vanishes even though every finite neutral cell contains the full <counterion> charge.

= Cylindrical screening charge
{parent=Counterion condensation}
{title2=$Q(r)=2\pi\int_a^r s\rho(s)\,ds$}

The positive mobile charge per unit axial length accumulated from cylinder radius $a$ to $r$ is $Q(r)=2\pi\int_a^r s\rho(s)\,ds$. For fixed negative line charge $-\lambda$, <Gauss's law> gives signed radial <electric field> $E_r=2[-\lambda+Q(r)]/(\epsilon r)$ in <Gaussian units>. Its absolute value is the field magnitude. An integral over a local infinite-dilution profile measures condensed charge, rather than every <ion> in a finite neutral cell.

= Manning parameter
{parent=Counterion condensation}
{c}
{title2=$\eta=\lambda\ell_B/q$}

The dimensionless line-charge coupling $\eta=\lambda\ell_B/q$, with negative cylinder line charge $-\lambda$ and <counterions> of charge $q>0$. Here the <Bjerrum length> uses the same reference charge $q$. The mean-field infinite-dilution threshold is $\eta=1$. If $\ell_B$ is instead tabulated for elementary charge $e$ and <counterions> have charge $ze$, the corresponding coupling is $z\lambda\ell_B/e$.

= Coulomb's law
{parent=Electrostatics}
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathbf F_{2\leftarrow1}=kq_1q_2\mathbf r/|\mathbf r|^3$}

<Coulomb's law> gives the mutual <electric force> between stationary <point charges> in <vacuum>. Put <electric charges> $q_1$ and $q_2$ at $\mathbf r_1$ and $\mathbf r_2$, and define the <displacement> **from charge 1 to charge 2** by $\mathbf r=\mathbf r_2-\mathbf r_1$, with separation $r=|\mathbf r|>0$ and <unit vector> $\widehat{\mathbf r}=\mathbf r/r$. In <SI> units, the <force> on charge 2 due to charge 1 is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf F_{2\leftarrow1}=kq_1q_2\frac{\mathbf r}{|\mathbf r|^3}=\frac{kq_1q_2}{r^2}\widehat{\mathbf r},\qquad k=\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}.}
$$
Here $\varepsilon_0$ is the <vacuum permittivity>. The magnitude is $k|q_1q_2|/r^2$. If $q_1q_2>0$, the <force> on charge 2 points away from charge 1; if $q_1q_2<0$, it points toward charge 1. Interchanging the charges gives $\mathbf F_{1\leftarrow2}=-\mathbf F_{2\leftarrow1}$, consistent with <Newton's third law> in this electrostatic setting. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-3-coulombs-law][OpenStax, University Physics II, §5.3: Coulomb's law]{ref}.

For a fully computed example, stipulate $q_1=+2\,\mathrm{nC}$, $q_2=-3\,\mathrm{nC}$, $\mathbf r_1=\mathbf0$, $\mathbf r_2=0.20\,\mathrm m\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$ and the approximate value $k=8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C^2}$. Then
$$
\mathbf F_{2\leftarrow1}=\frac{(8.99\times10^9)(2\times10^{-9})(-3\times10^{-9})}{(0.20)^2}\,\mathrm N\,\widehat{\mathbf x}=-1.3485\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm N\,\widehat{\mathbf x}.
$$
The <force> is attractive, of magnitude $1.3485\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm N$. The source <electric field> at charge 2 is $\mathbf E_1(\mathbf r_2)=449.5\,\mathrm{N/C}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$, pointing away from the positive source. Multiplication by the negative $q_2$ reverses the direction to give the attractive <force>. This field excludes charge 2's own self-field.

Holding the <electric charges> fixed while changing the separation from $R$ to $2R$ or $3R$ gives the normalized <inverse-square law> values
$$
\frac{|\mathbf F(R)|}{|\mathbf F(R)|}=1,\qquad \frac{|\mathbf F(2R)|}{|\mathbf F(R)|}=\frac14,\qquad \frac{|\mathbf F(3R)|}{|\mathbf F(R)|}=\frac19.
$$
For the stipulated example, separations $0.20$, $0.40$ and $0.60\,\mathrm m$ give magnitudes $1.3485\times10^{-6}$, $3.37125\times10^{-7}$ and $1.498333\ldots\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm N$. The diagram computes these ratios and uses one common arrow-length scale for both sign choices.

\Image[coulomb-force-distance.png]
{title=Equal and opposite Coulomb force vectors: like charges repel, unlike charges attract, and doubling or tripling separation gives one quarter or one ninth of the force}

The <inverse-square law> has a geometric explanation through <Gauss's law>: a source <point charge> gives fixed total <electric flux> through a concentric <sphere>, whose <surface area> is $4\pi r^2$. Dividing that flux by the area gives a radial <electric field> proportional to $1/r^2$. This connects a pairwise <force> calculation to a global conservation-like accounting of field sources. For several stationary <point charges>, the <superposition principle> adds the individual <electric fields> as <vectors>, and $\mathbf F_i=q_i\mathbf E_{\mathrm{other}}(\mathbf r_i)$ uses all sources except charge $i$ itself. The <Coulomb potential energy> gives an alternative route when the desired quantity is <work> rather than instantaneous <force>.

= Coulomb potential energy
{parent=Coulomb's law}
{c}
{title2=$U(r)=kq_1q_2/r$}

= Coulomb potential
{synonym}
{c}

The <Coulomb potential energy> is the interaction <potential energy> of two <point charges> in <vacuum>. With separation $r>0$, <electric charges> $q_1,q_2$, $k=1/(4\pi\varepsilon_0)$ and zero <potential energy> at infinite separation,
$$
\boxed{U(r)=\frac{kq_1q_2}{r}.}
$$
This is mutual interaction <energy>; it does not include the divergent self-energies of ideal <point charges>. Unlike <electric potential>, which has units of <volts>, $U$ has <SI> units of joules. With charge 1 fixed and positive radial direction away from it, the <conservative force> in <Coulomb's law> gives
$$
U(r)-U(\infty)=-\int_\infty^r\frac{kq_1q_2}{s^2}\,ds=\frac{kq_1q_2}{r},\qquad
F_r=-\frac{dU}{dr}=\frac{kq_1q_2}{r^2}.
$$
Thus the $1/r$ <potential energy> and the <inverse-square law> for <force> are two descriptions of the same interaction. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-1-electric-potential-energy][OpenStax, University Physics II, §7.1: electric potential energy]{ref}.

For $q_1=+2\,\mathrm{nC}$, $q_2=-3\,\mathrm{nC}$ and $r=0.20\,\mathrm m$, using the stipulated approximation $k=8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C^2}$,
$$
U=\frac{(8.99\times10^9)(2\times10^{-9})(-3\times10^{-9})}{0.20}\,\mathrm J=-2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J.
$$
At $r=0.40\,\mathrm m$, $U=-1.3485\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J$. Separating the pair from $0.20$ to $0.40\,\mathrm m$ therefore requires external <work> $\Delta U=+1.3485\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J$ if the initial and final <kinetic energies> agree; the <electric force> does $-1.3485\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J$ of <work>. Separating them all the way to infinity requires $+2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J$. The negative initial <potential energy> means that the pair lies below the chosen separated reference; it does not mean a negative <kinetic energy>.

Like-signed <electric charges> have positive $U$, which decreases as they separate, so their <electric force> is repulsive. Opposite-signed <electric charges> have negative $U$, which decreases as they approach, so their <electric force> is attractive. This attractive interaction supplies the <Coulomb potential> used in the <hydrogen atom>; determining its stationary quantum states also requires <quantum mechanics>. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/III_19.html#Ch19-S1][Feynman lectures III.19, §19–1: the Schrödinger equation for hydrogen]{ref}. Dividing $U$ by a nonzero test <electric charge> $q_2$ gives the source's <electric potential> $\Phi_1=kq_1/r$. More generally, the <electrostatic potential energy of point charges> sums each unordered charge pair once. This <energy> description is useful because <work> between two separations follows from endpoint values, without integrating a complicated trajectory.

= Electrostatic equilibrium
{parent=Electrostatics}

A state with a stationary <electric charge> distribution and no net drift of mobile charge driven by the <electric field>. A <conductor in electrostatic equilibrium> has zero interior <electric field>, while bound charges in an insulating material need not move to eliminate its interior field.

= Conductor in electrostatic equilibrium
{parent=Electrostatics}

The electric field vanishes inside a conductor at electrostatic equilibrium, so each connected conductor is equipotential and the exterior field is normal to its surface.

= Electrostatic boundary conditions at a conductor
{parent=Conductor in electrostatic equilibrium}

= Electrostatic boundary condition
{synonym}

A Gaussian pillbox gives
$$
(E_{\rm out}-E_{\rm in})\cdot n=\frac\sigma{\epsilon_0}.
$$
Since $E_{\rm in}=0$, the surface charge density is $\sigma=\epsilon_0E_{\rm out}\cdot n$.

= Surface charge density
{title2=$\sigma$}
{parent=Electrostatic boundary conditions at a conductor}
{wiki}

Surface charge density is electric charge per unit area. At a conductor in electrostatic equilibrium it equals $\epsilon_0\mathbf E_{\rm out}\mathbin{\cdot}\mathbf n$ for the normal pointing out of the conductor.

= Charge sharing between distant connected spheres
{parent=Conductor in electrostatic equilibrium}

Two widely separated conducting spheres of radii $R_1,R_2$ connected by a thin wire have equal potentials, so a total charge $Q$ divides as
$$
Q_i=\frac{R_i}{R_1+R_2}Q.
$$

= Charge on connected concentric spherical shells
{parent=Conductor in electrostatic equilibrium}

Two connected concentric spherical shells form one equipotential conductor. In the absence of charge in the inner cavity, all net charge lies on the exterior surface of the outer shell.

= Induced charge on a conducting sphere in a uniform electric field
{parent=Conductor in electrostatic equilibrium}

A neutral conducting sphere of radius $R$ in the field $E\widehat z$ has exterior potential and induced surface charge
$$
\Phi=-E\left(r-\frac{R^3}{r^2}\right)\cos\theta,
\qquad
\sigma=3\epsilon_0E\cos\theta.
$$
The surface integral of $\sigma$ is zero.

= Electrostatic energy
{parent=Electrostatics}
{wiki}

Electrostatic energy can be written as one half the charge-potential integral or epsilon-zero over two times the electric-field energy integral.

= Electrostatic energy of a three-plate capacitor
{parent=Electrostatic energy}
{title2=$W=\frac{\epsilon_0AV^2H}{H^2-a^2}$}

In the parallel-plate approximation, a central plate at potential $V$ between grounded plates at $z=\pm H$ forms two parallel capacitors of gaps $H+a$ and $H-a$. Summing their field energies gives the displayed formula. At fixed voltage it is minimized at $a=0$. Finite circular plates have fringe fields; the uniform-gap expression assumes those edge corrections are neglected.

= Electrostatic energy of a uniformly charged solid sphere
{parent=Electrostatic energy}
{title2=$U=\frac{3Q^2}{20\pi\epsilon_0R}$}

A solid sphere of radius $R$ and uniform <charge density> has total <electric charge> $Q$. <Gauss law> gives the radial <electric field> $E_r=Qr/(4\pi\epsilon_0R^3)$ inside and $E_r=Q/(4\pi\epsilon_0r^2)$ outside. The <electric potential> vanishing at infinity is $Q(3-r^2/R^2)/(8\pi\epsilon_0R)$ inside and $Q/(4\pi\epsilon_0r)$ outside. Integrating the <electrostatic energy> density $\epsilon_0E^2/2$ over both regions gives $U=3Q^2/(20\pi\epsilon_0R)$. In a fixed-density family with $Q\propto Z$ and $R^3\propto Z$, the <electrostatic energy> scales as $Z^{5/3}$.

= Electrostatic energy of alternating-charge concentric shells
{parent=Electrostatic energy}
{title2=$W=\frac{q^2}{8\pi\epsilon_0r_1}$}

A first spherical shell with charge $q$ followed by shells of charges $2(-1)^{n+1}q$ leaves enclosed charge $(-1)^{n+1}q$ after each shell. <Gauss's law> therefore gives the same squared <electric field> $q^2/(16\pi^2\epsilon_0^2r^4)$ in every gap outside the first radius. Integrating the <electrostatic energy> density over all space gives $W=q^2/(8\pi\epsilon_0r_1)$, independent of the other shell radii.

= Electrostatic energy of a uniformly charged spherical shell
{parent=Electrostatic energy}

A thin spherical shell of radius $a$ carrying total charge $Q$ has surface potential
$$
\Phi(a)=\frac{Q}{4\pi\epsilon_0a}
$$
and electrostatic self-energy
$$
U=\frac12Q\Phi(a)=\frac{Q^2}{8\pi\epsilon_0a}.
$$

= Electrostatic potential energy of point charges
{parent=Electrostatic energy}

For distinct <point charges> $q_i$ at positions $r_i$, the electrostatic <potential energy> is
$$
U=\frac1{4\pi\epsilon_0}\sum_{i<j}\frac{q_iq_j}{|r_i-r_j|}.
$$
When some charges are fixed, the terms depending on a movable charge form its potential energy in their <electric potential>.

= Coaxial cylindrical capacitor
{parent=Electrostatic energy}

For coaxial cylinders of radii $a<b$ and length $L\gg b$, carrying charges per unit length $\pm\lambda$,
$$
E(r)=\frac{\lambda}{2\pi\epsilon_0r}\widehat r,
\qquad
V=\frac{\lambda}{2\pi\epsilon_0}\log\frac ba,
\qquad
C=\frac{2\pi\epsilon_0L}{\log(b/a)}.
$$
The field energy is $U=\lambda^2L\log(b/a)/(4\pi\epsilon_0)=QV/2$.

= Coaxial capacitor with grounded inner and outer cylinders
{parent=Coaxial cylindrical capacitor}
{title2=$C'=2\pi\epsilon_0(1/\log\lambda+1/\log(2/\lambda))$}

A driven cylindrical conductor between two grounded coaxial conductors forms two <coaxial cylindrical capacitors> in parallel. For radii $a,\lambda a,2a$, the <capacitance per unit length> is the sum $C'=2\pi\epsilon_0[1/\log\lambda+1/\log(2/\lambda)]$. Writing $u=\log\lambda$ and $L=\log2$, the function $1/u+1/(L-u)$ is strictly <convex> on $(0,L)$ and symmetric about $L/2$. Hence $C'$ has its unique minimum at $\lambda=\sqrt2$, with value $8\pi\epsilon_0/\log2$. As $\lambda=1+\delta$ approaches the inner conductor, $C'\sim2\pi\epsilon_0/\delta$, agreeing with the local <parallel-plate capacitor> approximation.

= Electric dipole moment
{parent=Electrostatics}
{wiki}

The electric dipole moment is the integral of position weighted by charge density.

= Time derivative of the electric dipole moment
{parent=Electric dipole moment}

For a localized charge and current distribution satisfying <charge conservation from Maxwell equations>,
$$
\dot{\mathbf p}=\int\mathbf J(x,t)\,d^3x.
$$

= Electric dipole
{parent=Electric dipole moment}
{wiki}

An electric dipole is a pair of equal and opposite charges in a small separation limit with their charge-times-separation vector held fixed.

= Electric dipole-dipole interaction
{parent=Electric dipole}

Two point dipoles separated by $\mathbf r$ have interaction proportional to
$$
\frac{\mathbf p_1\cdot\mathbf p_2}{r^3}
-\frac{3(\mathbf p_1\cdot\mathbf r)(\mathbf p_2\cdot\mathbf r)}{r^5}.
$$

The formula combines the source <electric dipole> field with the second dipole's coupling to that field.

= Electric multipole expansion
{parent=Electrostatics}

= Multipole expansion
{synonym}

= Electrostatic multipole expansion
{synonym}

For a localized charge distribution, the far <electric potential> is an inverse-radius expansion whose first terms are the total charge monopole, the electric dipole moment, and the electric quadrupole moment.

= Electric quadrupole moment
{parent=Electric multipole expansion}
{title2=$Q_{ij}=\sum_a q_a(3r_{a,i}r_{a,j}-|r_a|^2\delta_{ij})$}

The trace-free <electric quadrupole moment> is a symmetric tensor measuring the second-order term in an <electric multipole expansion>. For a localized charge distribution its contribution to the far-field <electrostatic potential> is $Q_{ij}\widehat r_i\widehat r_j/(8\pi\epsilon_0r^3)$, with summation over indices. The moment may be nonzero even when the total <electric charge> or <electric dipole moment> is nonzero; it should not be confused with an <electric quadrupole> whose lower moments vanish. Changing the origin generally changes the tensor when lower moments are nonzero.

= Electric quadrupole
{parent=Electric multipole expansion}
{wiki}

An electric quadrupole is a charge distribution whose total charge and <electric dipole moment> vanish while its second charge moment does not. Its potential decays as $r^{-3}$.

= Multipole expansion of three collinear charges
{parent=Electric multipole expansion}

For charges $-Q,NQ,-MQ$ at $z=d,0,-d$, respectively,
$$
\Phi(r,\theta)=\frac{Q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\left[
\frac{N-M-1}{r}+\frac{(M-1)d\cos\theta}{r^2}
-\frac{(M+1)d^2P_2(\cos\theta)}{r^3}+O(r^{-4})\right].
$$
The monopole vanishes when $N=M+1$, the dipole vanishes when $M=1$, and both vanish when $(N,M)=(2,1)$.

= Dipole and quadrupole scaling limits of three collinear charges
{parent=Multipole expansion of three collinear charges}

Subject to $N=M+1$, keeping $Qd$ fixed as $d\to0$ gives a finite point-dipole limit when $M\ne1$. Keeping $Qd^2$ fixed gives a finite pure-quadrupole limit only when $M=1$; otherwise the dipole diverges.

= Transverse force from a collinear electric quadrupole
{parent=Multipole expansion of three collinear charges}

For charges $-Q,2Q,-Q$ at $z=d,0,-d$, the exact force on a charge $-Q$ at $(x,0,0)$ is
$$
F=-\frac{Q^2x}{2\pi\epsilon_0}
\left(\frac1{|x|^3}-\frac1{(x^2+d^2)^{3/2}}\right)\widehat x.
$$
It points toward the origin for every nonzero $x$.

= Magnetostatics
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

Magnetostatics studies time-independent magnetic fields and steady currents. In vacuum its field equations are $\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B=0$ and $\nabla\mathbin\times\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf J$.

= Magnetic scalar potential
{parent=Magnetostatics}
{title2=$\mathbf B=-\nabla\psi$}
{wiki}

In a simply connected current-free region of constant <magnetic permeability>, <Ampère's circuital law> makes the <magnetic field> curl-free, so it admits a scalar potential. The absence of magnetic monopoles then gives <Laplace's equation> for that potential. Some conventions define the potential for $\mathbf H$ instead of $\mathbf B$, differing by the constant permeability. Boundary conditions, including imposed fields at infinity, determine the potential up to an additive constant.

= Gravity-equivalent magnetic surface density
{parent=Magnetic scalar potential}
{title2=$\Sigma_M=-B_{z0}/\sqrt{\pi G\mu_0}$}

For an isolated razor-thin disk, rescale its exterior <magnetic scalar potential> to satisfy $\sqrt{4\pi G/\mu_0}\mathbf B=-\nabla\Phi_M$. Comparing the normal magnetic boundary derivative with $\partial_z\Phi=2\pi G\Sigma$ identifies the displayed equivalent signed <surface density>. Both exterior potentials satisfy <Laplace's equation>. The equivalent <surface density> is a representation of the magnetic boundary data, rather than physical negative mass.

= Maxwell equations
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

= Maxwell's equations
{c}
{synonym}

The <Maxwell equations> are the field equations of classical <electromagnetism>. In <SI> units, in vacuum with prescribed <charge density> $\rho$ and <current density> $\mathbf J$, they are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\nabla\cdot\mathbf E&=\rho/\epsilon_0,
&\nabla\cdot\mathbf B&=0,\\
\nabla\times\mathbf E&=-\frac{\partial\mathbf B}{\partial t},
&\nabla\times\mathbf B&=\mu_0\mathbf J+\mu_0\epsilon_0\frac{\partial\mathbf E}{\partial t}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Here $\mathbf E$ is the <electric field> in volts per metre, $\mathbf B$ the <magnetic field> in teslas, $\rho$ in coulombs per cubic metre, and $\mathbf J$ in amperes per square metre. The constants are <vacuum permittivity> $\epsilon_0$ and the vacuum value $\mu_0$ of <magnetic permeability>. “Vacuum” specifies the field laws without a material response; “source-free” additionally means $\rho=0$ and $\mathbf J=0$. Matter described by averaged fields instead requires <Maxwell equations in matter> and <constitutive relations>.

The first equation, <Gauss's law>, makes <electric charge> a source of <electric flux>. <Gauss's law for magnetism> sets the net <magnetic flux> through a closed surface to zero. The two <curl> equations, <Faraday's law> and the <Ampère-Maxwell equation>, couple changes of the <electric field> and <magnetic field>. Appropriate <initial conditions> and <boundary conditions> select a physical solution; the two <divergence> equations constrain the initial fields. \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-02-physics-ii-electricity-and-magnetism-spring-2007/2d2f227e6fd26ee25f366759e02f03dc_chapte13em_waves.pdf][MIT 8.02 notes, §13.3: Maxwell's equations]{ref}.

= Electromagnetic duality
{parent=Maxwell equations}
{wiki}

Vacuum <Maxwell equations> without sources are invariant under $E'=E\cos\alpha+cB\sin\alpha$, $cB'=cB\cos\alpha-E\sin\alpha$. At $\alpha=\pi/2$ this is $E'=cB$, $B'=-E/c$. Electric-conductor boundary conditions are not invariant under arbitrary duality rotations; the transformed fields must be tested separately against the wall conditions.

= Derivative-order ambiguity in covariant Maxwell potential equations
{parent=Maxwell equations}
{title2=$\nabla_b\nabla^a A^b-\nabla^a\nabla_bA^b=-C^a{}_bA^b$}

Use the negative curvature convention defined in <curvature-sign convention in Killing derivative identities>, and $C_{ab}=C^c{}_{acb}$. Covariant derivatives of a vector obey $[\nabla_b,\nabla^a]A^b=-C^a{}_bA^b$. Hence the flat-space equivalent expressions $\Box A^a-\partial_b\partial^aA^b$ and $\Box A^a-\partial^a\partial_bA^b$ acquire a difference $+C^a{}_bA^b$ under the <comma-to-semicolon prescription>. Covariantizing $F_{ab}=2\nabla_{[a}A_{b]}$ and its <Maxwell equations> gives $\Box A^a-\nabla^a(\nabla_bA^b)+C^a{}_bA^b=\mu_0J^a$. This form respects <gauge invariance>. Dropping the Ricci term is generally not gauge invariant. The two expressions coincide in a Ricci-flat <vacuum spacetime>; vanishing matter alone with nonzero <cosmological constant> does not imply Ricci flatness.

= Magnetic Galilean limit of the pre-Maxwell equations
{parent=Maxwell equations}

The magnetic nonrelativistic limit neglects displacement current and uses $\partial_t\mathbf B=-\nabla\times\mathbf E$, $\nabla\times\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf J$, $\nabla\cdot\mathbf B=0$. Under a <Galilean transformation> $\mathbf x'=\mathbf x-\mathbf vt$, $t'=t$, its consistent field transformations are $\mathbf B'=\mathbf B$, $\mathbf J'=\mathbf J$ and $\mathbf E'=\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$. Indeed $\partial_{t'}=\partial_t+\mathbf v\cdot\nabla$ and $\nabla\times(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)=-(\mathbf v\cdot\nabla)\mathbf B$, giving covariance of the induction equation. The full charge-current transformation has $\mathbf J'=\mathbf J-\rho_e\mathbf v$; neglecting its convective correction belongs to the magnetic quasineutral approximation, not exact relativistic electrodynamics. With $\mathbf u'=\mathbf u-\mathbf v$, the combination $\mathbf E+\mathbf u\times\mathbf B$ is invariant. Vanishing electric field in the fluid rest frame therefore gives the <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation>.

= Gauss's law for magnetism
{c}
{parent=Maxwell equations}
{wiki}

Gauss's law for magnetism is $\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B=0$. Its integral form says that the net magnetic flux through every closed surface vanishes, so the normal magnetic field is continuous across an ordinary interface.

= Vacuum Maxwell equations
{c}
{parent=Maxwell equations}

For the electromagnetic two-form $F$, the source-free Maxwell equations are $dF=0$ and $d(*F)=0$. The first is the Bianchi identity and the second is the dynamical equation.

= Anti-self-dual Maxwell equations
{parent=Vacuum Maxwell equations}
{title2=$F_{AA'BB'}=\epsilon_{A'B'}\varphi_{AB},\quad \varphi_{AB}=\varphi_{BA}$}

= ASD Maxwell equations
{synonym}
{c}

In the <two-component spinor> convention with primed symmetric <gauge curvature> self-dual, an <Abelian> <Maxwell field> is anti-self-dual when its primed symmetric <gauge curvature> <two-component spinor> vanishes. For a locally exact field $F=dA$, the <Bianchi identity> gives $dF=0$; the duality relation then also gives $d{*}F=0$. Thus it obeys the source-free <Maxwell equations>. In complexified Lorentzian signature the <Hodge star> <eigenvalues> are $\pm i$; the <two-component spinor> condition specifies which component is meant without imposing a Euclidean eigenvalue on that signature.

// Destination: general-relativity.bigb

= Maxwell equations in matter
{c}
{parent=Maxwell equations}

In macroscopic matter with free charge density $\rho$ and free current density $J$,
$$
\nabla\cdot D=\rho,
\quad
\nabla\cdot B=0,
\quad
\nabla\times E=-\partial_tB,
\quad
\nabla\times H=J+\partial_tD.
$$
The electric displacement $D$ and magnetic field strength $H$ encode the material response.

These are the <Maxwell equations> after separating free and bound material sources.

= Constitutive relations
{parent=Maxwell equations in matter}

Constitutive relations specify how matter responds to fields. A homogeneous isotropic linear electromagnetic medium obeys $\mathbf D=\varepsilon\mathbf E$ and $\mathbf B=\mu\mathbf H$.

= Permeability
{title2=$\mu$}
{disambiguate=electromagnetism}
{parent=Constitutive relations}
{wiki}

= Magnetic permeability
{synonym}

Electromagnetic permeability relates magnetic flux density to magnetic field strength through $\mathbf B=\mu\mathbf H$ in an isotropic linear medium.

= Vacuum permeability
{parent=Permeability (electromagnetism)}
{title2=$\mu_0$}

Vacuum permeability is the <magnetic permeability> appearing in the vacuum <Maxwell equations>. In <SI>, it relates vacuum magnetic field strength to <magnetic flux density> through $\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf H$ and obeys $\mu_0\varepsilon_0c^2=1$, where $c$ is the <speed of light> and $\varepsilon_0$ the <vacuum permittivity>.

= Linear anisotropic dielectric
{parent=Maxwell equations in matter}

A linear anisotropic medium has constitutive relations $D_i=\varepsilon_{ij}E_j$ and $B_i=\mu_{ij}H_j$. Symmetric, time-independent tensors make the field-energy density a quadratic form.

Here <permittivity> is a tensor, so the electric displacement need not be parallel to the electric field.

= Birefringence
{parent=Linear anisotropic dielectric}
{title2=$\Delta n=n_e-n_o$}
{wiki}

<Birefringence> is the existence of different optical propagation constants for different eigenpolarizations in an anisotropic material. It provides the propagation-mode separation needed for <adiabatic optical following in a twisted nematic>.

= Ampère's circuital law
{parent=Maxwell equations}
{c}
{wiki}

= Ampère-Maxwell equation
{c}
{synonym}

= Ampere-Maxwell law
{c}
{synonym}

= Ampère's law
{c}
{synonym}

The Ampère-Maxwell equation
$$
\nabla\times B=\mu_0J+\mu_0\epsilon_0\frac{\partial E}{\partial t}
$$
relates magnetic circulation to conduction and displacement current.

= Amperian loop
{parent=Ampère's circuital law}
{c}
{wiki}

An Amperian loop is a closed curve used in the integral form of Ampère's circuital law or the Ampère-Maxwell equation.

= Magnetostatic Ampère law
{parent=Ampère's circuital law}
{title2=$\oint_C\mathbf B\cdot d\boldsymbol\ell=\mu_0I_{\mathrm{enc}}$}

In vacuum <magnetostatics>, the <Ampère-Maxwell equation> reduces to $\nabla\times\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf J$. Applying <Stokes' theorem> gives $\oint_C\mathbf B\cdot d\boldsymbol\ell=\mu_0I_{\mathrm{enc}}$, where $I_{\mathrm{enc}}$ is the <electric current> crossing an oriented spanning surface. Time-dependent <electric fields> require the additional <displacement current> term in the <Ampère-Maxwell equation>.

= Displacement current
{title2=$\mathbf J_D=\epsilon_0\partial_t\mathbf E$}
{parent=Ampère's circuital law}
{wiki}

In vacuum, <displacement current> is the term $\mathbf J_D=\epsilon_0\partial_t\mathbf E$ in the <Ampère-Maxwell equation>. It has the units of <current density>, but need not describe <electric charge> crossing the region. In <Maxwell equations in matter>, the corresponding term is $\partial_t\mathbf D$, with $\mathbf D$ the <electric displacement field>.

Consider a slowly charging <parallel-plate capacitor> with vacuum gap, plate area $A$, and charge $Q(t)$ on its positive plate. Neglect fringing and assume the charging timescale is long compared with the light-travel time across the apparatus. The nearly uniform gap <electric field> has magnitude $E=Q/(\epsilon_0A)$, so the <displacement current> through the gap is
$$
I_D=\epsilon_0 A\frac{dE}{dt}=\frac{dQ}{dt}=I.
$$
For $A=0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$ and charging <electric current> $I=1.0\,\mathrm{mA}$, this gives $dE/dt\simeq1.13\times10^{10}\,\mathrm{V\,m^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$ and $J_D=0.10\,\mathrm{A/m^2}$. No conducting <electric current> crosses the gap. A spanning surface cutting the wire and another passing between the plates nevertheless give the same magnetic circulation when the full <Ampère-Maxwell equation> is used. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_18.html#Ch18-S2][Feynman lectures, II.18.2: charging a capacitor]{ref}.

The local consistency check is short. Without <displacement current>, taking the <divergence> of $\nabla\times\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf J$ would force $\nabla\cdot\mathbf J=0$, allowing no local accumulation of <electric charge>. With <displacement current>, the <divergence of a curl is zero> and <Gauss's law> give
$$
0=\mu_0\left(\nabla\cdot\mathbf J+\epsilon_0\partial_t\nabla\cdot\mathbf E\right)
=\mu_0\left(\nabla\cdot\mathbf J+\partial_t\rho\right).
$$
This is precisely the <charge continuity equation>. <Electric charge conservation> fixes the required <divergence> of the added term; it does not by itself exclude other divergence-free additions. The specific <Maxwell equations> also yield the <electromagnetic wave equation>, linking this consistency correction to propagation at the <speed of light>.

= Faraday's law of induction
{parent=Maxwell equations}
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=$\nabla\times\mathbf E=-\partial_t\mathbf B$}

= Faraday's law
{c}
{synonym}

= Faraday law
{c}
{synonym}

<Faraday's law of induction> relates a changing <magnetic field> to circulation of the <electric field>. For a fixed <oriented surface> $S$ with boundary $C=\partial S$, choose the positive direction around $C$ by the <right-hand rule>: the fingers follow $C$ while the thumb follows the chosen <unit normal>. The fixed-loop electromagnetic <electromotive force> is
$$
\mathcal E=\oint_C\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l
=-\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int_S\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S
=-\frac{d\Phi_B}{dt},
\qquad
\nabla\times\mathbf E=-\frac{\partial\mathbf B}{\partial t}.
$$
The final <derivative> of <magnetic flux> is an ordinary time derivative, with the <surface> held fixed. Reversing both the <unit normal> and the boundary direction reverses both signs. The loop may be an imagined path: the field law does not require a wire or an <electric current>. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_17.html#Ch17-S1][Feynman lectures, II.17.1: electric-field circulation and induction]{ref}.

For a computed example, a fixed single-turn loop has <area> $A=0.020\,\mathrm{m^2}$ and out-of-page <unit normal>. Prescribe an external uniform <magnetic field> in that direction with $B(t)=0.10\,\mathrm T+(0.30\,\mathrm{T/s})t$ for $0\le t\le1.0\,\mathrm s$. Then
$$
\Phi_{\mathrm{ext}}(t)=AB(t)
=0.0020\,\mathrm{Wb}+(0.0060\,\mathrm{Wb/s})t,
\qquad
\mathcal E_{\mathrm{CCW}}=-0.0060\,\mathrm V.
$$
In a quasistatic resistive model with <electrical resistance> $R=2.0\,\Omega$, neglect the loop's <self-inductance> and its own contribution to changing <magnetic flux>. <Ohm's law> gives
$$
I_{\mathrm{CCW}}=\frac{\mathcal E_{\mathrm{CCW}}}{R}=-0.0030\,\mathrm A.
$$
The <magnetic flux> rises from $0.0020\,\mathrm{Wb}$ to $0.0080\,\mathrm{Wb}$ while the induced <electromotive force> stays constant: it measures the rate of change, rather than the amount of <magnetic flux>. The negative sign means clockwise <electric current>; its <magnetic field> through the loop points into the page, as explained by <Lenz's law>. The toy model describes the interval of steady ramping, excluding switching transients at its ends.

\Image[faraday-changing-flux-and-lenz-current.png]
{title=<Faraday's law of induction> and <Lenz's law> for increasing out-of-page <magnetic flux>}
{description=Counterclockwise is the positive orientation. Dots denote the external out-of-page <magnetic field>, and crosses denote the in-page <magnetic field> produced by the clockwise induced <electric current>. The plots show $\Phi_{\mathrm{ext}}$ increasing from $2$ to $8\,\mathrm{mWb}$, with constant $\mathcal E=-6\,\mathrm{mV}$ and $I=-3\,\mathrm{mA}$ in the resistive approximation.}

For a moving conducting loop $C(t)$, the electromagnetic <electromotive force> instead includes the <Lorentz force> contribution from the local <velocity> $\mathbf v$ of each conductor segment:
$$
\mathcal E(t)=\oint_{C(t)}(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\mathbf l
=-\frac{d}{dt}\int_{S(t)}\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S.
$$
This form assumes a thin-wire model with a well-defined closed contour and spanning <oriented surface> that move compatibly, a <magnetic field> satisfying <Gauss's law for magnetism>, and conductor speeds small compared with the <speed of light>. Ideal sliding contacts such as those in <motional electromotive force> can be treated piecewise. Arbitrary changes of electrical connections require checking the actual contour and conductor velocities; differentiating the flux through an arbitrary chosen moving path is insufficient. The <velocity> here describes conductor motion, not the charge carriers' drift along the wire. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_17.html#Ch17-S2][Feynman lectures, II.17.2: limits of the flux rule]{ref}.

A short derivation of the fixed-loop integral statement applies <Stokes theorem> to the local <curl> equation and differentiates under the fixed <surface integral>. For the moving loop, the <magnetic flux transport for a moving circuit> adds the swept-area term $-\oint_C(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\mathbf l$ to $d\Phi_B/dt$. Combining these two identities gives the moving-loop statement. This derives the integral forms from the local field law; it is not a proof of that empirical law from mathematics alone.

The useful unity is geometric: changing the <magnetic field>, the enclosed <area>, or the orientation can change <magnetic flux> and hence produce an <electromotive force>. A coil's multiple turns are accounted for by <magnetic flux linkage>, rather than by multiplying a single-turn flux without checking which flux each turn receives. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-1-faradays-law][OpenStax University Physics 2, §13.1: flux changes and coils]{ref}.

Faraday's report read on 24 November 1831 describes opposite momentary responses when a neighboring circuit is connected and disconnected, with no continuing response while its steady <electric current> is maintained. These are concrete experimental roots of the changing-flux idea. The report predates the modern <Maxwell equations> notation used above. \a[https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14986/14986-h/14986-h.htm][Faraday, Experimental Researches in Electricity, First Series (1831), §§10–11, reprinted in volume I]{ref}.

= Lenz's law
{parent=Faraday's law of induction}
{c}
{wiki}

<Lenz's law> expresses the minus sign in <Faraday's law of induction>: the induced <electromotive force> drives a passive resistive loop's <electric current> in the direction whose own <magnetic field> opposes the imposed change in <magnetic flux>. **It opposes the change of flux, not invariably the external magnetic field.** Choose an <oriented surface> and its positive boundary direction first, find the sign of $d\Phi_{\mathrm{ext}}/dt$, and then use the <right-hand rule> to translate the signed <electric current> into a physical direction. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-2-lenzs-law][OpenStax University Physics 2, §13.2: current direction and changing flux]{ref}.

In the fixed-loop example in <Faraday's law of induction>, out-of-page <magnetic flux> increases at $0.0060\,\mathrm{Wb/s}$. With counterclockwise positive, $I=-0.0030\,\mathrm A$ is clockwise, producing an in-page <magnetic field> through the loop. Reverse the ramp to $B(t)=0.40\,\mathrm T-(0.30\,\mathrm{T/s})t$ on the same one-second interval: now $\mathcal E=+0.0060\,\mathrm V$ and $I=+0.0030\,\mathrm A$. The counterclockwise <electric current> produces an out-of-page <magnetic field>, supporting the decreasing external <magnetic flux>. A steady out-of-page <magnetic field> with fixed loop geometry gives no induced <electromotive force> in either direction.

For the increasing-area example in <motional electromotive force>, clockwise <electric current> runs down the right-hand rod. Its <magnetic force on a current-carrying wire> points left, opposing the imposed motion to the right. Writing $I_{\mathrm{mag}}=B\ell v/R$ for the current magnitude, the <power> required to maintain constant <speed> is
$$
P_{\mathrm{drive}}=(I_{\mathrm{mag}}\ell B)v
=\frac{(B\ell v)^2}{R}=I_{\mathrm{mag}}^2R=P_{\mathrm{Joule}}.
$$
Thus the sign agrees with <conservation of energy>: the external drive supplies the <Joule heating>. The <magnetic forces do no work> on individual charges; mechanical <work> by the drive supplies the electrical <energy>. This also explains the usefulness of <electromagnetic braking of a sliding rod> and the <terminal speed of a falling conducting loop>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-3-motional-emf][OpenStax University Physics 2, §13.3: mechanical power and resistive heating]{ref}.

The sign of induced <electromotive force> alone does not determine an arbitrary circuit's instantaneous <electric current>. Retaining constant <self-inductance> $L$ gives $L\,dI/dt+RI=-d\Phi_{\mathrm{ext}}/dt$, so a pre-existing <electric current> can persist while the induced drive opposes its change. The distinction matters in the <lossless limit of magnetic loop braking>. The resistive toy examples use $L=0$ as an approximation, not a general consequence of <Lenz's law>.

= Terminal speed of a falling conducting loop
{parent=Lenz's law}

If a loop of resistance $R$ has magnetic flux $\Phi_B=-kz$, then its induced current is $I=k\dot z/R$. When its magnetic force is $-kI$ in the vertical direction, its equation under gravity is
$$
m\ddot z=-mg-\frac{k^2}{R}\dot z,
$$
so its downward terminal speed is $Rmg/k^2$.

= Lossless limit of magnetic loop braking
{parent=Terminal speed of a falling conducting loop}
{title2=$\mathcal L I+Kz=\text{constant}$}

For an external loop flux $Kz$, positive current orientation and upward <Lorentz force> $KI$, including <self-inductance> gives $\mathcal L\dot I+RI=-K\dot z$ and $m\ddot z=-mg+KI$. At zero resistance total linked flux is conserved. If initially $I=0$ at $z=z_0$, then $I=-K(z-z_0)/\mathcal L$ and the mechanical motion is an undamped oscillator about $z_0-mg\mathcal L/K^2$. The finite-resistance terminal speed tends to zero as $R\to0$, but neglecting <self-inductance> is a singular approximation in that limit. A lossless loop does not dispose of <energy> through <Joule heating>.

= Gauss's law
{parent=Maxwell equations}
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=$\oint_{\partial V}\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf n\,dS=Q_{\mathrm{enclosed}}/\varepsilon_0$}

= Gauss law
{c}
{synonym}

<Gauss's law> relates the <electric field> on a <closed surface> to the net <electric charge> inside it. In <SI> units, with outward <unit normal> $\mathbf n$,
$$
\boxed{\oint_{\partial V}\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf n\,dS=\frac{Q_{\mathrm{enclosed}}}{\varepsilon_0},\qquad Q_{\mathrm{enclosed}}=\int_V\rho\,dV.}
$$
Here $\rho$ describes a volume <charge density>, and $\varepsilon_0$ is the <vacuum permittivity>; for ideal point or surface charges, use the enclosed <electric charge> directly rather than an ordinary volume-density integral. The left side is <electric flux>: the <surface integral> of the outward normal component of the <electric field>. Field directed outward contributes positively; field directed inward contributes negatively. The equality uses net signed <electric charge>, so positive and negative enclosed charges can cancel. The <Gaussian surface> is an imagined accounting boundary, not necessarily a material object. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-2-explaining-gausss-law][OpenStax, explaining Gauss's law]{ref}.

Where the <electric field> and <charge density> are smooth, the local statement is $\nabla\cdot\mathbf E=\rho/\varepsilon_0$. The <divergence theorem> connects this <divergence> to the <surface integral>. This is the satisfying connection between local sources and global accounting: a surface can register how much net <electric charge> it encloses without revealing where that charge is located within it. A <point charge> requires care with the singular field at its position; the integral formulation remains useful with a boundary that avoids the charge.

The law fixes total <electric flux>, rather than every value of the <electric field>. In an uncharged region, a uniform <electric field> $\mathbf E=(E_0,0,0)$ crosses a cube of side $\ell$. Its left and right faces contribute $-E_0\ell^2$ and $+E_0\ell^2$; the other four faces contribute zero. Thus **the net electric flux is zero while the electric field is nonzero everywhere**. Charges outside a <Gaussian surface> can change the field on it without changing its net <electric flux>. Conversely, fields cannot generally be reconstructed from enclosed <electric charge> alone. <Electrostatics> also requires the <electric field> to be the negative <gradient> of an <electric potential>, with appropriate boundary information. This distinction is emphasized in \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_05.html][Feynman lectures II.5, application of Gauss' law]{ref}.

\Image[gaussian-flux-and-field.png]
{title=Zero net flux can coexist with a nonzero uniform field, and equal enclosed charge can give different field magnitudes on different spheres}

<Spherical symmetry> supplies the extra information needed for a particularly simple calculation. An isolated <spherically symmetric> charge distribution in vacuum produces a radial <electric field>, constant in magnitude on each concentric <sphere>. Its <electric flux> is therefore $4\pi r^2E_r(r)$, giving
$$
E_r(r)=\frac{Q_{\mathrm{enclosed}}(r)}{4\pi\varepsilon_0r^2}.
$$
Outside the distribution, doubling $r$ quadruples the <surface area> and divides the radial <electric field> by four, while the <electric flux> stays $Q/\varepsilon_0$. The inverse-square dependence is an area-dilution result. The <electric field of a uniformly charged ball> and <electric field of a uniformly charged spherical shell> show that the same total charge can give different interior fields. These symmetry arguments and their limits are developed in \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-3-applying-gausss-law][OpenStax, applying Gauss's law]{ref}.

= Electric field of a uniformly charged ball
{parent=Gauss's law}
{title2=$E_r(r)=Qr/(4\pi\varepsilon_0R^3)\quad(0\leq r\leq R)$}

= Electric field of a uniformly charged solid sphere
{synonym}

Distribute a positive <electric charge> $Q$ uniformly through a <closed ball> of radius $R$ in vacuum, with no external field. Its volume <charge density> is $3Q/(4\pi R^3)$. For $0<r<R$, the enclosed fraction is $(r/R)^3$. Applying <Gauss's law> on a concentric <Gaussian surface> gives
$$
E_r(r)=\begin{cases}\dfrac{Qr}{4\pi\varepsilon_0R^3},&0\leq r\leq R,\\[4pt]\dfrac{Q}{4\pi\varepsilon_0r^2},&r\geq R.\end{cases}
$$
The <electric field> grows linearly inside and decays as an inverse square outside. With $E_R=Q/(4\pi\varepsilon_0R^2)$, its values at $r/R=1/2,1,2$ are $E_R/2,E_R,E_R/4$. The field vanishes at the centre by <spherical symmetry> and is continuous at the surface because this ideal volume distribution has no singular surface layer. Its interior behavior differs from a <conductor in electrostatic equilibrium>, where mobile charge redistributes.

\Image[gauss-charged-ball-and-shell.png]
{title=A uniform volume charge and a uniform thin-shell charge have the same exterior field but different interior fields}

= Electric field of a uniformly charged spherical shell
{parent=Gauss's law}
{title2=$E_r(r)=0\ (r<R),\quad E_r(r)=Q/(4\pi\varepsilon_0r^2)\ (r>R)$}

Place the same positive <electric charge> $Q$ uniformly on the <sphere> of radius $R$, with no external field. Every concentric interior <Gaussian surface> encloses zero charge; <spherical symmetry> then makes the interior <electric field> zero. Outside, it is again $Q/(4\pi\varepsilon_0r^2)$. At $R$, the inner and outer limits differ by $Q/(4\pi\varepsilon_0R^2)=\sigma/\varepsilon_0$, with <surface charge density> $\sigma=Q/(4\pi R^2)$. The ideal surface charge makes the field discontinuous, so the plot displays one-sided limits rather than assigning a single field value on the charged sheet. An isolated charged conducting sphere in <electrostatic equilibrium> realizes this exterior/interior pattern.

= Hyperbolic-space Coulomb field
{parent=Gauss's law}
{title2=$E(\xi)=Q/[4\pi\epsilon_0R^2\sinh^2(\xi/R)]$}

In static time times round three-dimensional <hyperbolic space> of curvature radius $R$, a geodesic sphere has area $4\pi R^2\sinh^2(\xi/R)$. The <Gauss law> therefore gives the displayed radial field. It approaches the flat-space inverse-square field near the charge and falls exponentially at large geodesic distance. The curvature changes the area of a sphere, not the conserved flux.

= Force between hemispheres of a uniformly charged ball
{parent=Gauss's law}
{title2=$F=3Q^2/(64\pi\varepsilon_0R^2)$}

For uniform <charge density> $\rho_e=3Q/(4\pi R^3)$, <Gauss's law> gives the interior <electric field> $E=\rho_e r/(3\varepsilon_0)$. Integrate its vertical force density $\rho_e^2z/(3\varepsilon_0)$ over the upper hemisphere: $\int z\,dV=\pi R^4/4$. Internal pair forces in that hemisphere cancel, so its net force is exactly the force from the lower hemisphere. The result is upward and has magnitude $3Q^2/(64\pi\varepsilon_0R^2)$.

= Gaussian surface
{c}
{parent=Gauss's law}
{wiki}

A <Gaussian surface> is a <closed surface> used with the integral form of <Gauss's law> to relate <electric flux> to enclosed <electric charge>. It need not coincide with the surface of any charged body. Choosing it to match a <symmetry> can make the normal component of the <electric field> constant on a portion of it, allowing that component to be taken outside a <surface integral>. A concentric <sphere> works for an isolated <spherically symmetric> charge distribution; an arbitrarily placed <sphere> generally does not.

Zero enclosed <electric charge> implies zero total <electric flux>. Inferring zero <electric field> additionally needs information such as the <spherical symmetry> used in the <electric field of a uniformly charged spherical shell>. The imagined boundary does not itself block or redirect the <electric field>.

= Gaussian pillbox
{c}
{parent=Gauss's law}

A Gaussian pillbox is a thin cylindrical <Gaussian surface> crossing an interface. Shrinking its height converts a divergence law into a jump condition for the field's normal component.

= Charge conservation from Maxwell equations
{parent=Maxwell equations}

Taking the divergence of the Ampère-Maxwell equation and using Gauss's law gives
$$
\partial_t\rho+\nabla\cdot J=0.
$$
Hence the charge in a fixed volume changes only through current crossing its boundary.

= Charge continuity equation
{parent=Charge conservation from Maxwell equations}
{title2=$\partial_t\rho+\nabla\cdot\mathbf J=0$}

= Continuity equation for electric charge
{synonym}

The local form of <conservation of electric charge> relates <charge density> to <current density>. Taking the <divergence> of the <Ampère-Maxwell equation> and substituting <Gauss's law> proves the equation, using that the <divergence of a curl is zero>.

= Covariant Maxwell equation with the minus-plus-plus-plus metric
{parent=Maxwell equations}
{c}

With metric signature $(-,+,+,+)$, $A_0=-\Phi/c$, and $j^\mu=(c\rho,J)$, the field tensor satisfies
$$
\partial_\nu F^{\mu\nu}=\mu_0j^\mu,
\qquad
\partial_{[\lambda}F_{\mu\nu]}=0.
$$
Its components are $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, so these tensor equations reproduce all four Maxwell equations.

= Source-free Maxwell equations in a linear medium
{parent=Maxwell equations}

For $D=\epsilon E$ and $B=\mu H$ with no free charge or current,
$$
\nabla\cdot D=0,\qquad
\nabla\cdot B=0,\qquad
\nabla\times E=-\partial_tB,\qquad
\nabla\times H=\partial_tD.
$$

= Plane electromagnetic wave in a linear medium
{parent=Source-free Maxwell equations in a linear medium}

A plane wave in a homogeneous linear medium satisfies
$$
\omega^2=\frac{|k|^2}{\epsilon\mu},
\qquad
v=\frac1{\sqrt{\epsilon\mu}},
\qquad
B_0=\frac1\omega k\times E_0.
$$

= Transversality of an electromagnetic plane wave
{parent=Plane electromagnetic wave in a linear medium}

The source-free divergence equations imply $\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf E_0=\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf B_0=0$, so both fields are transverse to the wavevector.

= Magnetic vector potential
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A magnetic vector potential satisfies B equal to its curl and is defined up to a gradient.

= Magnetic helicity
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{title2=$H=\int_V\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf B\,dV,\quad\mathbf B=\nabla\times\mathbf A$}
{wiki}

The <magnetic helicity> integrates the <dot product> of a <magnetic vector potential> and its <magnetic field>. It measures linkage and twisting of magnetic flux. For a <solenoidal vector field> $\mathbf B$ tangent to the boundary, it is unchanged by the gauge transformation $\mathbf A\mapsto\mathbf A+\nabla\chi$, since the change is $\int_V\nabla\cdot(\chi\mathbf B)\,dV=\int_{\partial V}\chi\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S=0$. If its density satisfies $\partial_t h=\nabla\cdot(f\mathbf B-h\mathbf u)$ with both vector fields tangent to the boundary of a fixed volume, the <divergence theorem> gives conservation of its integral. This is an instance of <helicity conservation by tangent boundary conditions>.

= Magnetic-energy lower bound from helicity
{parent=Magnetic helicity}
{title2=$E_B\geq |H_M|/(2\mu_0 C_D)$}

In a bounded regular domain, fix the global extension or circulation convention for the <magnetic vector potential>. A bounded inverse of <curl> on tangent <solenoidal vector fields> gives $\|\mathbf A\|_2\leq C_D\|\mathbf B\|_2$. The <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> then gives $|H_M|\leq C_D\|\mathbf B\|_2^2=2\mu_0 C_DE_B$. One construction extends the tangent field by zero outside the domain and uses the whole-space <Biot-Savart law>; its kernel of size $|\mathbf x-\mathbf y|^{-2}$ is integrable over a bounded region, giving the required $L^2$ bound. Nonzero conserved <magnetic helicity> therefore prevents complete decay of <magnetic energy>.

= Current helicity
{parent=Magnetic helicity}
{title2=$H_c=\langle\mathbf b\cdot\nabla\times\mathbf b\rangle$}

Current helicity correlates a <magnetic field> with its <curl>, which is proportional to <electric current density>. It differs from <magnetic helicity>, which uses a <magnetic vector potential>. For a solenoidal monochromatic field of wavenumber $k$ in a periodic domain, choose $\mathbf a_b=\nabla\times\mathbf b/k^2$. Then $\nabla\times\mathbf a_b=\mathbf b$ and $H_c=k^2\langle\mathbf a_b\cdot\mathbf b\rangle$.

= Gauge dependence of helicity in an open magnetic flux tube
{parent=Magnetic helicity}
{title2=$H_m/L=\pi bCR_0^2$}

An open <flux tube> carries <magnetic flux> through its end caps. Its ordinary <magnetic helicity> can therefore change under a single-valued <gauge transformation>. For $\mathbf B=aR\mathbf e_\phi+b\mathbf e_z$ inside $R<R_0$ and zero outside, a regular family of <magnetic vector potentials> is $A_\phi=bR/2$, $A_z=C-aR^2/2$ inside and $A_\phi=bR_0^2/(2R)$, $A_z=C-aR_0^2/2$ outside. Their tangential components agree at $R_0$. Inside, $\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf B=bC$, so the helicity per unit length is $\pi bCR_0^2$. Changing $C$ is the gauge transformation $\Lambda=\Delta C\,z$, and changes this result whenever $b\ne0$. The convention $A_z=0$ outside gives $C=aR_0^2/2$ and helicity per unit length $\pi abR_0^4/2$, while $C=0$ gives zero. Without an end-cap or gauge convention the ordinary helicity is not unique.

= Material transport of magnetic helicity density
{parent=Magnetic helicity}
{title2=$D_t(\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf B/\rho)$}

For smooth <ideal magnetohydrodynamics>, write $\mathbf B=\nabla\times\mathbf A$ and $\partial_t\mathbf A=\mathbf u\times\mathbf B-\nabla\varphi$. The <continuity equation> and induction equation give $D_t(\mathbf B/\rho)=(\mathbf B/\rho)\cdot\nabla\mathbf u$. The vector-potential equation becomes $D_t\mathbf A=\nabla(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf A-\varphi)-(\nabla\mathbf u)^T\mathbf A$. Contracting these two equations cancels their velocity-gradient terms and proves
$$
D_t\left(\frac{\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf B}{\rho}\right)=\frac{\mathbf B}{\rho}\cdot\nabla(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf A-\varphi).
$$
Consequently the <magnetic helicity> in a material volume satisfies $dH_m/dt=\int_{\partial V}(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf A-\varphi)\mathbf B\cdot\mathbf n\,dS$. It is conserved when the <magnetic field> is tangent to the boundary. A single-valued <gauge transformation> changes $H_m$ by $\int_{\partial V}\Lambda\mathbf B\cdot\mathbf n\,dS$, which also vanishes under that condition.

= Radial-gauge potential for a divergence-free magnetic field
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{title2=$\mathbf A(\mathbf x)=\int_0^1s\,\mathbf B(s\mathbf x)\times\mathbf x\,ds$}

For a smooth <divergence>-free field on $\mathbb R^3$, the <differential form> $F=\iota_{\mathbf B}(dx^1\wedge dx^2\wedge dx^3)$ is closed. The radial homotopy formula gives a global <magnetic vector potential> $\mathbf A(\mathbf x)=\int_0^1s\,\mathbf B(s\mathbf x)\times\mathbf x\,ds$ with <curl> $\mathbf B$. For constant $\mathbf B$, this reduces to $\mathbf A=\tfrac12\mathbf B\times\mathbf x$.

= Neumann's mutual-inductance formula
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{c}

For thin closed wire loops $C_1,C_2$, the mutual inductance is
$$
L_{12}=L_{21}
=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}
\oint_{C_1}\oint_{C_2}
\frac{dx_1\cdot dx_2}{|x_1-x_2|}.
$$

= Coulomb gauge
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{c}
{wiki}

The Coulomb gauge imposes
$$
\nabla\cdot A=0
$$
on the <magnetic vector potential>. A <gauge transformation> $A\mapsto A+\nabla\chi$ reaches this gauge when $\chi$ solves
$$
\nabla^2\chi=-\nabla\cdot A.
$$

= Radiation gauge
{parent=Coulomb gauge}
{title2=$A_0=0,\quad\partial_iA_i=0$}

For a free <electromagnetic field> with suitable boundary conditions, combine <Coulomb gauge> $\partial_iA_i=0$ with $A_0=0$. <Gauss's law> makes the second condition consistent in the source-free problem. The remaining field operator is spatially transverse and describes the two radiative <photon> polarizations. A constrained scalar potential must be restored when charges are present.

= Gauge transformation
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{wiki}

The transformation $A\mapsto A+\nabla\chi$ leaves $B=\nabla\times A$ unchanged because the <curl> of a <gradient> vanishes.

= Gauge-covariant time translation
{parent=Gauge transformation}
{title2=$\delta A_\nu=\alpha F_{0\nu}$}

For the <Maxwell field>, the variation $\delta A_\nu=\alpha F_{0\nu}=\alpha(\partial_0A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_0)$ is time translation combined with a <gauge transformation>. With constant $\alpha$, $\delta F_{\mu\nu}=\alpha\partial_0F_{\mu\nu}$, and the Lagrangian changes by $\alpha\partial_0\mathcal L$. Its <Noether current> is directly the time component of the <gauge-invariant Maxwell stress-energy tensor>, providing the physical energy density without a potential-dependent improvement step.

= Magnetic dipole moment
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{wiki}

For a localized steady current density,
$$
m=\frac12\int x\times J(x)\,d^3x.
$$
It has dimensions $\mathrm{A\,m^2}$.

= Localized steady-current moment identities
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}
{title2=$\int j_i=0,\quad\int x_i j_k=-\int x_k j_i$}

A localized steady <electric current density> satisfies $\nabla\cdot\mathbf j=0$. Integrate the divergences of $x_i\mathbf j$ and $x_ix_k\mathbf j$ over a region enclosing its support; all boundary fluxes vanish. The first identity removes the monopole term from the magnetic vector potential expansion. The antisymmetric first moment is equivalent to the <magnetic dipole moment> $\frac12\int\mathbf x\times\mathbf j\,dV$.

= Magnetic dipole potential energy
{title2=$U=-\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf B$}
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}

In an external field that varies slowly across a localized magnetic dipole,
$$
U=-\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf B,
\qquad
\mathbf F=\nabla(\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf B).
$$

The <magnetic dipole moment> determines the coupling to the external magnetic field.

= Bohr magneton
{title2=$\mu_B$}
{c}
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}
{wiki}

The Bohr magneton,
$$
\mu_B=\frac{e\hbar}{2m_e},
$$
is the natural unit of an electron's orbital and spin magnetic moment.

= Coulomb-gauge vector potential of a localized steady current
{c}
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}

If $\nabla\cdot J=0$ and the current decays enough for boundary terms to vanish, then
$$
A(x)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\int\frac{J(x')}{|x-x'|}\,d^3x'
$$
satisfies $\nabla\cdot A=0$. Its far field is
$$
A(x)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\frac{m\times x}{|x|^3}+\cdots.
$$

= Magnetic dipole moment of a rigidly rotating charge distribution
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}

A circular charge distribution rotating with angular speed $\omega$ can be decomposed into current loops. A hoop of line density $\eta$ and radius $R$ has $m=\pi\eta\omega R^3\hat n$; a disc of surface density $\sigma$ has $m=\pi\sigma\omega R^4\hat n/4$.

= Biot-Savart law
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

For a steady <current density> $J$, the <magnetic field> is
$$
B(r)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\int
\frac{J(r')\times(r-r')}{|r-r'|^3}\,d^3r'.
$$

= On-axis magnetic field of a circular current loop
{parent=Biot-Savart law}

A circular loop of radius $R$ carrying <electric current> $I$ has, on its axis,
$$
B(z)=\frac{\mu_0IR^2}{2(R^2+z^2)^{3/2}}\,\hat z,
$$
where the sign of $\hat z$ is fixed by the <right-hand rule>. At its centre, $B(0)=\mu_0I/(2R)$.

= Magnetic-field cancellation between oppositely driven coaxial loops
{parent=Biot-Savart law}

If coaxial loops of radii $R$ and $2R$, separated by $D$, carry currents $I$ and $2I$ in opposite senses, their on-axis fields cancel between the loops at distance $D/3$ from the smaller loop.

= Beltrami field
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A Beltrami field is a vector field parallel to its curl:
$$
\nabla\times J=\lambda J.
$$
If it is divergence-free and used as a magnetostatic current with constant nonzero $\lambda$, then $B=\mu_0J/\lambda$ solves $\nabla\times B=\mu_0J$ and $\nabla\cdot B=0$.

= Relativistic electromagnetism
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

Relativistic electromagnetism combines electric and magnetic fields into Lorentz tensors.

= Relativistic charged-particle action
{parent=Relativistic electromagnetism}

For metric signature $(-,+,+,+)$, a particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ coupled to a four-potential has the reparametrization-invariant action
$$
S=-mc\int\sqrt{-\dot x^2}\,d\lambda
+q\int A_\mu(x)\dot x^\mu\,d\lambda.
$$

= Electromagnetic minimal coupling
{parent=Relativistic charged-particle action}

Electromagnetic minimal coupling replaces the free kinetic momentum in an action or Hamiltonian by a combination involving the electromagnetic potential. For $L=-m\sqrt{\dot x^a\dot x_a}-qA_a\dot x^a$, the canonical covector is $P_a=-m\dot x_a/\sqrt{\dot x^2}-qA_a$, so $P_a+qA_a$ recovers the potential-independent covector. Under $A_a\mapsto A_a+\partial_a\chi$, the action changes by an endpoint term. The fixed-endpoint equations are the <relativistic Lorentz force> equation and are gauge invariant.

= Gauge invariance of the charged-particle worldline action
{parent=Relativistic charged-particle action}

Under $A_\mu\mapsto A_\mu+\partial_\mu\chi$, the interaction changes by
$$
q\int\frac{d\chi(x(\lambda))}{d\lambda}\,d\lambda
=q\,[\chi]_{\rm endpoints}.
$$
Fixed-endpoint equations of motion are therefore gauge invariant.

= Lorentz-force equation from the worldline action
{parent=Relativistic charged-particle action}
{c}

Varying the <relativistic charged-particle action> with fixed endpoints gives
$$
m\frac{du_\mu}{d\tau}=qF_{\mu\nu}u^\nu,
$$
or the equivalent raised-index equation.

= Electromagnetic four-potential
{parent=Relativistic electromagnetism}

= Four-potential
{synonym}

The four-potential combines the <electric potential> and <magnetic vector potential> as $A^\mu=(\phi/c,\mathbf A)$. The electromagnetic four-potential $A_\mu$ transforms under
$$
A_\mu\mapsto A_\mu+\partial_\mu\chi,
$$
while the antisymmetric field tensor $F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu$ is unchanged.

= Electromagnetic multipole expansion
{parent=Electromagnetic four-potential}

An electromagnetic multipole expansion expresses the potentials or fields of a localized source as successively smaller monopole, dipole, quadrupole, and higher moments in the ratio of source size to observation distance.

= Retarded potential
{parent=Electromagnetic four-potential}
{wiki}

= Retarded electromagnetic potential
{synonym}

A retarded potential evaluates each source at the event on the source's past light cone. This accounts for propagation at the speed of light and enforces <causality>.

= Retarded time
{title2=$t_{\rm ret}$}
{parent=Retarded potential}
{wiki}

For a source point $y(s)$ and field event $(x,t)$, the retarded time solves
$$
t_{\rm ret}+\frac{|x-y(t_{\rm ret})|}{c}=t.
$$

= Uniqueness of retarded time for a subluminal source
{parent=Retarded time}

For $R(s)=|x-y(s)|$ and $|v|<c$, the function $F(s)=s+R(s)/c$ has
$$
F'(s)=1-\widehat R\cdot v/c>0.
$$
It is strictly increasing, so the retarded-time equation has at most one solution.

= Liénard–Wiechert potential
{c}
{parent=Retarded potential}
{wiki}

The potentials of a point charge $q$ are
$$
\phi=\frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}
\frac1{R-\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf R/c},
\qquad
\mathbf A=\frac{\mathbf v}{c^2}\phi,
$$
with every source quantity evaluated at the <retarded time>.

= Lorenz gauge condition
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic four-potential}
{wiki}

= Lorenz gauge
{c}
{synonym}

The Lorenz gauge condition is
$$
\frac1{c^2}\partial_t\phi+\nabla\cdot\mathbf A=0,
$$
or $\partial_\mu A^\mu=0$ in covariant notation.

= Residual Lorenz gauge symmetry
{parent=Lorenz gauge condition}
{title2=$\Box\lambda=0$}

An Abelian <gauge transformation> $A_a\mapsto A_a+\partial_a\lambda$ changes $\partial\cdot A$ by $\Box\lambda$. Consequently <gauge fixing> by a quadratic function of $\partial\cdot A$ retains transformations satisfying $\Box\lambda=0$, subject to the same <boundary conditions>. Eliminating those residual transformations is part of specifying an inverse with a particular <Green function> prescription.

= Electromagnetic field tensor
{title2=$F^{\mu\nu}$}
{parent=Electromagnetic four-potential}
{wiki}

The electromagnetic field tensor is
$$
F^{\mu\nu}=\partial^\mu A^\nu-\partial^\nu A^\mu.
$$
It packages the electric and magnetic fields into an antisymmetric rank-two Lorentz tensor and transforms as
$$
F'^{\mu\nu}(x')
=\Lambda^\mu{}_\rho\Lambda^\nu{}_\sigma F^{\rho\sigma}(x).
$$

= Dual electromagnetic field tensor
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{title2=$\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}$}

The dual electromagnetic field tensor is obtained by applying the <Hodge star operator> to the <electromagnetic field tensor>. For metric signature $(+---)$ and $\epsilon^{0123}=+1$, define $\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}=\tfrac12\epsilon^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}F_{\rho\sigma}$. With $F^{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F^{ij}=-\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, it has components $\widetilde F^{0i}=-B_i$ and $\widetilde F^{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}E_k/c$. The homogeneous <Maxwell equations> become $\partial_\mu\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}=0$.

= Maxwell Lagrangian
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{title2=$\mathcal L=-\frac14F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}$}

The source-free electromagnetic <Lagrangian density> is quadratic in the <electromagnetic field tensor>. Its <Euler-Lagrange field equation> is $\partial_\mu F^{\mu\nu}=0$. Antisymmetry of $F$ makes the density invariant under <gauge transformations>, and contraction of its indices makes it a <Lorentz scalar>.

= Maxwell action
{c}
{parent=Maxwell Lagrangian}
{title2=$S_E=\tfrac14\int F_{\mu\nu}F_{\mu\nu}\,d^4x$}

The source-free <Maxwell Lagrangian> gives $S=-\tfrac14\int F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}\,d^4x$ in mostly-minus Lorentzian signature. Its <Euclidean action> is the displayed positive quadratic functional of the <electromagnetic field tensor>. Variation, with boundary terms vanishing, yields $\partial_\mu F_{\mu\nu}=0$.

= First-order Maxwell action
{parent=Maxwell action}
{title2=$S=\frac1{16\pi}\int\sqrt{-g}\,[F^{ab}F_{ab}-2F^{ab}(\nabla_aA_b-\nabla_bA_a)]$}

Vary the antisymmetric auxiliary <electromagnetic field tensor> and the <electromagnetic four-potential> independently. The first variation sets $F_{ab}=\nabla_aA_b-\nabla_bA_a$ algebraically, and variation of the potential gives $\nabla_bF^{ab}=0$. Substitution of the algebraic equation reduces the first-order action to the usual negative quadratic <Maxwell action>. Metric variation gives the same stress tensor after eliminating the auxiliary field, because the chain-rule term multiplying its variational equation then vanishes.

= Electromagnetic Bianchi identity
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}

Because the <electromagnetic field tensor> is the <exterior derivative> $F=dA$ of a potential, $dF=d^2A=0$. For a torsion-free connection this is
$$
\nabla_aF_{bc}+\nabla_bF_{ca}+\nabla_cF_{ab}=0,
$$
which is the covariant form of the two homogeneous <Maxwell equations>.

= Lorentz force
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf F=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$}
{wiki}

= Lorentz force law
{c}
{synonym}

The <Lorentz force> is the <force> exerted by an <electric field> $\mathbf E$ and a <magnetic field> $\mathbf B$ on a body with <electric charge> $q$ and <velocity> $\mathbf v$. In <SI> units its form in nonrelativistic <classical mechanics>, using <mass> $m$ and <Newton's second law>, is
$$
\mathbf F=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B),\qquad
m\frac{d\mathbf v}{dt}=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B).
$$
Both fields are evaluated at the body's <position> at the current <time>. The <electric field> contributes $q\mathbf E$ even at rest, whereas the magnetic <force> depends on <velocity> through a <cross product>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-4-electric-field][OpenStax, electric force and field]{ref}; \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-2-magnetic-fields-and-lines][OpenStax, magnetic force on moving charge]{ref}.

For a concrete <charged particle in a uniform magnetic field>, use <nondimensionalization> to stipulate $m=q=B=1$, <magnetic field> $\mathbf B=(0,0,1)$, zero <electric field>, initial <position> $(0,0,0)$, and initial <velocity> $(1,0,0)$. These are rescaled quantities, not an assertion that the <mass>, <electric charge>, and <magnetic field> have the same physical units. At the start, $(1,0,0)\times(0,0,1)=(0,-1,0)$, so the magnetic <acceleration> points toward $-y$. The resulting <circular motion> is
$$
\mathbf r(t)=(\sin t,\cos t-1,0),\qquad
\mathbf v(t)=(\cos t,-\sin t,0),\qquad
\mathbf a(t)=(-\sin t,-\cos t,0).
$$
At $t=\pi/2$ the <position> is $(1,-1,0)$ and the <velocity> is $(0,-1,0)$; at $t=\pi$ the <position> is $(0,-2,0)$ and the <velocity> is $(-1,0,0)$. Thus the <circle> has center $(0,-1,0)$ and <radius> one. Viewed from $+z$ toward the $xy$ <plane>, the motion is clockwise. Changing only the <electric charge> to $q=-1$ gives <position> $(\sin t,1-\cos t,0)$ and reverses the bending to counterclockwise.

\Image[lorentz-force-orbits.png]
{title=<Lorentz force>: <circular motion> and a <helix> in the same uniform <magnetic field>}
{description=Stipulated rescaled $m=q=B=1$ and zero <electric field>. Left: positive <electric charge> follows the clockwise <circle> from initial <velocity> $(1,0,0)$; the <velocity> is tangent and the magnetic <force> points inward. Dots denote a <magnetic field> out of the <plane>. Right: adding parallel <velocity> $v_z=0.5$ gives <helical motion in a uniform magnetic field>, with <position> $z=0.5t$ and the same transverse <radius> one. Straight arrows show <magnetic field lines>, distinct from the curved path.}

Adding initial <velocity> $v_z=0.5$ leaves the transverse <circular motion> unchanged and gives
$$
\mathbf r(t)=(\sin t,\cos t-1,0.5t),\qquad
\mathbf v(t)=(\cos t,-\sin t,0.5).
$$
This is a <helix> of transverse <radius> one, <cyclotron frequency> $\omega_c=1$, and transverse <period> $T=2\pi$; the advance along $z$ in one turn is $\pi$. The parallel component of <velocity> stays constant because the magnetic <force> has no parallel component: there is no magnetic <acceleration> along the <magnetic field>. If the initial <velocity> were entirely parallel to the <magnetic field>, the magnetic <force> would vanish and the path would be straight. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-3-motion-of-a-charged-particle-in-a-magnetic-field][OpenStax, circular and helical motion]{ref}.

More generally, with zero <electric field>, no other <forces>, and a uniform constant <magnetic field> of magnitude $B>0$, decompose <velocity> into perpendicular and parallel components. For nonzero <electric charge> $q$, the perpendicular <speed> $v_\perp$ determines the <gyroradius>, while the <cyclotron frequency> is an <angular frequency>:
$$
r=\frac{mv_\perp}{|q|B},\qquad
\omega_c=\frac{|q|B}{m},\qquad
T=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_c}=\frac{2\pi m}{|q|B}.
$$
These are nonrelativistic formulas; see the <relativistic cyclotron orbit> for the correction. The beauty is that increasing perpendicular <speed> increases the <radius> in exactly the proportion needed to leave the <period> unchanged. Conversely, known <magnetic field>, perpendicular <speed>, and measured <radius> determine the magnitude of the <electric charge> divided by <mass>. This makes the same geometry useful for comparing bodies with different <electric charge> and <mass>.

Two short checks explain the result. First, the <cross product> gives $\mathbf v\times\mathbf B=(-\sin t,-\cos t,0)$ for both stipulated positive-<electric charge> paths, exactly their <acceleration>. Second, the transverse magnetic <force> supplies the <centripetal acceleration>, so $mv_\perp^2/r=|q|v_\perp B$; for $v_\perp>0$ this gives the <gyroradius>, and $\omega_c=v_\perp/r$ gives the <cyclotron frequency>.

<Magnetic forces do no work>: the instantaneous <power> of the magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> vanishes because a <cross product> is perpendicular to its first argument. The <work-energy theorem> therefore gives
$$
\frac{dK}{dt}=\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf v
=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v
+q\mathbf v\cdot(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)
=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v,
\qquad K=\tfrac12m|\mathbf v|^2.
$$
The <dot product> $\mathbf v\cdot(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$ is zero at every instant, rather than only after a full turn. With zero <electric field> and no other <forces>, the <kinetic energy> and <speed> remain constant; the <electric field> can increase or decrease <kinetic energy> according to the sign of $q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v$. In the two stipulated examples the constant <kinetic energy> is respectively $K=1/2$ and $K=5/8$ in rescaled units. The extra parallel <velocity> raises <kinetic energy> without changing the <gyroradius> or the <cyclotron frequency>.

For an electric example in rescaled units, stipulate $m=q=1$, zero <magnetic field>, constant <electric field> $\mathbf E=(2,0,0)$, no other <forces>, and rest at $t=0$. The <Lorentz force> gives <acceleration> $(2,0,0)$ and <velocity> $(2t,0,0)$, hence <kinetic energy> $K=2t^2$ and <power> $dK/dt=4t=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v$. At $t=1$, the <kinetic energy> has risen from zero to two: the <electric field> has done positive <work>.

The existing <electromagnetic field tensor> formulation expresses the same interaction covariantly. For <four-momentum> $p^\mu$ and <four-velocity> $u^\mu$, using <proper time> $\tau$, the <Lorentz force law> is
$$
\frac{dp^\mu}{d\tau}=qF^{\mu\nu}u_\nu.
$$
Its spatial and temporal components are
$$
\frac{d\mathbf p}{dt}=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B),
\qquad
\frac{d\mathcal E}{dt}=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v.
$$

= Gyroradius
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$r=mv_\perp/(|q|B)$}

= Larmor radius
{c}
{synonym}

The <gyroradius> is the <radius> of the transverse <circular motion> of a <charged particle in a uniform magnetic field>. In nonrelativistic <classical mechanics>, with zero <electric field> and no other <forces>, it is $mv_\perp/(|q|B)$, where $v_\perp$ is the <speed> perpendicular to the <magnetic field>. The parallel component of <velocity> changes the advance of the <helix>, rather than its transverse <radius>.

= Larmor theorem
{c}
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\omega=-e\mathbf B/(2m)$}

For particles with the same charge-to-mass ratio, rotationally invariant mutual <central forces>, and a weak uniform <magnetic field>, a frame with the displayed <angular velocity> cancels the velocity-dependent magnetic <Lorentz force> against the <Coriolis force>. The remaining rotational and magnetic terms are quadratic in $\mathbf B$. Thus, to first order in the field, the rotating-frame equations coincide with the zero-field equations. This is the classical dynamical basis of <Larmor precession>; equality is an approximation rather than an exact removal of the magnetic field.

= Radial escape in an inverse-radius axial magnetic field
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\dot r^2=v_\perp^2-(\ell/r-c)^2,\quad c=qB_0/m$}

For an axial <magnetic field> $\mathbf B=(B_0/r)\mathbf e_z$, let $L=mr^2\dot\theta$. The <Lorentz force> gives $\dot L=-qB_0\dot r$, so $\ell=(L+qB_0r)/m$ is constant. Since <magnetic forces do no work> and $\dot z$ is constant, the transverse speed $v_\perp$ is constant and $\dot r^2=v_\perp^2-(\ell/r-c)^2$. If $c\ell>0$ and $0<v_\perp<|c|$, two finite radial <turning points> confine the motion. If $v_\perp>|c|$, an outward branch escapes to infinite radius. At equality a marginal escaping branch exists when $c\ell>0$, but $\ell=0$ gives a circular orbit instead. The transverse <escape velocity> threshold is $|qB_0|/m$; motion parallel to the <magnetic field> cannot provide transverse escape energy.

= Velocity rotation in a time-dependent axial magnetic field
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\dot x+i\dot y=V\exp\left(i\phi-\frac{iq}{m}\int_0^t B_z(s)\,ds\right)$}

Under the magnetic-only <Lorentz force>, planar velocity has constant magnitude and an accumulated phase determined by the time integral of the axial field. For $B_z(t)=\beta t$, the planar position involves <Fresnel integrals>. With initial planar velocity $(1,0)$ and zero initial position, its limit is $(\sqrt{\pi m/(4q\beta)},-\sqrt{\pi m/(4q\beta)})$. The model prescribes a magnetic-only force; a physical time-dependent field may also produce an induced electric field.

// Target: number-theory.bigb

= Magnetic forces do no work
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$(q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot\mathbf v=0$}

The magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> is perpendicular to the particle <velocity>. Its power is therefore zero, so with no other forces the <kinetic energy> remains constant. This remains true relativistically because $dE/dt=\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf v$; a magnetic force can change momentum direction without changing total energy.

= Geometrization of the Lorentz force by an affine connection
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\Gamma^i{}_{tt}=-E^i,\quad\Gamma^i{}_{tj}=\epsilon^i{}_{jk}B^k$}

For smooth stationary fields on $\mathbb R^3$, set $\Gamma^i{}_{tt}=-E^i$ and $\Gamma^i{}_{tj}=\Gamma^i{}_{jt}=\epsilon^i{}_{jk}B^k$, with all other <Christoffel symbols> zero. This <torsion-free connection> makes the equation $\ddot{\mathbf x}=\mathbf E+2\mathbf B\times\dot{\mathbf x}$ a <geodesic equation> with time as <affine parameter>. Arbitrary smooth fields suffice; a metric realization imposes additional potential conditions.

= Electric drift in crossed uniform fields
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\mathbf v_D=\mathbf E\times\mathbf B/B^2$}

= Electric drift
{synonym}

In uniform perpendicular electric and <magnetic fields>, the guiding centre of a charged particle drifts with $\mathbf v_D=\mathbf E\times\mathbf B/|\mathbf B|^2$. This <velocity> is independent of the charge and mass; cyclotron motion is superposed on it.

= Lorentz four-force
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$f^\mu=qF^\mu{}_{\nu}u^\nu$}
{c}

The covariant force on a charged particle is its charge times the electromagnetic field-strength tensor contracted with its <four-velocity>. Antisymmetry keeps the four-force orthogonal to that velocity and preserves the rest mass.

= Magnetic force on a current-carrying wire
{parent=Lorentz force}

A wire carrying current $I$ in a magnetic field experiences
$$
d\mathbf F=I\,d\boldsymbol\ell\times\mathbf B.
$$
This follows by summing the magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> over the moving charge carriers.

= Relativistic cyclotron orbit
{title2=$\omega_\tau=qB/m$}
{parent=Lorentz force}

In a constant <magnetic field> $B\widehat z$ with no <electric field>, a charged relativistic particle moves in a circle in the perpendicular plane. Its angular frequency with respect to <proper time> is
$$
\omega_\tau=\frac{qB}{m},
$$
whereas its angular frequency in an inertial coordinate time is $\omega_t=qB/(\gamma m)$. The orbit radius at speed $v$ is
$$
R=\frac{\gamma mv}{|q|B}.
$$

= Charged-particle action over one relativistic cyclotron orbit
{parent=Relativistic cyclotron orbit}

For $qB>0$, choose $A_y=Bx$ and parametrize one clockwise orbit by $x=R\cos(\omega_\tau\tau)$ and $y=-R\sin(\omega_\tau\tau)$. Over $\Delta\tau=2\pi/\omega_\tau$, the <relativistic charged-particle action> is
$$
S=-mc^2\Delta\tau+q\oint A\mathbin\cdot dx
=-\frac{\pi mc^2}{\omega_\tau}(1+\gamma^2).
$$
The same formula with $|\omega_\tau|$ applies for either orbit orientation.

= Lorentz force density
{title2=$\mathbf f_{\rm L}$}
{parent=Lorentz force}

The Lorentz force per unit volume is $\mathbf f_{\rm L}=\rho_e\mathbf E+\mathbf j\times\mathbf B/c$ in Gaussian units.

= E-cross-B drift
{title2=$\mathbf E\times\mathbf B/B^2$}
{c}
{parent=Lorentz force}
{wiki}

In uniform perpendicular electric and magnetic fields, a charged particle's cyclotron orbit has guiding-centre velocity
$$
\mathbf v_D=\frac{\mathbf E\times\mathbf B}{B^2},
$$
independent of its charge and mass.

= Relativistic electromagnetic work-energy theorem
{parent=Lorentz force}

For $\mathcal E=\gamma mc^2$, the temporal component of the covariant Lorentz-force equation gives
$$
\frac{d\mathcal E}{dt}=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v.
$$
The magnetic field does no work because $\mathbf v\cdot(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)=0$.

= Coordinate acceleration of a relativistic charged particle
{parent=Lorentz force}

Combining $d(\gamma m\mathbf v)/dt=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$ with $mc^2\,d\gamma/dt=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v$ gives
$$
\frac{d\mathbf v}{dt}
=\frac q{m\gamma}
\left[
\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B
-\frac{\mathbf v(\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf E)}{c^2}
\right].
$$

= Electromagnetic field invariants
{title2=$F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu},\,F_{\mu\nu}\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}$}
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{wiki}

For signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and a consistent orientation,
$$
F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}
=2\left(B^2-\frac{E^2}{c^2}\right),
\qquad
F_{\mu\nu}\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}
=-\frac4c\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf B.
$$
Overall signs depend on tensor and orientation conventions, while their vanishing does not.

= Null electromagnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetic field invariants}
{wiki}

A null field has both electromagnetic invariants zero:
$$
\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf B=0,
\qquad
E^2=c^2B^2.
$$
For a nonzero constant null field, the mixed tensor is nilpotent of index three,
$$
F^\mu{}_\rho F^\rho{}_\sigma F^\sigma{}_\nu=0.
$$

= Electromagnetic stress-energy tensor
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{wiki=Electromagnetic_stress–energy_tensor}

For metric signature $(-,+,+,+)$,
$$
T^{\mu\nu}=\frac1{\mu_0}
\left(F^{\mu\rho}F^\nu{}_{\rho}
-\frac14\eta^{\mu\nu}F^{\rho\sigma}F_{\rho\sigma}\right).
$$
In particular, $T^{00}=\tfrac12(\epsilon_0E^2+B^2/\mu_0)$.

= Electromagnetic stress-energy divergence identity
{parent=Electromagnetic stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$\nabla_bT^{ab}=F^a{}_c\nabla_bF^{cb}/(4\pi)$}

For signature $(+---)$ in Gaussian electromagnetic units, $T^{ab}=(4\pi)^{-1}[-F^a{}_cF^{bc}+g^{ab}F_{cd}F^{cd}/4]$. The <electromagnetic Bianchi identity> gives $F^{bc}\nabla_bF^a{}_c=\nabla^a(F_{cd}F^{cd})/4$, so differentiating the stress tensor proves the displayed identity. Stress conservation implies the source-free <Maxwell equations> if the matrix $F^a{}_c$ is invertible; otherwise it only forces the field divergence into that matrix's kernel. Conversely source-free Maxwell equations always imply stress conservation.

= Gauge-compensated time translation in electromagnetism
{parent=Electromagnetic stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$\delta A_\nu=\alpha F_{0\nu}$}

With signature $(+,-,-,-)$ and $\mathcal L=-F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}/4$, combine a time translation $\alpha\partial_0A_\nu$ with the <gauge transformation> of parameter $-\alpha A_0$. Its <Noether current> is $J^\mu=-F^{\mu\nu}F_{0\nu}-\delta^\mu_0\mathcal L$, the gauge-invariant energy current. The density is $(|\mathbf E|^2+|\mathbf B|^2)/2$. On the source-free field equations it differs from the canonical translation current by $\partial_\nu(A_0F^{\mu\nu})$, an antisymmetric superpotential divergence, so total energies agree when the boundary term vanishes.

= Null energy condition for the electromagnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetic stress-energy tensor}

For every null vector $k$, put $q^\rho=k_\mu F^{\mu\rho}$. Antisymmetry gives $q\cdot k=0$, so $q$ has nonnegative norm and
$$
T^{\mu\nu}k_\mu k_\nu=\frac1{\mu_0}q^\rho q_\rho\geq0.
$$

= Lorentz transformation of electromagnetic fields
{parent=Relativistic electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

Lorentz boosts mix transverse electric and magnetic field components.

= Relativistic charge density of counterstreaming beams
{parent=Lorentz transformation of electromagnetic fields}
{title2=$\lambda'=-\gamma vI/c^2$}

Two equal opposite line charge densities moving in opposite directions have zero net line charge but a nonzero total current $I$. Under a longitudinal <Lorentz boost>, the charge-current four-vector gives $\lambda'=\gamma(\lambda-vI/c^2)=-\gamma vI/c^2$. Equivalently, for lab densities $n$ and speeds $\pm u$, their separate boosted number densities are $\gamma n(1\mp vu/c^2)$. Their imbalance creates the boosted radial <electric field>, consistently with <Lorentz transformation of electromagnetic fields> and <Gauss's law>.

= Electromagnetic jump conditions across a normally moving plane
{parent=Lorentz transformation of electromagnetic fields}

If a plane is stationary in one frame and an observer moves normally to it with velocity $\mathbf v$, then
$$
[B'_n]=0,
\qquad
[\mathbf E'_t]=\mathbf v\mathbin\times[\mathbf B'_t].
$$
The second relation accounts for the magnetic-field discontinuity moving through the observer's frame.

= Electromagnetic wave
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

An <electromagnetic wave> is a propagating disturbance of the <electric field> and <magnetic field> obeying the <Maxwell equations>. In source-free vacuum its speed is
$$
c=\frac1{\sqrt{\mu_0\epsilon_0}}\simeq2.998\times10^8\,\mathrm{m/s},
$$
the <speed of light>. An <electromagnetic wave> can travel through vacuum: a conducting medium is unnecessary. A single traveling <plane electromagnetic wave> has mutually perpendicular <electric field>, <magnetic field>, and propagation direction. This statement applies to that wave; arbitrary <near and far field> configurations need not share these directions or the relation $|\mathbf B|=|\mathbf E|/c$.

The visualization compares perpendicular components on a common scale: $c\mathbf B$ has the same units as $\mathbf E$. The curves are field values along space, rather than paths traced by particles. Their common phase moves toward $+z$, while the <Poynting vector> records the associated <energy flux>.

\Image[electromagnetic-plane-wave.png]
{title=Vacuum plane wave with electric field along x, magnetic field along y, and energy flux along positive z}

The <electromagnetic wave equation> gives a propagation law, while the full <Maxwell equations> additionally enforce the field directions and relative amplitudes. This is why solving an independent scalar <wave equation> for each component is insufficient to construct an <electromagnetic wave>. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_20.html][Feynman lectures, II.20: solutions in free space]{ref}.

= Electromagnetic waveguide
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}

An electromagnetic waveguide confines an <electromagnetic wave> transversely while allowing propagation along its axis. Conducting or dielectric boundaries select transverse modes. In a uniform guide a mode with transverse eigenvalue $k_\perp^2$ has longitudinal wavenumber $k_x^2=k^2-k_\perp^2$; below cutoff it is evanescent. At an open <aperture>, the confined field radiates into an angular spectrum rather than retaining a single guided mode.

= Scalar-potential conducting waveguide modes
{parent=Electromagnetic waveguide}
{title2=$\gamma^2=\omega^2/c^2-k^2$}

For dependence $e^{i(kz-\omega t)}$, a <Helmholtz equation> $(\Delta_\perp+\gamma^2)\psi=0$ generates transverse-magnetic fields $e_\perp=\nabla_\perp\psi$, $e_z=-i\gamma^2\psi/k$, $b_\perp=\omega\,\widehat z\times\nabla_\perp\psi/(kc^2)$, $b_z=0$, for $k\ne0$. The conducting wall requires a <Dirichlet boundary condition> on $\psi$. A transverse-electric representation is $e_\perp=\widehat z\times\nabla_\perp\psi$, $e_z=0$, $b_\perp=-k\nabla_\perp\psi/\omega$, $b_z=i\gamma^2\psi/\omega$, with a <Neumann boundary condition>. These are families of modes, not a completeness assertion for multiply connected guides and their possible TEM fields.

= Electromagnetic aperture angular spectrum
{parent=Electromagnetic waveguide}
{title2=$\mathbf q=(\kappa,\nu,\eta),\quad\kappa=\sqrt{k^2-\nu^2-\eta^2}$}

For an aperture normal to $\mathbf e_x$, let $F_y,F_z$ be its tangential <electric field> traces extended by zero outside the aperture. With the outgoing branch of $\kappa$, the <Weyl plane-wave representation> and the curl aperture integral give a spectrum proportional to $(-(\nu\widehat F_y+\eta\widehat F_z)/\kappa,\widehat F_y,\widehat F_z)$. Each wave is transverse to $\mathbf q$. The longitudinal electric component is regenerated by oblique diffraction even when it vanishes at the input. For $G=e^{ikr}/(4\pi r)$ and a unit prefactor on the stated curl integral, the spectrum has an overall factor $1/2$; a doubled half-space Green kernel gives factor one. The normalization must follow the chosen Green kernel and equivalent-current convention.

= Rectangular electromagnetic waveguide
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}

A rectangular electromagnetic waveguide has a vacuum interior bounded by four <perfect conductor> walls parallel to its axis. The harmonic fields obey zero tangential <electric field> and zero normal <magnetic field> at the walls. <Maxwell equations> determine transverse fields from longitudinal components; a purely longitudinal oscillating <magnetic field> by itself cannot propagate, since its divergence would be nonzero.

= Waveguide cutoff frequency
{parent=Rectangular electromagnetic waveguide}
{title2=$\omega_{mn}=c\pi\sqrt{m^2/a^2+n^2/b^2}$}

For a <transverse electric mode of a rectangular waveguide>, the dispersion relation is $\omega^2=c^2(k^2+\kappa_{mn}^2)$, with $\kappa_{mn}^2=(m\pi/a)^2+(n\pi/b)^2$. Below $\omega_{mn}=c\kappa_{mn}$, the longitudinal wave number is imaginary and the mode is evanescent. If $a\ge b$, the lowest propagating cutoff is $\omega_{10}=\pi c/a$, or ordinary frequency $c/(2a)$.

= Waveguide phase and group velocities
{parent=Waveguide cutoff frequency}
{title2=$v_{\mathrm{ph}}v_g=c^2$}

Above a mode's <waveguide cutoff frequency> $\omega_c$, its <phase velocity> is $c/[1-(\omega_c/\omega)^2]^{1/2}$ and its <group velocity> is $c[1-(\omega_c/\omega)^2]^{1/2}$. Differentiate $\omega^2=c^2k^2+\omega_c^2$ to obtain the latter. Phase travels faster than $c$ while the wave packet travels slower, so the phase speed does not imply superluminal information transport.

= Transverse electric mode of a rectangular waveguide
{parent=Rectangular electromagnetic waveguide}
{title2=$\mathrm{TE}_{mn}$}

For a transverse electric mode, $E_z=0$ and $B_z=B_0(x,y)e^{i(kz-\omega t)}$. Put $\Delta=(\omega/c)^2-k^2$. <Maxwell equations> give $B_x=ik\partial_xB_z/\Delta$, $B_y=ik\partial_yB_z/\Delta$, $E_x=i\omega\partial_yB_z/\Delta$, and $E_y=-i\omega\partial_xB_z/\Delta$. The wall conditions become <Neumann boundary conditions> for $B_0$, whose separated solutions are $\cos(m\pi x/a)\cos(n\pi y/b)$. The constant $(0,0)$ candidate is excluded by Faraday's equation because its corresponding electric field vanishes.

= Transverse magnetic polarization
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{title2=$\mathbf H=H_y\widehat{\mathbf y}$}

= TM wave
{c}
{synonym}

= TM polarization
{c}
{synonym}

Relative to a propagation plane, transverse magnetic polarization has its magnetic field perpendicular to that plane. At a perfectly conducting corrugation its scalar magnetic component obeys a Neumann condition. It reduces to a scalar two-dimensional problem, but not to the Dirichlet problem for the transverse electric component.

= Transverse electric polarization
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{title2=$\mathbf E=E_y\widehat{\mathbf y}$}

= TE wave
{c}
{synonym}

= TE polarization
{c}
{synonym}

Relative to a propagation plane, transverse electric polarization has its electric field perpendicular to that plane. For a conducting corrugation independent of the perpendicular coordinate, the scalar electric component obeys the Helmholtz equation with Dirichlet data at the metal surface.

= TE wave equation in a stratified magnetodielectric
{c}
{parent=Transverse electric polarization}
{title2=$E_{y,xx}+E_{y,zz}-\frac{\mu'}\mu E_{y,z}+\omega^2\mu\varepsilon E_y=0$}

With permittivity and permeability depending only on $z$ and time dependence $e^{i\omega t}$, a <transverse electric polarization> $\mathbf E=\widehat{\mathbf y}E_y(x,z)$ obeys the displayed equation. <Maxwell's equations> give $H_x=E_{y,z}/(i\omega\mu)$ and $H_z=-E_{y,x}/(i\omega\mu)$. The latter satisfies $H_{z,xx}+H_{z,zz}+(\mu'/\mu)H_{z,z}+[\omega^2\mu\varepsilon+(\log\mu)'']H_z=0$. At normal incidence $H_z=0$; the nonzero magnetic component is $H_x$.

= Refractive index
{title2=$n$}
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

The refractive index is the ratio of the vacuum speed of light to a medium's phase speed. In a homogeneous isotropic medium, $n=c\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}$ in compatible units.

= Inverse-square refractive-index profile
{parent=Refractive index}
{title2=$k^2(z)=k_0^2[a^2-b^2/(z-z_0)^2]$}

A <transverse electric polarization> with constant permeability and conserved transverse wavenumber $k_0\sin\theta_0$ satisfies $e''+[\beta^2-(\nu^2-1/4)/(z-z_0)^2]e=0$, with $\beta^2=k_0^2(a^2-\sin^2\theta_0)$ and $\nu^2=1/4+k_0^2b^2$. Substitution $e=\sqrt{|z-z_0|}Z_\nu(\beta|z-z_0|)$ reduces it to the <Bessel equation>. The profile is singular at $z_0$, so each side requires its own boundary conditions.

= Electromagnetic wave equation
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{title2=$\nabla^2\mathbf E-c^{-2}\partial_t^2\mathbf E=0$}
{wiki}

In source-free vacuum the <electric field> and <magnetic field> obey
$$
\nabla^2\mathbf E-c^{-2}\partial_t^2\mathbf E=0,\qquad
\nabla^2\mathbf B-c^{-2}\partial_t^2\mathbf B=0.
$$
A brief derivation takes the <curl> of <Faraday's law> and substitutes the <Ampère-Maxwell equation>. The <vector calculus> identity $\nabla\times(\nabla\times\mathbf E)=\nabla(\nabla\cdot\mathbf E)-\nabla^2\mathbf E$, together with source-free <Gauss's law>, leaves $\nabla^2\mathbf E=\mu_0\epsilon_0\partial_t^2\mathbf E$. The same argument gives the <magnetic field> equation. The operator $\nabla^2$ is the <Laplacian>, applied componentwise.

= Plane electromagnetic wave
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{title2=$\mathbf B=\widehat{\mathbf k}\times\mathbf E/c$}
{wiki}

A monochromatic vacuum <plane electromagnetic wave> has phase $\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf r-\omega t$, where $\mathbf k$ is the <wavevector>, $\omega>0$ the <angular frequency>, and $\omega=c|\mathbf k|$. Its field amplitudes obey $\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf E_0=\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf B_0=0$ and $\mathbf B_0=\mathbf k\times\mathbf E_0/\omega$.

For the <linear polarization> and orientation in the visualization, choose a right-handed <Cartesian coordinate system> and put
$$
\mathbf E(z,t)=E_0\cos(kz-\omega t)\widehat{\mathbf x},\qquad
\mathbf B(z,t)=\frac{E_0}{c}\cos(kz-\omega t)\widehat{\mathbf y},\qquad
k>0,\quad\omega=ck.
$$
Constant phase means $z=ct+\text{constant}$, so propagation is along $+z$. The <cross product> $\widehat{\mathbf x}\times\widehat{\mathbf y}=\widehat{\mathbf z}$ fixes the positive <Poynting vector>. Both fields reverse together every half-period and are in phase. Reversing the propagation direction with the same electric orientation reverses the magnetic orientation.

These fields are spatially uniform across every transverse plane. An infinite <plane electromagnetic wave> is an idealization with infinite total <electromagnetic energy>; it is useful as a local approximation and as a building block for <superposition>. For <frequency> $f=100\,\mathrm{MHz}$, $\omega=2\pi f$ and <wavelength> $\lambda=c/f\simeq3.00\,\mathrm m$, independently of $E_0$.

= Polarization of an electromagnetic wave
{parent=Plane electromagnetic wave}

= Electromagnetic polarization
{synonym}

The polarization of an electromagnetic wave describes the path traced by its transverse electric-field vector at a fixed point. A monochromatic wave may be linearly, circularly, or elliptically polarized; in vacuum it has two independent transverse polarization states.

This specializes <polarization of waves> to electric-field oscillations.

= Faraday effect
{c}
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

= Faraday rotation
{c}
{synonym}

The Faraday effect rotates the orientation of linear <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> during propagation through a magnetized medium. The two opposite <circular polarizations> accumulate different phases; their recombination rotates the linear polarization. In a sufficiently high-frequency cold <plasma>, this angle varies as the squared <wavelength> times a <rotation measure>.

= Rotation measure
{parent=Faraday effect}
{title2=$\mathrm{RM}=a_0\int n_e B_\parallel\,dz$}

For the high-frequency <Faraday effect> in a cold <plasma>, the rotation measure is the coefficient in $\Delta\phi=\lambda^2\mathrm{RM}$. It is a density-weighted line integral of the line-of-sight <magnetic field>; the coefficient $a_0$ depends on the units. A sign convention for the line of sight fixes its sign. Correlations across an extended source can constrain a foreground <magnetic energy spectrum>, subject to statistical assumptions.

= Long-path projection of a magnetic correlation
{parent=Rotation measure}
{title2=$C_{\mathrm{RM}}(s)\simeq a_0^2n_e^2L\int H(k_\perp)e^{i\mathbf k_\perp\cdot\mathbf s}d^2k_\perp/(2\pi)^2$}

For constant <electron> density, common path length $L$, and a <solenoidal isotropic spectral tensor>, the rotation-measure <correlation> contains the window $|\int_0^Le^{ik_zz}dz|^2$. As $L$ becomes large compared with the <correlation length>, this window acts as $2\pi L\delta(k_z)$ within the <integral>. The transverse projector then has $zz$ component one, yielding the displayed two-dimensional <Fourier transform>. For finite $L$, the windowed integral retains longitudinal modes and endpoint corrections. The method of using such <rotation measure> correlations to infer magnetic spectra is developed by https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0302426[Enßlin and Vogt].

= Hankel inversion of a rotation-measure correlation
{c}
{parent=Long-path projection of a magnetic correlation}
{title2=$H(k)=\frac{2\pi}{a_0^2n_e^2L}\int_0^\infty rJ_0(kr)C_{\mathrm{RM}}(r)\,dr$}

Invert the two-dimensional <Fourier transform> in the <long-path projection of a magnetic correlation>, then perform the angular integration in <polar coordinates>. The angular integral is $2\pi J_0(kr)$, a <Bessel function of the first kind>, giving the displayed <Hankel transform> with the stated <Fourier-Hankel normalization for an isotropic spectrum>. The result recovers the scalar tensor coefficient $H$, whose shell-integrated <magnetic energy spectrum> has an additional factor $k^2$.

= Polarizer
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

= Polariser
{synonym}

A linear <polarizer> transmits one <linear polarization> and suppresses the orthogonal one. Two ideal crossed <polarizers> block light unless an intervening medium changes its <polarization of an electromagnetic wave>. A <twisted nematic field effect> uses this selection to convert field-driven reorientation into brightness modulation.

= Stokes parameters
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}
{c}
{title2=$(I,Q,U,V)$}

The Stokes parameters $I,Q,U,V$ describe intensity, two linear-polarization components and circular polarization. $Q$ and $U$ depend on the chosen transverse basis. On the celestial sphere, $Q\pm iU$ transform as spin-two fields rather than as ordinary scalars.

= Linear polarization
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}

A wave is linearly polarized when its oscillating <electric field> always lies along one fixed line. For a transverse <plane wave>, that line is perpendicular to the <wavevector>. Unlike <circular polarization>, the field oscillates along the line rather than rotating through directions.

= Circular polarization
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

An electromagnetic wave has circular polarization when its transverse <electric field> has constant magnitude and rotates once during each period. In complex-amplitude notation, two orthogonal components have equal magnitude and a relative phase of one quarter-cycle.

= Perfect conductor
{parent=Plane electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

At the surface of a perfect conductor, the tangential <electric field> and normal <magnetic field> vanish. An incident <plane electromagnetic wave> is therefore accompanied by a reflected wave whose tangential electric field cancels the incident field at the surface.

= Scalar polarization reduction at a conducting corrugation
{parent=Perfect conductor}
{title2=$E_y|_\Gamma=0,\quad\partial_nH_y|_\Gamma=0$}

If a metal surface is $z=h(x)$ and the incident field is independent of $y$, Maxwell's equations separate into <TE polarization> and <TM polarization>. The metal condition that the tangential electric field vanish sets $E_y=0$ in the TE sector. In the TM sector $\mathbf E=i\nabla\times(H_y\widehat{\mathbf y})/(\omega\epsilon)$, so the same condition gives $(\partial_z-h'\partial_x)H_y=0$. This explains why the TE problem agrees with scalar Dirichlet acoustics while the TM problem has different rough-surface boundary forcing.

= Normal reflection of an electromagnetic wave from a perfect conductor
{parent=Perfect conductor}

At normal incidence, the reflected electric amplitude is the negative of the incident amplitude at the surface, while the reflected magnetic amplitude has the same sign. Their superposition forms a <standing wave> with an electric node and a magnetic antinode at the conductor.

= Interface conditions for electromagnetic fields
{parent=Plane electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

Across an interface with unit normal $\mathbf n$ from medium one to medium two, <Maxwell equations> imply $\mathbf n\times(\mathbf E_2-\mathbf E_1)=0$, $\mathbf n\cdot(\mathbf B_2-\mathbf B_1)=0$, $\mathbf n\cdot(\mathbf D_2-\mathbf D_1)=\sigma_f$, and $\mathbf n\times(\mathbf H_2-\mathbf H_1)=\mathbf K_f$. Here $\sigma_f$ and $\mathbf K_f$ are the free surface <electric charge> and <electric current> densities.

= Dielectric-interface boundary conditions
{parent=Interface conditions for electromagnetic fields}

Without free surface charge or current, the tangential components of $E$ and $H$ and the normal components of $D$ and $B$ are continuous across an interface.

= Phase matching at a planar wave interface
{parent=Dielectric-interface boundary conditions}

Equality of the boundary fields for every tangential position and time forces equal frequency and equal tangential wavevector for incident, reflected, and transmitted plane waves.

= Snell law for electromagnetic waves
{parent=Phase matching at a planar wave interface}
{c}

For refractive indices $n_-$ and $n_+$,
$$
\theta_R=\theta_I,
\qquad
n_-\sin\theta_I=n_+\sin\theta_T.
$$

= Total internal reflection
{parent=Snell law for electromagnetic waves}
{wiki}

Total internal reflection occurs when a wave travels from higher to lower <refractive index> above the critical incidence angle, leaving no propagating transmitted plane wave normal to the interface.

= Critical angle for total internal reflection
{title2=$\theta_c$}
{parent=Total internal reflection}

For refractive indices $n_->n_+$, the critical angle satisfies $\sin\theta_c=n_+/n_-$. Above it, the transmitted normal wavenumber is imaginary and the transmitted field is an <evanescent wave>.

= Fresnel equations
{c}
{parent=Dielectric-interface boundary conditions}
{wiki}

The <Fresnel equations> give reflection and transmission amplitudes for both transverse-electric and transverse-magnetic <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> at a planar interface. They follow by applying <interface conditions for electromagnetic fields> to incident, reflected and refracted <plane electromagnetic waves>.

= Transverse-electric Fresnel reflection coefficient
{parent=Fresnel equations}

When the permeabilities agree, the signed reflected-to-incident electric-field ratio for polarization normal to the plane of incidence is
$$
r_{\rm TE}
=\frac{n_-\cos\theta_I-n_+\cos\theta_T}
{n_-\cos\theta_I+n_+\cos\theta_T}.
$$
For incidence from lower to higher refractive index it never vanishes.

= Reflected-to-transmitted transverse-electric amplitude ratio
{parent=Transverse-electric Fresnel reflection coefficient}

For equal permeabilities on the two sides of an interface, tangential-field continuity and <Snell law for electromagnetic waves> give
$$
\frac{E_R}{E_T}
=\frac12\left(1-\frac{\tan\theta_R}{\tan\theta_T}\right)
$$
for consistently signed electric amplitudes normal to the plane of incidence.

= Plane wave in an anisotropic dielectric
{parent=Plane electromagnetic wave}

For $D_i=\varepsilon_{ij}E_j$, $B=\mu H$, and a plane wave with electric polarization $e$, the source-free Maxwell equations imply
$$
k\times(k\times e)+\omega^2\mu\varepsilon e=0.
$$
If $e$ is an eigenvector of $\varepsilon$, then $e\perp k$, the dispersion relation is $|k|^2=\omega^2\mu\varepsilon_e$, and the <Poynting vector> is parallel to $k$.

= Poynting vector
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf S=\mathbf E\times\mathbf B/\mu_0$}
{wiki}

In vacuum the <Poynting vector>
$$
\mathbf S=\frac1{\mu_0}\mathbf E\times\mathbf B
$$
is the local <energy flux>, measured in watts per square metre. Its <surface integral> $P=\int_A\mathbf S\cdot\mathbf n\,dA$ gives signed <power> crossing a surface with unit <normal vector> $\mathbf n$. Positive flux follows the chosen normal; for a closed surface the usual convention is outward. <Power> depends on the normal component and area, rather than just the field magnitude.

The <Poynting vector> applies to steady fields as well as <electromagnetic waves>. In a driven resistive <electrical circuit>, surrounding fields can carry <energy> into the conductor, where electrical <work> becomes <Joule heating>. This turns a circuit-level power balance into a spatial description of energy delivery. \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/res-6-001-electromagnetic-fields-and-energy-spring-2008/0f814aee1c72517df7d7e7e96b7365e1_11.pdf][Haus and Melcher, Electromagnetic Fields and Energy, chapter 11, §§11.0–11.3]{ref}.

= Poynting theorem
{parent=Poynting vector}
{c}
{title2=$\partial_tu+\nabla\cdot\mathbf S=-\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E$}

= Poynting's theorem
{c}
{synonym}

The <Poynting theorem> is the local <energy> balance of classical <electromagnetism>. For vacuum field laws, with <current density> $\mathbf J$, the <electromagnetic energy> per unit volume is
$$
u=\frac{\epsilon_0|\mathbf E|^2}{2}+\frac{|\mathbf B|^2}{2\mu_0},
$$
and
$$
\partial_tu+\nabla\cdot\mathbf S=-\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E.
$$
Thus a decrease in local <electromagnetic energy> can supply outward <energy flux> or <work> on <electric charge>. The term $\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E$ is <power> transferred to matter per unit volume. Its sign matters: positive values remove field <energy>, while negative values describe matter supplying the field. The magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> supplies no work term.

For a fixed volume $V$, the <divergence theorem> gives the directly measurable balance
$$
\frac{d}{dt}\int_Vu\,dV
=-\int_{\partial V}\mathbf S\cdot\mathbf n\,dA
-\int_V\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E\,dV.
$$
For example, $2.0\,\mathrm W$ entering a region with $0.50\,\mathrm W$ leaving and $1.20\,\mathrm W$ transferred to matter means stored <electromagnetic energy> increases at $0.30\,\mathrm W$. In source-free vacuum only the boundary flux changes the stored <energy>; a nonzero local <Poynting vector> can still have zero net closed-surface flux.

A brief proof takes the <dot product> of the <Ampère-Maxwell equation> with $\mathbf E/\mu_0$ and uses <Faraday's law> with $\mathbf B/\mu_0$. The identity $\nabla\cdot(\mathbf E\times\mathbf B)=\mathbf B\cdot(\nabla\times\mathbf E)-\mathbf E\cdot(\nabla\times\mathbf B)$ turns the remaining terms into the <divergence> of the <Poynting vector>. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_27.html#Ch27-S3][Feynman lectures, II.27.3: local field-energy conservation]{ref}.

= Closed-surface Poynting flux of a plane wave
{parent=Poynting theorem}

For a real linearly polarized monochromatic vacuum <plane wave>, the local <Poynting vector> is proportional to $\cos^2(\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf r-\omega t)\widehat{\mathbf k}$. Its flux through a closed surface generally oscillates at $2\omega$, with zero cycle average, although particular volume geometries can cancel it identically. For a circularly polarized plane wave of constant amplitude the <Poynting vector> is spatially and temporally constant, so the net flux through every closed surface is exactly zero. A nonzero constant local energy flux must not be confused with the net closed-surface flux.

= Electromagnetic energy
{title2=$U_{\rm EM}$}
{parent=Poynting theorem}

In vacuum the electromagnetic energy density is
$$
u=\frac{\varepsilon_0E^2}{2}+\frac{B^2}{2\mu_0},
$$
and the field energy in a region is $U_{\rm EM}=\int u\,dV$.

= Magnetic energy
{parent=Electromagnetic energy}
{title2=$E_B$}

In vacuum-permeability <magnetohydrodynamics>, the <magnetic energy> in a volume is $E_B=\int B^2/(2\mu_0)\,dV$. Its volume density equals the <magnetic pressure>.

= Magnetic energy spectrum
{parent=Magnetic energy}
{title2=$E_B(k)$}

The shell-integrated magnetic energy spectrum distributes <magnetic energy> over <wavenumbers>. With a <solenoidal isotropic spectral tensor> normalized to $\widehat C_{ii}=2H(k)$ and the inverse <Fourier transform> measure $d^3k/(2\pi)^3$, one has $\mathbb E|\mathbf B|^2=\pi^{-2}\int k^2H(k)dk$. In <Gaussian units>, $E_B(k)=k^2H(k)/(8\pi^3)$ integrates to the energy density $\mathbb E|\mathbf B|^2/(8\pi)$. A spectrum per unit mass or normalized to $B^2/2$ has different constant factors. Scale-local fluctuation estimates give $\delta B_l^2$ proportional to $kE_B(k)$ at $k\sim l^{-1}$ when the spectrum is normalized consistently.

= Energy density and flux of a plane electromagnetic wave
{parent=Poynting theorem}

For a single traveling vacuum <plane electromagnetic wave>, $\mathbf B=\widehat{\mathbf k}\times\mathbf E/c$ gives equal electric and magnetic contributions to <electromagnetic energy>:
$$
u_E=u_B=\frac12\epsilon_0|\mathbf E|^2,\qquad
u=\epsilon_0|\mathbf E|^2,\qquad
\mathbf S=cu\widehat{\mathbf k}.
$$
For the fields with <linear polarization> along $x$ and magnetic orientation along $y$ specified above,
$$
\mathbf S(z,t)=\epsilon_0cE_0^2\cos^2(kz-\omega t)\widehat{\mathbf z},\qquad
\langle\mathbf S\rangle=\frac{\epsilon_0cE_0^2}{2}\widehat{\mathbf z}.
$$
Angle brackets mean an average over one period at fixed position. The factor $1/2$ comes from averaging squared <cosine>; $E_0$ is the peak amplitude, whereas the <root mean square> field is $E_{\mathrm{rms}}=E_0/\sqrt2$. The equal energy contributions oscillate together, rather than trading energy as in an <RLC circuit>. \a[https://live.ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-013-electromagnetics-and-applications-spring-2009/d3be4ea78b036a6362230fb41780cf54_MIT6_013S09_notes.pdf][MIT 6.013 course notes, §2.7.3: power and energy in uniform plane waves]{ref}.

For $E_0=100\,\mathrm{V/m}$, using $c=299792458\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and $\epsilon_0\simeq8.854\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{F/m}$,
$$
B_0=\frac{100}{c}\,\mathrm{V/m}\simeq3.34\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm T,
\qquad
\langle S_z\rangle\simeq13.3\,\mathrm{W/m^2},
\qquad
\langle u\rangle\simeq4.43\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{J/m^3}.
$$
A $0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$ surface normal to $+z$ therefore has average crossing <power> $0.133\,\mathrm W$. This is incident <power>; the amount absorbed depends on the surface. Doubling the field amplitude quadruples the average flux.

= Poynting theorem in a linear anisotropic medium
{c}
{parent=Poynting theorem}

For symmetric time-independent constitutive tensors,
$$
u=\frac12(\varepsilon_{ij}E_iE_j+\mu_{ij}H_iH_j),
\qquad
S=E\times H,
$$
and the macroscopic Maxwell equations give $\partial_tu+\nabla\cdot S=-E\cdot J$.

= Near and far field
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

The <near and far field> distinguish source-dependent electromagnetic field regions. Close to a localized source, reactive fields and induction terms contribute; sufficiently far away, the leading transverse fields decrease as the inverse distance and carry outgoing power. The <radiation zone> describes this far-field limit.

= Radiation zone
{parent=Near and far field}

In the radiation zone of a localized source, the leading electromagnetic fields decay as $1/R$, are transverse to the observation direction, and satisfy $B=\widehat R\times E/c$. Terms decaying faster than $1/R$ do not contribute to the limiting radiated power.

= Intensity of an isotropic radiator
{parent=Radiation zone}

An isotropic source of average power $P$ has intensity
$$
\langle S\rangle=\frac{P}{4\pi r^2}
$$
at distance $r$. For monochromatic vacuum radiation, its electric-field amplitude is $E_0=\sqrt{2\langle S\rangle/(\epsilon_0c)}$.

= Multipole radiation
{parent=Radiation zone}
{wiki}

<Multipole radiation> expands radiation from localized time-dependent sources into electric and magnetic multipoles. <Electric dipole radiation> is the leading electric term, while <magnetic dipole radiation> and higher electric multipoles contribute when the leading term vanishes or higher precision is needed.

= Electric dipole radiation
{parent=Multipole radiation}

For a localized source whose size is small compared with its radiation wavelength, the radiation fields of its electric dipole moment $p$ are
$$
E_{\rm rad}=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi R}\widehat R\times(\widehat R\times\ddot p(t-R/c)),
\qquad
B_{\rm rad}=\frac1c\widehat R\times E_{\rm rad}.
$$
Their total instantaneous power is
$$
\mathcal P=\frac{\mu_0}{6\pi c}|\ddot p(t-R/c)|^2.
$$

= Electric-dipole approximation for radiation
{parent=Electric dipole radiation}

= Dipole approximation for radiation
{synonym}

For a source of characteristic size $a$ and <angular frequency> $\omega$, the electric-dipole approximation requires
$$
\frac{\omega a}{c}\ll1,
$$
so the source is much smaller than its radiation wavelength. Observing the pure $1/R$ radiation field additionally requires the <radiation zone>, $R\gg c/\omega$ and $R\gg a$.

= Angular distribution of electric dipole radiation
{parent=Electric dipole radiation}

If $\theta$ is the angle between the observation direction and $\ddot p$, then
$$
\frac{dP}{d\Omega}
=\frac{\mu_0}{16\pi^2c}|\ddot p|^2\sin^2\theta.
$$
Integrating over <solid angle> uses $\int\sin^2\theta\,d\Omega=8\pi/3$ and gives the total <electric dipole radiation> power.

= Radiation damping of a harmonically oscillating charge
{parent=Electric dipole radiation}

A charge $q$ undergoing <simple harmonic motion> of <angular frequency> $\omega$ and slowly varying amplitude $A$ radiates the averaged power
$$
\langle P\rangle=\frac{\mu_0q^2A^2\omega^4}{12\pi c}.
$$
Its mechanical energy $E=mA^2\omega^2/2$ therefore has <exponential decay> rate $\gamma=\mu_0q^2\omega^2/(6\pi mc)$ and <half-life>
$$
t_{1/2}=\frac{6\pi mc\log2}{\mu_0q^2\omega^2}.
$$

= Magnetic dipole radiation
{parent=Multipole radiation}

Electromagnetic duality replaces the electric dipole $p$ by $m/c$ for a magnetic dipole moment $m$. Therefore
$$
\mathcal P=\frac{\mu_0}{6\pi c^3}|\ddot m|^2.
$$

This is the magnetic-dipole term of the <multipole radiation> expansion.

= Magnetic-dipole spin-down
{parent=Magnetic dipole radiation}

If a rigid body's inclined magnetic dipole rotates at angular speed $\Omega$, its radiated power is $C\Omega^4$. Conservation of rotational energy $I\Omega^2/2$ then gives
$$
\dot\Omega=-\frac CI\Omega^3,
\qquad
\Omega(t)^{-2}=\Omega_0^{-2}+\frac{2C}{I}t.
$$
